/* Past-year questions for Rank mode · JEE Mains · Laws of Motion, grouped by topic and sub-topic.
   Sources: JEE Main (2019-2025), JEE Advanced and IIT-JEE (1992-2024), AIEEE (2002-2006). Wording as in the paper; solutions written for this site.
   MCQ: o + c (index or array of indices). Numerical: n. Multi-part: a. s = worked solution. y = exam and year. fig = diagram (PhysicaFig spec). */
window.PhysicaRankPyqLom=[
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A block of mass 1 kg, moving along x with speed vᵢ = 10 m/s enters a rough region ranging from x = 0.1 m to x = 1.9 m. The retarding force acting on the block in this range is Fᵣ = −kx N, with k = 10 N/m. Then the final speed of the block as it crosses rough region is","o":["6 m/s","8 m/s","10 m/s","4 m/s"],"c":1,"s":"The force depends on position, so use the work-energy theorem.\nWork done by the retarding force = −∫kx dx from 0.1 to 1.9 = −(k/2)(1.9² − 0.1²).\n= −5 × (3.61 − 0.01) = −18 J.\nInitial kinetic energy = ½ × 1 × 10² = 50 J.\nFinal kinetic energy = 50 − 18 = 32 J, so ½ × 1 × v² = 32 and v² = 64.\nv = 8 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 7 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"An object with mass 500 g moves along x-axis with speed v = 4√x m/s. The force acting on the object is","o":["8 N","6 N","5 N","4 N"],"c":3,"s":"Acceleration in terms of position: a = v dv/dx.\nv² = 16x, so 2v dv/dx = 16 and v dv/dx = 8 m/s².\nThe acceleration is constant: a = 8 m/s².\nF = ma = 0.5 × 8 = 4 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 23 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A massless spring gets elongated by amount x₁ under a tension of 5 N. Its elongation is x₂ under the tension of 7 N. For the elongation of (5x₁ − 2x₂), the tension in the spring will be,","o":["39 N","11 N","20 N","15 N"],"c":1,"s":"For a spring, tension is proportional to elongation (T = kx).\nT₁ = 5 N gives x₁ and T₂ = 7 N gives x₂, so x₂ = (7/5)x₁.\n5x₁ − 2x₂ = 5x₁ − (14/5)x₁ = (11/5)x₁.\nTension is proportional to elongation, so T = 5 × (11/5) = 11 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A balloon and its content having mass M is moving up with an acceleration 'a'. The mass that must be released from the content so that the balloon starts moving up with an acceleration '3a' will be\n(Take 'g' as acceleration due to gravity)","o":["{3Ma;2a+g}","{3Ma;2a−g}","{2Ma;3a+g}","{2Ma;3a−g}"],"c":2,"s":"Let U be the constant upward force on the balloon (buoyancy). First case: U − Mg = Ma, so U = M(g + a).\nAfter releasing mass Δm, the mass is M − Δm and U stays the same.\nU − (M − Δm)g = (M − Δm)(3a), so U = (M − Δm)(g + 3a).\nEquate: M(g + a) = (M − Δm)(g + 3a), so M − Δm = M(g + a)/(g + 3a).\nΔm = M[(g + 3a) − (g + a)]/(g + 3a) = 2Ma/(3a + g)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A wooden block, initially at rest on the ground, is pushed by a force which increases linearly with time t. Which of the following curve best describes acceleration of the block with time?","fig":{"w":340,"h":250,"alt":"Four acceleration versus time graphs: (a) a straight line through the origin, (b) a straight line starting above the origin, (c) a curve rising steeply, (d) a horizontal line.","d":[["l",40,95,40,25,"a"],["l",40,95,160,95,"a"],["t",34,29,"a","e"],["t",158,109,"t","e"],["t",95,123,"(a)","m"],["l",40,95,140,35],["l",210,95,210,25,"a"],["l",210,95,330,95,"a"],["t",204,29,"a","e"],["t",328,109,"t","e"],["t",265,123,"(b)","m"],["l",210,77,310,33],["l",40,210,40,140,"a"],["l",40,210,160,210,"a"],["t",34,144,"a","e"],["t",158,224,"t","e"],["t",95,238,"(c)","m"],["p","M40 210 Q120 205 140 145"],["l",210,210,210,140,"a"],["l",210,210,330,210,"a"],["t",204,144,"a","e"],["t",328,224,"t","e"],["t",265,238,"(d)","m"],["l",210,170,310,170]]},"o":["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],"c":0,"s":"Force F = kt increases linearly with time, and the block is pushed with this force.\nBy Newton's second law, a = F/m = (k/m)t.\nAcceleration is directly proportional to t, so the a–t graph is a straight line through the origin.\nThis is graph (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A wooden block of mass 5 kg rests on a soft horizontal floor. When an iron cylinder of mass 25 kg is placed on the top of the block, the floor yields and the block and the cylinder together go down with an acceleration of 0.1 ms⁻². The action force of the system on the floor is equal to","o":["196 N","291 N","297 N","294 N"],"c":1,"s":"Total mass = 5 + 25 = 30 kg, moving down with a = 0.1 m/s².\nForces on the system: weight mg down and normal reaction N up from the floor.\nmg − N = ma, so N = m(g − a) = 30 × (9.8 − 0.1) = 30 × 9.7 = 291 N.\nBy Newton's third law, the force of the system on the floor is also 291 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A particle moves in x-y plane under the influence of a force F⃗ such that its linear momentum is p⃗(t) = î cos(kt) − ĵ sin(kt). If k is constant, the angle between F⃗ and p⃗ will be","o":["π/4","π/3","π/2","π/6"],"c":2,"s":"Force is the rate of change of momentum: F⃗ = dp⃗/dt.\nF⃗ = −k sin(kt) î − k cos(kt) ĵ.\nDot product: F⃗·p⃗ = −k sin(kt)cos(kt) + k cos(kt)sin(kt) = 0.\nThe dot product is zero, so the two vectors are perpendicular.\nThe angle is π/2."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"A light string passing over a smooth light pulley connects two blocks of masses m₁ and m₂ (where m₂ > m₁). If the acceleration of the system is {g;√2}, then the ratio of the masses {m₁;m₂} is","o":["{√2−1;√2+1}","{√3+1;√2−1}","{1−√5;√2−1}","{1+√5;√5−1}"],"c":0,"s":"For an Atwood machine, a = g(m₂ − m₁)/(m₁ + m₂).\nPut a = g/√2 and r = m₁/m₂: (1 − r)/(1 + r) = 1/√2.\n√2(1 − r) = 1 + r, so √2 − 1 = r(1 + √2).\nr = (√2 − 1)/(√2 + 1)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A body of weight 200 N is suspended from a tree branch through a chain of mass 10 kg. The branch pulls the chain by a force equal to (if g = 10 m/s²)","o":["100 N","300 N","150 N","200 N"],"c":1,"s":"The body and chain are at rest, so the branch must support both.\nWeight of the chain = 10 × 10 = 100 N.\nWeight of the body = 200 N.\nForce by the branch = 200 + 100 = 300 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"A player caught a cricket ball of mass 150 g moving at a speed of 20 m/s. If the catching process is completed in 0.1 s, the magnitude of force exerted by the ball on the hand of the player is","o":["30 N","300 N","3 N","150 N"],"c":0,"s":"Mass = 150 g = 0.15 kg. The ball is brought to rest.\nChange in momentum = 0.15 × 20 = 3 kg m/s.\nAverage force = change in momentum / time = 3 / 0.1 = 30 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"A light unstretchable string passing over a smooth light pulley connects two blocks of masses m₁ and m₂. If the acceleration of the system is {g;8}, then the ratio of the masses {m₂;m₁} is","o":["5 : 3","8 : 1","9 : 7","4 : 3"],"c":2,"s":"For an Atwood machine, a = g(m₂ − m₁)/(m₁ + m₂), taking m₂ as the heavier mass.\nPut a = g/8 and r = m₂/m₁: (r − 1)/(r + 1) = 1/8.\n8r − 8 = r + 1, so 7r = 9.\nr = 9/7, so m₂ : m₁ = 9 : 7."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A body of mass 4 kg experiences two forces F⃗₁ = 5î + 8ĵ + 7k̂ and F⃗₂ = 3î − 4ĵ − 3k̂. The acceleration acting on the body is","o":["2î + ĵ + k̂","4î + 2ĵ + 2k̂","−2î − ĵ − k̂","2î + 3ĵ + 3k̂"],"c":0,"s":"Net force = F⃗₁ + F⃗₂ = (5 + 3)î + (8 − 4)ĵ + (7 − 3)k̂ = 8î + 4ĵ + 4k̂ N.\nAcceleration = net force / mass = (8î + 4ĵ + 4k̂)/4.\na = 2î + ĵ + k̂ m/s²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"A cricket player catches a ball of mass 120 g moving at 25 m/s speed. If the catching process is completed in 0.1 s then the magnitude of force exerted by the ball on the hand of player will be (in SI unit)","o":["30","24","12","25"],"c":0,"s":"Mass = 120 g = 0.12 kg. The ball stops, so the change in momentum = 0.12 × 25 = 3 kg m/s.\nAverage force = 3 / 0.1 = 30 N.\nThe answer in SI units is 30."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"All surfaces shown in figure are assumed to be frictionless and the pulleys and the string are light. The acceleration of the block of mass 2 kg is","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"A 2 kg block on a frictionless 30 degree incline is joined by a string over a pulley at the top of the incline to a movable pulley which carries a 4 kg block; the other end of the string is fixed to the ceiling.","d":[["p","M20 170 L200 170 L200 66 Z"],["l",10,170,215,170],["p","M90 129.6 L116 114.6 L108 100.7 L82 115.7 Z","f"],["t",78,102,"2 kg","e"],["t",56,166,"30°"],["c",197,64,7],["l",111,108,192,60],["l",197,57,270,57],["l",270,57,270,100],["c",285,100,15],["l",300,100,300,15],["l",285,115,285,130],["r",265,130,40,30,"f"],["t",285,150,"4 kg","m"],["l",240,15,330,15],["l",250,15,244,7],["l",265,15,259,7],["l",280,15,274,7],["l",295,15,289,7],["l",310,15,304,7],["l",325,15,319,7]]},"o":["{g;4}","g","{g;3}","{g;2}"],"c":2,"s":"The 4 kg block hangs from a movable pulley. If it moves down by x, the string on both sides of that pulley shortens, so the 2 kg block moves up the incline by 2x. Hence a(2 kg) = 2a(4 kg).\nLet a be the acceleration of the 4 kg block. The string tension is T.\n4 kg block: 4g − 2T = 4a (two string segments hold the movable pulley).\n2 kg block along the incline: T − 2g sin30° = 2(2a), so T = g + 4a.\nSubstitute: 4g − 2g − 8a = 4a, so 12a = 2g and a = g/6.\nAcceleration of the 2 kg block = 2a = g/3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"A spherical body of mass 100 g is dropped from a height of 10 m from the ground. After hitting the ground, the body rebounds to a height of 5 m. The impulse of force imparted by the ground to the body is given by (given g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["4.32 kg ms⁻¹","43.2 kg ms⁻¹","2.39 kg ms⁻¹","23.9 kg ms⁻¹"],"c":2,"s":"Speed just before hitting: v₁ = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 10) = 14 m/s (downward).\nSpeed just after rebound: v₂ = √(2 × 9.8 × 5) = √98 ≈ 9.9 m/s (upward).\nThe direction reverses, so impulse = m(v₁ + v₂) = 0.1 × (14 + 9.9).\nImpulse ≈ 2.39 kg m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Blocks pulled or pushed together","q":"Three blocks A, B and C are pulled on a horizontal smooth surface by a force of 80 N as shown in figure. The tensions T₁ and T₂ in the string are respectively","fig":{"w":340,"h":130,"alt":"Blocks A (5 kg), B (3 kg) and C (2 kg) in a row on a smooth horizontal surface, joined by strings with tensions T1 and T2, and pulled to the right by a force of 80 N applied to C.","d":[["l",20,90,290,90],["r",30,54,70,36,"f"],["t",65,77,"5 kg","m"],["l",100,72,130,72],["t",115,64,"T₁","m"],["r",130,54,60,36,"f"],["t",160,77,"3 kg","m"],["l",190,72,215,72],["t",202,64,"T₂","m"],["r",215,54,55,36,"f"],["t",242,77,"2 kg","m"],["l",270,72,325,72,"a"],["t",300,60,"80 N","m"],["t",65,48,"A","m"],["t",160,48,"B","m"],["t",242,48,"C","m"]]},"o":["80 N, 100 N","88 N, 96 N","40 N, 64 N","60 N, 80 N"],"c":2,"s":"All three blocks move together with the same acceleration.\na = F/(total mass) = 80/(5 + 3 + 2) = 8 m/s².\nT₁ pulls only block A: T₁ = 5 × 8 = 40 N.\nT₂ pulls blocks A and B: T₂ = (5 + 3) × 8 = 64 N.\nSo T₁ = 40 N and T₂ = 64 N."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2024","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A block of 5 kg moves along the x-direction subject to the force F = (−20x + 10) N, with the value of x in metre. At time t = 0 s, it is at rest at position x = 1 m. The position and momentum of the block at t = (π/4) s are","o":["−0.5 m, 5 kg m/s","0.5 m, 0 kg m/s","0.5 m, −5 kg m/s","−1 m, 5 kg m/s"],"c":2,"s":"F = −20(x − 0.5), so the motion is simple harmonic about x = 0.5 m with ω² = 20/5 = 4, ω = 2 rad/s.\nAt t = 0 the block is at rest at x = 1 m, so the amplitude is 0.5 m: x = 0.5 + 0.5 cos(2t).\nAt t = π/4: x = 0.5 + 0.5 cos(π/2) = 0.5 m.\nv = −0.5 × 2 sin(2t) = −sin(π/2) = −1 m/s.\nMomentum = 5 × (−1) = −5 kg m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"At any instant the velocity of a particle of mass 500 g is (2tî + 3t²ĵ) ms⁻¹. If the force acting on the particle at t = 1 s is (î + xĵ) N, then the value of x will be","o":["2","3","6","4"],"c":1,"s":"Acceleration = dv/dt = 2î + 6t ĵ. At t = 1 s: a = 2î + 6ĵ m/s².\nForce = ma = 0.5 × (2î + 6ĵ) = î + 3ĵ N.\nCompare with î + xĵ: x = 3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 11 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"An average force of 125 N is applied on a machine gun firing bullets each of mass 10 g at the speed of 250 m/s to keep it in position. The number of bullets fired per second by the machine gun is","o":["5","50","100","25"],"c":1,"s":"To keep the gun in position, the applied force balances the recoil force from the bullets.\nMomentum given to bullets per second = n × m × v = n × 0.01 × 250 = 2.5n.\nSet 2.5n = 125, so n = 50.\n50 bullets are fired per second."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 11 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A body of mass 500 g moves along x-axis such that it's velocity varies with displacement x according to the relation v = 10√x m/s, the force acting on the body is","o":["166 N","125 N","5 N","25 N"],"c":3,"s":"v² = 100x. Differentiate with respect to x: 2v dv/dx = 100, so v dv/dx = 50.\nAcceleration a = v dv/dx = 50 m/s² (constant).\nF = ma = 0.5 × 50 = 25 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"Three forces F₁ = 10 N, F₂ = 8 N, F₃ = 6 N are acting on a particle of mass 5 kg. The forces F₂ and F₃ are applied perpendicular so that particle remains at rest. If the force F₁ is removed, then the acceleration of the particle is","o":["0.5 ms⁻²","2 ms⁻²","4.8 ms⁻²","7 ms⁻²"],"c":1,"s":"The particle is at rest, so the three forces balance. F₂ and F₃ are perpendicular, so their resultant is √(8² + 6²) = 10 N, equal and opposite to F₁.\nIf F₁ is removed, the net force becomes 10 N (the resultant of F₂ and F₃).\na = F/m = 10/5 = 2 m/s²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 15 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"The position vector of a particle related to time t is given by r⃗ = (10tî + 15t²ĵ + 7k̂) m. The direction of net force experienced by the particle is","o":["positive y-axis","positive x-axis","positive z-axis","in x-y plane"],"c":0,"s":"Velocity = dr⃗/dt = 10î + 30t ĵ.\nAcceleration = dv/dt = 30 ĵ m/s², which is constant and along +y.\nForce = m × acceleration, so the net force is along the positive y-axis."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"Figures (a), (b), (c) and (d) show variation of force with time.\nThe impulse is highest in figure","fig":{"w":340,"h":290,"alt":"Four force versus time graphs. (a) triangle with peak 0.5 N over 1.0 s; (b) rectangle of height 0.5 N lasting 2.0 s; (c) force falling linearly from 0.75 N to zero over 1.0 s; (d) force rising linearly from zero to 0.5 N over 2.0 s then dropping to zero.","d":[["l",30,110,30,35,"a"],["l",30,110,170,110,"a"],["t",24,40,"F(N)","e"],["t",170,124,"t(s)","e"],["t",95,140,"Figure (a)","m"],["p","M30 110 L65 60 L100 110"],["l",30,60,65,60,"d"],["t",24,64,"0.5","e"],["t",100,124,"1.0","m"],["t",24,114,"0","e"],["l",205,110,205,35,"a"],["l",205,110,345,110,"a"],["t",199,40,"F(N)","e"],["t",345,124,"t(s)","e"],["t",270,140,"Figure (b)","m"],["p","M205 60 L305 60 L305 110"],["t",199,64,"0.5","e"],["t",305,124,"2.0","m"],["t",199,114,"0","e"],["l",30,250,30,175,"a"],["l",30,250,170,250,"a"],["t",24,180,"F(N)","e"],["t",170,264,"t(s)","e"],["t",95,280,"Figure (c)","m"],["p","M30 190 L100 250"],["t",24,194,"0.75","e"],["t",100,264,"1.0","m"],["t",24,254,"0","e"],["l",205,250,205,175,"a"],["l",205,250,345,250,"a"],["t",199,180,"F(N)","e"],["t",345,264,"t(s)","e"],["t",270,280,"Figure (d)","m"],["p","M205 250 L305 205 L305 250"],["l",205,205,305,205,"d"],["t",199,209,"0.5","e"],["t",305,264,"2.0","m"],["t",199,254,"0","e"]]},"o":["Figure (b)","Figure (c)","Figure (a)","Figure (d)"],"c":0,"s":"Impulse = area under the force–time graph.\n(a) Triangle: ½ × 1.0 × 0.5 = 0.25 N s.\n(b) Rectangle: 0.5 × 2.0 = 1.0 N s.\n(c) Triangle: ½ × 1.0 × 0.75 = 0.375 N s.\n(d) Triangle: ½ × 2.0 × 0.5 = 0.5 N s.\nThe largest area, and so the highest impulse, is in Figure (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 24 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"As per given figure, a weightless pulley P is attached on a double inclined frictionless surfaces. The tension in the string (massless) will be (if, g = 10 m/s²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":230,"alt":"A double incline with a pulley P at the top. A 4 kg block on the steep 60 degree face is joined by a string over the pulley to a 1 kg block on the 30 degree face.","d":[["p","M20 200 L89 80 L297 200 Z"],["l",10,200,310,200],["p","M49 149.5 L60 130.5 L46.1 122.5 L35.1 141.5 Z","f"],["t",28,125,"4 kg","e"],["p","M200.8 144.5 L185.2 135.5 L192.2 123.4 L207.8 132.4 Z","f"],["t",215,126,"1 kg"],["c",89,73,8],["l",53,126.5,83,76],["l",188.7,129.5,96,76],["t",100,58,"P"],["t",38,194,"60°"],["t",262,194,"30°"]]},"o":["(4√3 − 1) N","4(√3 − 1) N","(4√3 + 1) N","4(√3 + 1) N"],"c":3,"s":"Masses: 4 kg on the 60° face, 1 kg on the 30° face. The 4 kg block slides down.\nDriving force along the string = 4g sin60° − 1g sin30° = 40 × (√3/2) − 10 × ½ = 20√3 − 5.\nAcceleration a = (20√3 − 5)/(4 + 1) = 4√3 − 1 m/s².\nFor the 1 kg block moving up its slope: T − 1g sin30° = 1 × a.\nT = 5 + 4√3 − 1 = 4√3 + 4 = 4(√3 + 1) N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A force acts for 20 s on a body of mass 20 kg, starting from rest, after which the force ceases and then body describes 50 m in the next 10 s. The value of force will be","o":["20 N","10 N","40 N","5 N"],"c":3,"s":"After the force stops, the body moves with constant speed: v = 50 m / 10 s = 5 m/s.\nThis is the speed gained in the first 20 s from rest: a = 5/20 = 0.25 m/s².\nF = ma = 20 × 0.25 = 5 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"The figure represents the momentum time (p-t) curve for a particle moving along an axis under the influence of the force. Identify the regions on the graph where the magnitude of the force is maximum and minimum respectively?\nIf (t₃ − t₂) < t₁","fig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"Momentum versus time graph with three regions: a, a moderate rise from time 0 to t1; b, a very gentle rise from t1 to t2; c, a steep drop from t2 to t3, where t3 minus t2 is less than t1.","d":[["l",40,170,40,20,"a"],["l",40,170,320,170,"a"],["t",32,28,"p","e"],["t",322,184,"t","e"],["p","M40 130 L100 95 L230 80 L262 165"],["l",100,95,100,170,"d"],["l",230,80,230,170,"d"],["l",262,165,262,170,"d"],["t",100,184,"t₁","m"],["t",226,184,"t₂","m"],["t",266,184,"t₃"],["t",62,100,"a","m"],["t",165,70,"b","m"],["t",276,118,"c"]]},"o":["c and b","a and b","b and c","c and a"],"c":0,"s":"Force = slope of the p–t graph (F = dp/dt), so a steeper slope means a larger force.\nRegion a: moderate positive slope. Region b: almost flat, so the smallest slope.\nRegion c: momentum falls quickly in a time (t₃ − t₂) shorter than t₁, so it has the steepest slope.\nMaximum force is in c and minimum force is in b."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"100 balls each of mass m moving with speed v simultaneously strike a wall normally and reflected back with same speed, in time t s. The total force exerted by the balls on the wall is","o":["{100mv;t}","200 mvt","{200mv;t}","{mv;100t}"],"c":2,"s":"Each ball reverses direction, so its change in momentum is mv − (−mv) = 2mv.\nFor 100 balls the total change in momentum = 100 × 2mv = 200mv.\nForce = change in momentum / time = 200mv/t."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"As shown in figure, a 70 kg garden roller is pushed with a force of F⃗ = 200 N at an angle of 30° with horizontal. The normal reaction on the roller is\n(Given, g = 10 m s⁻²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"A garden roller on the ground, pushed by a force F of 200 N whose line of action makes 30 degrees below the horizontal through its centre.","d":[["l",30,160,310,160,"B"],["c",180,105,55],["l",50,105,300,105,"d"],["l",85,50,180,105,"a"],["t",70,44,"F = 200 N","m"],["t",138,102,"30°","e","s"]]},"o":["800 N","600 N","200√3 N","800√2 N"],"c":0,"s":"The push is directed downwards at 30° to the horizontal, so it has a downward vertical component F sin30° = 200 × ½ = 100 N.\nVertical balance: N = mg + F sin30°.\nN = 70 × 10 + 100 = 800 N."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2023","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A particle of mass m is moving in the xy-plane such that its velocity at a point (x, y) is given as v⃗ = α(yx̂ + 2xŷ), where α is a non-zero constant. What is the force F⃗ acting on the particle?","o":["F⃗ = 2mα²(xx̂ + yŷ)","F⃗ = mα²(yx̂ + 2xŷ)","F⃗ = 2mα²(yx̂ + xŷ)","F⃗ = mα²(xx̂ + 2yŷ)"],"c":0,"s":"Velocity components: vₓ = αy and v_y = 2αx.\nAcceleration: aₓ = dvₓ/dt = α dy/dt = α v_y = 2α²x.\na_y = d v_y/dt = 2α dx/dt = 2α vₓ = 2α²y.\nSo a = 2α²(x x̂ + y ŷ) and F = ma = 2mα²(x x̂ + y ŷ)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"Three masses M = 100 kg, m₁ = 10 kg and m₂ = 20 kg are arranged in a system as shown in figure. All the surfaces are frictionless and strings are inextensible and weightless. The pulleys are also weightless and frictionless. A force F is applied on the system so that the mass m₂ moves upward with an acceleration of 2 m s⁻². The value of F is\n(Take g = 10 m s⁻²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":170,"alt":"Block M of 100 kg on a smooth table is pushed to the right by force F. Block m1 of 10 kg rests on top of M and is joined by a string over a pulley at the top right corner of M to block m2 of 20 kg that hangs against the right side of M.","d":[["l",75,140,230,140],["r",120,90,110,50,"f"],["t",175,120,"M = 100 kg","m"],["r",140,62,28,28,"f"],["t",154,52,"m₁ = 10 kg","m"],["c",232,84,7],["l",168,77,232,77],["l",239,84,239,118],["r",230,118,20,24,"f"],["t",260,134,"m₂ = 20 kg"],["l",68,115,118,115,"a"],["t",90,106,"F","m"]]},"o":["3360 N","3380 N","3120 N","3240 N"],"c":0,"s":"F pushes M to the right. Block m₂ hangs against the side of M, so it shares M's horizontal acceleration A. Block m₁ moves 2 m/s² (equal to m₂'s upward acceleration) to the left relative to M.\nm₂ vertically: T − m₂g = m₂ × 2, so T = 20 × 12 = 240 N.\nm₁ horizontally (towards the pulley): T = m₁(A − 2), so 240 = 10(A − 2) and A = 26 m/s².\nHorizontal external force on the whole system is only F: F = MA + m₁(A − 2) + m₂A.\nF = 100 × 26 + 240 + 20 × 26 = 2600 + 240 + 520 = 3360 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Lift and apparent weight","q":"A monkey of mass 50 kg climbs on a rope which can withstand the tension (T) of 350 N. If monkey initially climbs down with an acceleration of 4 m/s² and then climbs up with an acceleration of 5 m/s². Choose the correct option (g = 10 m/s²)","o":["T = 700 N while climbing upward","T = 350 N while going downward","Rope will break while climbing upward","Rope will break while going downward"],"c":2,"s":"Climbing down with acceleration 4 m/s² downward: mg − T = ma, so T = 50 × (10 − 4) = 300 N, which is less than 350 N. The rope holds.\nClimbing up with acceleration 5 m/s² upward: T − mg = ma, so T = 50 × (10 + 5) = 750 N.\n750 N is more than 350 N, so the rope breaks while the monkey climbs upward."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"A ball of mass 0.15 kg hits the wall with its initial speed of 12 m s⁻¹ and bounces back without changing its initial speed. If the force applied by the wall on the ball during the contact is 100 N, calculate the time duration of the contact of ball with the wall.","o":["0.018 s","0.036 s","0.009 s","0.072 s"],"c":1,"s":"The ball reverses direction at the same speed, so the change in momentum = 2mv = 2 × 0.15 × 12 = 3.6 kg m/s.\nImpulse = F × t, so t = 3.6 / 100.\nt = 0.036 s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"Two masses M₁ and M₂ are tied together at the two ends of a light inextensible string that passes over a frictionless pulley. When the mass M₂ is twice that of M₁, the acceleration of the system is a₁. When the mass M₂ is thrice that of M₁, the acceleration is a₂. The ratio {a₁;a₂} will be","o":["{1;3}","{2;3}","{3;2}","{1;2}"],"c":1,"s":"Atwood machine: a = g(M₂ − M₁)/(M₁ + M₂).\nM₂ = 2M₁: a₁ = g(2 − 1)/(2 + 1) = g/3.\nM₂ = 3M₁: a₂ = g(3 − 1)/(3 + 1) = g/2.\na₁/a₂ = (g/3)/(g/2) = 2/3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block 'A' takes 2 s to slide down a frictionless incline of 30° and length 'l' kept inside a lift going up with uniform velocity v. If the incline is changed to 45°, the time taken by the block, to slide down the incline, will be approximately","o":["2.66 s","0.83 s","1.68 s","0.70 s"],"c":2,"s":"The lift moves with uniform velocity, so it is an inertial frame and there is no pseudo force. The block slides with a = g sinθ.\nFrom l = ½ g sinθ t²: t² = 2l/(g sinθ), so t is proportional to 1/√(sinθ).\nt₄₅/t₃₀ = √(sin30°/sin45°) = √(0.5/0.7071) = 0.841.\nt₄₅ = 2 × 0.841 ≈ 1.68 s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"In two different experiments, an object of mass 5 kg moving with a speed of 25 m s⁻¹ hits two different walls and comes to rest within (i) 3 second, (ii) 5 seconds, respectively. Choose the correct option out of the following:","o":["Impulse and average force acting on the object will be same for both the cases.","Impulse will be same for the cases but the average force will be different.","Average force will be same for both the cases but the impulse will be different.","Average force and impulse will be different for both the cases."],"c":1,"s":"Impulse = change in momentum = 5 × 25 = 125 N s in both cases, since the mass, the initial speed and the final speed (zero) are the same.\nAverage force = impulse / time. In (i) it is 125/3 ≈ 41.7 N and in (ii) it is 125/5 = 25 N.\nSo the impulse is the same, but the average force is different."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"A balloon has mass of 10 g in air. The air escapes from the balloon at a uniform rate with velocity 4.5 cm/s. If the balloon shrinks in 5 s completely. Then, the average force acting on that balloon will be (in dyne)","o":["3","9","12","18"],"c":1,"s":"Work in CGS units. Mass of air = 10 g, escape speed = 4.5 cm/s, time = 5 s.\nRate of mass loss = 10/5 = 2 g/s.\nForce (thrust) = (dm/dt) × v = 2 × 4.5 = 9 g cm/s² = 9 dyne."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"A pressure-pump has a horizontal tube of cross sectional area 10 cm² for the outflow of water at a speed of 20 m/s. The force exerted on the vertical wall just in front of the tube which stops water horizontally flowing out of the tube, is\n[Given : density of water = 1000 kg/m³]","o":["300 N","500 N","250 N","400 N"],"c":3,"s":"Area A = 10 cm² = 10⁻³ m². Mass of water hitting the wall per second = ρAv = 1000 × 10⁻³ × 20 = 20 kg/s.\nThe water is stopped, so force = (dm/dt) × v = 20 × 20.\nF = 400 N (equivalently ρAv² = 1000 × 10⁻³ × 400)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"A uniform metal chain of mass m and length 'L' passes over a massless and frictionless pulley. It is released from rest with a part of its length 'l' is hanging on one side and rest of its length 'L − l' is hanging on the other side of the pulley. At a certain point of time, when l = {L;x}, the acceleration of the chain is {g;2}. The value of x is ______.","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"A chain hangs over a pulley with length l on the left side and the longer length L minus l on the right side.","d":[["l",150,15,190,15],["l",170,15,170,24],["c",170,40,16],["l",154,40,154,100,"B"],["l",186,40,186,160,"B"],["t",146,76,"l","e"],["t",196,105,"L − l"]]},"o":["6","2","1.5","4"],"c":3,"s":"The chain is released with L − l hanging on the longer side, so it moves towards that side.\nUnbalanced weight = weight of the longer part − weight of the shorter part = (m/L)g[(L − l) − l].\nAcceleration a = (unbalanced weight)/m = g(L − 2l)/L.\nSet a = g/2: (L − 2l)/L = ½, so l = L/4.\nTherefore x = 4."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Lift and apparent weight","q":"A person is standing in an elevator. In which situation, he experiences weight loss?","o":["When the elevator moves upward with constant acceleration.","When the elevator moves downward with constant acceleration.","When the elevator moves upward with uniform velocity.","When the elevator moves downward with uniform velocity."],"c":1,"s":"Apparent weight is the normal reaction N from the floor.\nMoving up with acceleration a: N = m(g + a), which is more than mg (weight gain).\nMoving down with acceleration a: N = m(g − a), which is less than mg (weight loss).\nWith uniform velocity, a = 0 and N = mg (no change).\nSo weight loss happens when the elevator moves downward with constant acceleration."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"In the arrangement shown in figure a₁, a₂, a₃ and a₄ are the accelerations of masses m₁, m₂, m₃ and m₄ respectively. Which of the following relation is true for this arrangement?","fig":{"w":340,"h":240,"alt":"Mass m1 hangs on one side of a fixed pulley; the other side holds a movable pulley. A string over this movable pulley carries m2 on one side and a second movable pulley on the other. A string over the second movable pulley carries m3 and m4.","d":[["l",140,15,220,15],["l",180,15,180,25],["c",180,35,10],["l",170,35,170,55],["r",156,55,28,20,"f"],["t",170,70,"m₁","m"],["l",190,35,190,80],["c",190,95,16],["l",174,95,174,150],["r",160,150,28,20,"f"],["t",174,165,"m₂","m"],["l",206,95,206,120],["c",206,132,12],["l",194,132,194,200],["r",180,200,28,20,"f"],["t",194,215,"m₃","m"],["l",218,132,218,170],["r",204,170,28,20,"f"],["t",218,185,"m₄","m"]]},"o":["4a₁ + 2a₂ + a₃ + a₄ = 0","a₁ + 4a₂ + 3a₃ + a₄ = 0","a₁ + 4a₂ + 3a₃ + 2a₄ = 0","2a₁ + 2a₂ + 3a₃ + a₄ = 0"],"c":0,"s":"Take downward as positive and use constant string lengths. Let the movable pulleys be P₂ and P₃.\nString over the fixed pulley: m₁ and P₂ move oppositely, so a(P₂) = −a₁.\nString over P₂ holds m₂ and P₃: a₂ + a(P₃) = 2a(P₂) = −2a₁, so a(P₃) = −2a₁ − a₂.\nString over P₃ holds m₃ and m₄: a₃ + a₄ = 2a(P₃) = −4a₁ − 2a₂.\nHence 4a₁ + 2a₂ + a₃ + a₄ = 0."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"A block of metal weighing 2 kg is resting on a frictionless plane (as shown in figure). It is struck by a jet releasing water at a rate of 1 kg s⁻¹ and at a speed of 10 m s⁻¹. Then, the initial acceleration of the block, in m s⁻², will be","fig":{"w":340,"h":150,"alt":"A 2 kg block on a frictionless plane is hit horizontally by a water jet from a nozzle on the left; the block's acceleration is to be found.","d":[["l",20,130,320,130],["r",20,82,60,16,"f"],["l",80,90,190,90,"a"],["t",135,76,"water jet","m","s"],["r",190,70,60,60,"f"],["t",220,105,"2 kg","m"],["l",250,100,300,100,"a"],["t",275,92,"a = ?","m"]]},"o":["3","6","5","4"],"c":2,"s":"The water hits the block and stops horizontally, so all its horizontal momentum is given to the block.\nForce = (dm/dt) × v = 1 kg/s × 10 m/s = 10 N.\nAcceleration of the block = F/m = 10/2 = 5 m/s²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Lift and apparent weight","q":"A block of mass M placed inside a box descends vertically with acceleration 'a'. The block exerts a force equal to one-fourth of its weight on the floor of the box. The value of 'a' will be","o":["{g;4}","{g;2}","{3g;4}","g"],"c":2,"s":"The force on the floor equals the normal reaction N = Mg/4.\nThe block moves down with acceleration a: Mg − N = Ma.\nMg − Mg/4 = Ma, so a = 3g/4."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 1 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on block and wedge","q":"A block of mass m slides on the wooden wedge, which in turn slides backward on the horizontal surface. The acceleration of the block with respect to the wedge is\n(Given m = 8 kg, M = 16 kg). Assume all the surfaces shown in the figure to be frictionless.","fig":{"w":300,"h":165,"alt":"A right-angled wedge of mass M with a 30 degree angle at the bottom right, a block m on its sloping face, and an arrow showing the wedge sliding left on the ground","d":[["l",20,140,280,140],["p","M80 140 V53 L230 140 Z"],["p","M97 63 L116 74 L123 62 L104 51 Z","f"],["t",100,44,"m","s"],["t",100,120,"M","m"],["t",196,134,"30°","s"],["l",70,100,30,100,"a"]]},"o":["{3;5}g","{4;3}g","{6;5}g","{2;3}g"],"c":3,"s":"Wedge angle θ = 30°. All surfaces are frictionless, so the wedge also moves (backwards) when the block slides.\nLet A be the wedge acceleration. In the wedge frame the block feels a pseudo force mA (horizontal) as well as mg and N.\nWedge (horizontal): N sinθ = M A. Block (perpendicular to slope): N = m g cosθ − m A sinθ.\nSolving: A = m g sinθ cosθ/(M + m sin²θ).\nBlock along the slope (relative to wedge): a = g sinθ + A cosθ = (M + m) g sinθ/(M + m sin²θ).\na = (24 × 0.5 g)/(16 + 8 × 0.25) = 12g/18 = 2g/3.\nAnswer: (d) 2g/3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 1 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"An object of mass 'm' is being moved with a constant velocity under the action of an applied force of 2 N along a frictionless surface with the surface profile shown in the figure (a triangle of base D with equal base angles θ). The correct applied force vs distance graph will be","fig":{"w":340,"h":262,"alt":"A triangular frictionless surface profile of base D with equal slope angles, and four force versus distance graphs (a) to (d) with values of 2 N and minus 2 N","d":[["p","M90 40 L170 4 L250 40 Z"],["l",90,40,250,40],["l",90,48,250,48,"b"],["t",170,59,"D","ms"],["t",112,36,"θ","s"],["t",226,36,"θ","s"],["t",4,76,"(a)","s"],["l",30,159,30,69,"a"],["l",12,113,150,113,"a"],["l",27,87,33,87],["t",25,91,"2N","es"],["l",27,139,33,139],["t",25,143,"−2N","es"],["t",34,72,"F","s"],["t",146,124,"x","s"],["t",120,124,"D","ms"],["p","M30 113 V139 H75 V87 H120 V113","B"],["t",174,76,"(b)","s"],["l",200,159,200,69,"a"],["l",182,113,320,113,"a"],["l",197,87,203,87],["t",195,91,"2N","es"],["l",197,139,203,139],["t",195,143,"−2N","es"],["t",204,72,"F","s"],["t",316,124,"x","s"],["t",290,124,"D","ms"],["p","M200 113 L245 139 L290 113","B"],["t",4,174,"(c)","s"],["l",30,257,30,167,"a"],["l",12,211,150,211,"a"],["l",27,185,33,185],["t",25,189,"2N","es"],["l",27,237,33,237],["t",25,241,"−2N","es"],["t",34,170,"F","s"],["t",146,222,"x","s"],["t",120,222,"D","ms"],["p","M30 211 L75 185 L120 211","B"],["t",174,174,"(d)","s"],["l",200,257,200,167,"a"],["l",182,211,320,211,"a"],["l",197,185,203,185],["t",195,189,"2N","es"],["l",197,237,203,237],["t",195,241,"−2N","es"],["t",204,170,"F","s"],["t",316,222,"x","s"],["t",290,222,"D","ms"],["p","M200 211 V185 H245 V237 H290 V211","B"]]},"o":["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],"c":3,"s":"Constant velocity means zero net force along the surface, so the applied force must balance the component of weight, mg sinθ = 2 N.\nOn the rising slope (first half, 0 to D/2) the force must push the object up the slope, along the motion: +2 N.\nOn the falling slope (second half, D/2 to D) gravity pulls the object along its motion, so the applied force must act backwards: −2 N.\nThe angles are equal, so the force is a constant +2 N, then a constant −2 N, each for a distance D/2. It is a step graph, not a triangle.\nAnswer: graph (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"The initial mass of a rocket is 1000 kg. Calculate at what rate the fuel should be burnt so that the rocket is given an acceleration of 20 m s⁻². The gases come out at a relative speed of 500 m s⁻¹ with respect to the rocket. [Use g = 10 m s⁻²]","o":["10 kg s⁻¹","60 kg s⁻¹","500 kg s⁻¹","6.0 × 10² kg s⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"Thrust of the rocket = (relative speed of gas) × (rate of burning) = u (dm/dt).\nNet force on the rocket: u (dm/dt) − m g = m a.\nSo u (dm/dt) = m (g + a) = 1000 × (10 + 20) = 30000 N.\ndm/dt = 30000/500 = 60 kg/s.\nAnswer: (b) 60 kg s⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"The boxes of masses 2 kg and 8 kg are connected by a string passing over smooth pulleys. Calculate the time taken by box of mass 8 kg to strike the ground starting from rest. (use g = 10 m/s²)","fig":{"w":300,"h":190,"alt":"A massless pulley carrying the 8 kg block hangs from a string fixed to the ceiling, the string goes over a fixed pulley to a 2 kg block resting on the ground, the 8 kg block is 20 cm above the ground","d":[["l",20,8,280,8,"B"],["c",149,45,15],["c",149,45,2,"F"],["c",120,72,14],["c",120,72,2,"F"],["p","M106 8 V72 A14 14 0 0 0 134 72 V45 A15 15 0 0 1 164 45 V148"],["l",120,72,120,104],["r",104,104,32,28,"f"],["t",140,122,"8 kg","s"],["r",150,148,28,24,"f"],["t",182,164,"2 kg","s"],["l",20,172,280,172],["l",96,134,96,171,"b"],["t",92,156,"20 cm","es"]]},"o":["0.25 s","0.34 s","0.2 s","0.4 s"],"c":3,"s":"The 8 kg box hangs from a movable pulley (string fixed to the ceiling), so if it moves down by x the 2 kg box moves up by 2x. So a₂ = 2a₁.\n2 kg box: T − 20 = 2a₂ = 4a₁. 8 kg box (two string segments pull up): 80 − 2T = 8a₁.\nSubstitute T = 20 + 4a₁: 80 − 40 − 8a₁ = 8a₁, so a₁ = 2.5 m/s² and T = 30 N (more than 20 N, so the 2 kg box does rise).\nThe 8 kg box falls 0.2 m: 0.2 = ½ × 2.5 × t², so t² = 0.16.\nt = 0.4 s. Answer: (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 25 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A force F⃗ = (40î + 10ĵ) N acts on a body of mass 5 kg. If the body starts from rest, its position vector r⃗ at time t = 10 s, will be","o":["(100î + 400ĵ) m","(400î + 100ĵ) m","(100î + 100ĵ) m","(400î + 400ĵ) m"],"c":1,"s":"Acceleration a⃗ = F⃗/m = (8î + 2ĵ) m/s², constant.\nStarting from rest: r⃗ = ½ a⃗ t².\nr⃗ = ½ × (8î + 2ĵ) × 10² = 50 × (8î + 2ĵ) = (400î + 100ĵ) m.\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A particle of mass M originally at rest is subjected to a force whose direction is constant but magnitude varies with time according to the relation\nF = F₀[1 − ({t − T;T})²]\nwhere F₀ and T are constants. The force acts only for the time interval 2T. The velocity v of the particle after time 2T is","o":["{4F₀T;3M}","{F₀T;3M}","{F₀T;2M}","{2F₀T;M}"],"c":0,"s":"Impulse = ∫F dt = change in momentum = M v (starts from rest).\nPut u = t − T. As t goes from 0 to 2T, u goes from −T to T.\n∫F dt = F₀ ∫(1 − u²/T²) du from −T to T = F₀ [2T − 2T³/(3T²)] = F₀ (2T − 2T/3) = 4F₀T/3.\nSo M v = 4F₀T/3, v = 4F₀T/(3M).\nAnswer: (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"Two masses A and B, each of mass M are fixed together by a massless spring. A force acts on the mass B as shown in figure. If the mass A starts moving away from mass B with acceleration 'a' then the acceleration of mass B will be","fig":{"w":300,"h":90,"alt":"Two masses A and B joined by a spring, with a force F pulling B away to the left","d":[["c",100,45,8,"F"],["c",230,45,8,"F"],["p","M108 45 H125 L131 31 L143 59 L155 31 L167 59 L179 31 L191 59 L197 45 H222"],["l",92,45,35,45,"a"],["t",62,36,"F","m"],["t",100,74,"B","m"],["t",230,74,"A","m"]]},"o":["{Ma−F;M}","{MF;F+Ma}","{F+Ma;M}","{F−Ma;M}"],"c":3,"s":"Mass A accelerates with a, so the spring pulls A with a force M a (towards B). By Newton's third law, the spring pulls B towards A with the same force M a.\nThis spring force on B acts opposite to F.\nFor B: F − M a = M a_B.\na_B = (F − M a)/M.\nAnswer: (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A small ball of mass m is thrown upward with velocity u from the ground. The ball experiences a resistive force mkv² where v is its speed. The maximum height attained by the ball is","o":["{1;k} ln(1 + {ku²;2g})","{1;k} tan⁻¹({ku²;2g})","{1;2k} tan⁻¹({ku²;g})","{1;2k} ln(1 + {ku²;g})"],"c":3,"s":"Going up, gravity and drag both act downwards: m a = −(m g + m k v²), so a = −(g + k v²).\nWrite a = v dv/dh: v dv/(g + k v²) = −dh.\nIntegrate from v = u (h = 0) to v = 0 (h = H): H = ∫₀ᵘ v dv/(g + k v²) = (1/2k) ln[(g + k u²)/g].\nH = {1;2k} ln(1 + ku²/g).\nAnswer: (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 5 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"A spaceship in space sweeps stationary interplanetary dust. As a result, its mass increases at a rate {dM(t);dt} = bv²(t), where v(t) is its instantaneous velocity. The instantaneous acceleration of the satellite is","o":["−bv³(t)","−{bv³(t);M(t)}","−{2bv³(t);M(t)}","−{bv³(t);2M(t)}"],"c":1,"s":"The dust is at rest, so it brings no momentum. Momentum of the ship is M v, and no external force acts.\nd(Mv)/dt = 0, so M dv/dt + v dM/dt = 0.\nM dv/dt = −v (b v²) = −b v³.\nAcceleration = −b v³(t)/M(t).\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 9 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"A ball is thrown vertically up (taken as +z-axis) from the ground. The correct momentum-height (p-h) diagram is","fig":{"w":340,"h":250,"alt":"Four momentum versus height graphs (a) to (d): two with straight lines, one semi-elliptical arc and one parabola opening towards the origin","d":[["t",4,12,"(a)","s"],["l",60,118,60,6,"a"],["l",20,62,160,62,"a"],["t",66,12,"p","s"],["t",156,56,"h","es"],["t",55,74,"O","es"],["p","M60 17 L120 62 L60 107","B"],["t",174,12,"(b)","s"],["l",230,118,230,6,"a"],["l",190,62,330,62,"a"],["t",236,12,"p","s"],["t",326,56,"h","es"],["t",225,74,"O","es"],["p","M230 17 A60 45 0 0 1 230 107","B"],["t",4,137,"(c)","s"],["l",60,243,60,131,"a"],["l",20,187,160,187,"a"],["t",66,137,"p","s"],["t",156,181,"h","es"],["t",55,199,"O","es"],["p","M60 142 Q120 165 120 187 Q120 209 60 232","B"],["t",174,137,"(d)","s"],["l",230,243,230,131,"a"],["l",190,187,330,187,"a"],["t",236,137,"p","s"],["t",326,181,"h","es"],["t",225,199,"O","es"],["p","M230 142 L290 187 L210 239","B"]]},"o":["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],"c":2,"s":"Let the initial momentum be p₀ = m u. At height h, v² = u² − 2 g h, so p² = p₀² − 2 m² g h.\nThis is a parabola (h is linear in p²), not a straight line and not a circle or ellipse.\nAt h = 0 the momentum is +p₀ (up). It falls to zero at the top, h = u²/2g, with the curve meeting the h-axis at a right angle (vertex).\nOn the way down the momentum is negative, and |p| grows again as h decreases, giving the lower half of the same parabola.\nThe curve is symmetric about the h-axis and bulges to the right: graph (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A ball is thrown upward with an initial velocity V₀ from the surface of the earth. The motion of the ball is affected by a drag force equal to mγv² (where m is mass of the ball, v is its instantaneous velocity and γ is a constant). Time taken by the ball to rise to its zenith is","o":["{1;√(γg)} tan⁻¹(√{γ;g} V₀)","{1;√(2γg)} tan⁻¹(√{2γ;g} V₀)","{1;√(γg)} sin⁻¹(√{γ;g} V₀)","{1;√(γg)} ln(1 + √{γ;g} V₀)"],"c":0,"s":"While rising, gravity and drag both act downwards: dv/dt = −(g + γ v²).\nSo dt = −dv/(g + γ v²). Time to rise from V₀ to 0: t = ∫₀^V₀ dv/(g + γ v²).\nUse ∫dv/(g + γ v²) = (1/√(gγ)) tan⁻¹(v √(γ/g)).\nt = {1;√(γg)} tan⁻¹(V₀ √(γ/g)).\nAnswer: (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A bullet of mass 20 g has an initial speed of 1 m s⁻¹, just before it starts penetrating a mud wall of thickness 20 cm. If the wall offers a mean resistance of 2.5 × 10⁻² N, the speed of the bullet after emerging from the other side of the wall is close to","o":["0.1 m s⁻¹","0.7 m s⁻¹","0.3 m s⁻¹","0.4 m s⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"Use the work-energy theorem. Work done by the resistance = change in kinetic energy.\n−F d = ½ m v² − ½ m u², with m = 0.02 kg, d = 0.2 m, F = 0.025 N.\n−0.025 × 0.2 = 0.01 v² − 0.01 × 1, so −0.005 = 0.01 v² − 0.01.\nv² = 0.5, v ≈ 0.7 m/s.\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A particle of mass m is moving in a straight line with momentum p. Starting at time t = 0, a force F = kt acts in the same direction on the moving particle during time interval T so that its momentum changes from p to 3p. Here k is a constant. The value of T is","o":["2√{p;k}","√{2k;p}","√{2p;k}","2√{k;p}"],"c":0,"s":"Impulse = change in momentum: ∫₀ᵀ F dt = 3p − p = 2p.\n∫₀ᵀ k t dt = ½ k T².\n½ k T² = 2p, so T² = 4p/k.\nT = 2√(p/k).\nAnswer: (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016 · Online","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A particle of mass m is acted upon by a force F given by the empirical law F = {R;t²} v(t). If this law is to be tested experimentally by observing the motion starting from rest, the best way is to plot","o":["log v(t) against 1/t","v(t) against t²","log v(t) against 1/t²","log v(t) against t"],"c":0,"s":"Newton's second law: m dv/dt = (R/t²) v.\nSeparate the variables: dv/v = (R/m) dt/t².\nIntegrate: ln v = −R/(m t) + constant.\nSo ln v (or log v) is a straight line when plotted against 1/t, with slope −R/m. That is the best test.\nAnswer: (a)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2012","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A particle of mass m is at rest at the origin at time t = 0. It is subjected to a force F(t) = F₀e⁻ᵇᵗ in the x-direction. Its speed v(t) is depicted by which of the following curves?","fig":{"w":340,"h":216,"alt":"Four speed versus time graphs (a) to (d): two rising and levelling off, with different level labels, and two that rise to a peak and then fall","d":[["t",4,12,"(a)","s"],["l",40,100,40,8,"a"],["l",40,75,160,75,"a"],["t",34,10,"v","es"],["t",158,88,"t","es"],["l",40,25,150,25,"d"],["t",36,28,"F₀b/m","es"],["p","M40 75 Q75 25 145 25","B"],["t",174,12,"(b)","s"],["l",210,100,210,8,"a"],["l",210,75,330,75,"a"],["t",204,10,"v","es"],["t",328,88,"t","es"],["l",210,25,320,25,"d"],["t",206,28,"F₀/mb","es"],["p","M210 75 Q245 25 315 25","B"],["t",4,120,"(c)","s"],["l",40,208,40,116,"a"],["l",40,183,160,183,"a"],["t",34,118,"v","es"],["t",158,196,"t","es"],["l",40,133,150,133,"d"],["t",36,136,"F₀/mb","es"],["p","M40 183 C65 148 80 133 95 133 C115 133 125 176 155 182","B"],["t",174,120,"(d)","s"],["l",210,208,210,116,"a"],["l",210,183,330,183,"a"],["t",204,118,"v","es"],["t",328,196,"t","es"],["l",210,133,320,133,"d"],["t",206,136,"F₀/mb","es"],["p","M210 183 C230 183 240 133 265 133 C290 133 295 182 325 183","B"]]},"o":["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],"c":1,"s":"m dv/dt = F₀ e^(−bt), starting from rest.\nIntegrate: v = (F₀/m) ∫₀ᵗ e^(−bt) dt = (F₀/mb)(1 − e^(−bt)).\nAt t = 0, v = 0. v keeps increasing (the force never becomes negative) but the slope falls, and v tends to the limit F₀/(mb) as t → ∞.\nSo the curve starts at the origin, rises and flattens at F₀/mb. Graphs (c) and (d) wrongly fall back, and graph (a) levels off at F₀b/m, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2010","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"Two fixed frictionless inclined planes making an angle 30° and 60° with the vertical are shown in the figure. Two blocks A and B are placed on the two planes. What is the relative vertical acceleration of A with respect to B?","fig":{"w":340,"h":170,"alt":"Two fixed frictionless inclined planes: plane A is steep with 60 degrees at its base and block A near its top, plane B is shallow with 30 degrees at its base and block B near its top","d":[["l",15,150,330,150],["p","M30 25 V150 H102 Z"],["p","M34 32 L43 47 L53 41 L44 26 Z","f"],["t",58,34,"A","s"],["t",96,144,"60°","es"],["p","M150 70 V150 H289 Z"],["p","M157 74 L172 83 L178 72 L163 63 Z","f"],["t",184,70,"B","s"],["t",262,144,"30°","es"]]},"o":["4.9 m s⁻² in vertical direction","4.9 m s⁻² in horizontal direction","9.8 m s⁻² in vertical direction","zero"],"c":0,"s":"Plane A makes 60° with the horizontal (30° with the vertical). Plane B makes 30° with the horizontal.\nOn a frictionless incline of angle θ, acceleration along the plane is g sinθ. Its vertical component is g sin²θ.\nBlock A: a_A(vertical) = g sin²60° = 3g/4. Block B: a_B(vertical) = g sin²30° = g/4. Both blocks move down.\nRelative vertical acceleration = 3g/4 − g/4 = g/2 = 4.9 m/s², in the vertical direction.\nAnswer: (a)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2010","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"The figure shows the position - time (x-t) graph of one-dimensional motion of a body of mass 0.4 kg. The magnitude of each impulse is","fig":{"w":340,"h":165,"alt":"Position versus time graph that zigzags between 0 and 2 metres, rising from 0 to 2 m in 2 s and falling back to 0 in the next 2 s, repeating until 16 s","d":[["l",40,130,40,12,"a"],["l",30,130,330,130,"a"],["t",34,24,"x (m)","es"],["t",36,45,"2","es"],["l",37,40,43,40],["l",40,40,312,40,"d"],["t",320,150,"t (s)","s"],["p","M40 130 L74 40 L108 130 L142 40 L176 130 L210 40 L244 130 L278 40 L312 130","B"],["l",40,127,40,133],["t",40,146,"0","ms"],["l",74,127,74,133],["t",74,146,"2","ms"],["l",108,127,108,133],["t",108,146,"4","ms"],["l",142,127,142,133],["t",142,146,"6","ms"],["l",176,127,176,133],["t",176,146,"8","ms"],["l",210,127,210,133],["t",210,146,"10","ms"],["l",244,127,244,133],["t",244,146,"12","ms"],["l",278,127,278,133],["t",278,146,"14","ms"],["l",312,127,312,133],["t",312,146,"16","ms"],["l",74,40,74,130,"d"],["l",142,40,142,130,"d"],["l",210,40,210,130,"d"],["l",278,40,278,130,"d"]]},"o":["0.2 N s","0.4 N s","0.8 N s","1.6 N s"],"c":2,"s":"Read the graph: x rises from 0 to 2 m in 2 s, so the speed is 1 m/s. It then falls from 2 m to 0 in 2 s, so the velocity is −1 m/s.\nThe velocity reverses at x = 2 m and x = 0 (the body bounces), and the speed stays 1 m/s.\nMomentum before = +0.4 × 1 = +0.4 kg m/s, after = −0.4 kg m/s.\nImpulse = change in momentum = 0.4 − (−0.4) = 0.8 N s.\nAnswer: (c)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2009","tp":"plf","st":"Pseudo force and accelerating frames","q":"A piece of wire is bent in the shape of a parabola y = kx² (y-axis vertical) with a bead of mass m on it. The bead can slide on the wire without friction. It stays at the lowest point of the parabola when the wire is at rest. The wire is now accelerated parallel to the x-axis with a constant acceleration a. The distance of the new equilibrium position of the bead, where the bead can stay at rest with respect to the wire, from the y-axis is","o":["{a;gk}","{a;2gk}","{2a;gk}","{a;4gk}"],"c":1,"s":"In the frame of the wire, the bead feels gravity m g (down) and a pseudo force m a (opposite to the wire's acceleration).\nFor rest on a smooth wire, the net force must be perpendicular to the wire, so the resultant of mg and ma is along the normal.\nIf the tangent makes angle θ with the horizontal, tanθ = ma/mg = a/g.\nSlope of the parabola: tanθ = dy/dx = 2kx. So 2kx = a/g.\nx = a/(2gk). Answer: (b)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2008","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"A body of mass m = 3.513 kg is moving along the x-axis with a speed of 5.00 m s⁻¹. The magnitude of its momentum is recorded as","o":["17.57 kg m s⁻¹","17.6 kg m s⁻¹","17.565 kg m s⁻¹","17.56 kg m s⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"Momentum p = m v = 3.513 × 5.00 = 17.565 kg m/s.\nIn a product the result keeps as many significant figures as the least precise factor. The speed 5.00 has 3 significant figures (the mass has 4), so the answer is quoted to 3 significant figures.\n17.565 rounds to 17.6 kg m/s.\nAnswer: (b) 17.6 kg m s⁻¹."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2007","tp":"plc","st":"Blocks pulled or pushed together","q":"A block of mass m is connected to another block of mass M by a spring (massless) of spring constant k. The blocks are kept on a smooth horizontal plane. Initially the blocks are at rest and the spring is unstretched. Then a constant force F starts acting on the block of mass M to pull it. Find the force of the block of mass m.","o":["{MF;m+M}","{mF;M}","{(M+m)F;m}","{mF;m+M}"],"c":3,"s":"The centre of mass of the two blocks has acceleration a = F/(M + m). The spring force on m oscillates about the value at which both blocks accelerate together, and that value (the equilibrium or mean spring force) is the one asked for.\nThe only horizontal force on m is the spring force, so for common acceleration it equals m a = mF/(M + m).\nAnswer: (d)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2007","tp":"pln","st":"Variable force, F(t) and F(x)","q":"A particle moves in the X-Y plane under the influence of a force such that its linear momentum is p⃗(t) = A[î cos(kt) − ĵ sin(kt)], where A and k are constants. The angle between the force and the momentum is","o":["0°","30°","45°","90°"],"c":3,"s":"Force F⃗ = dp⃗/dt = A k [−î sin(kt) − ĵ cos(kt)].\nDot product: F⃗·p⃗ = A²k [−sin(kt)cos(kt) + sin(kt)cos(kt)] = 0.\nZero dot product means the force is perpendicular to the momentum (the speed is constant, only the direction turns).\nAngle = 90°. Answer: (d)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2007","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"Two particles of mass m each are tied at the ends of a light string of length 2a. The whole system is kept on a frictionless horizontal surface with the string held tight so that each mass is at a distance a from the center P (as shown in the figure). Now, the mid-point of the string is pulled vertically upwards with a small but constant force F. As a result, the particles move towards each other on the surface. The magnitude of acceleration, when the separation between them becomes 2x is","fig":{"w":340,"h":125,"alt":"Two masses m at the two ends of a string of length 2a on a horizontal surface, each a distance a from the midpoint P, with an upward force F applied at P","d":[["l",50,95,290,95],["c",50,95,9,"F"],["c",290,95,9,"F"],["c",170,95,3,"F"],["l",170,95,170,25,"a"],["t",180,35,"F"],["t",170,112,"P","ms"],["t",50,78,"m","m"],["t",290,78,"m","m"],["l",50,120,170,120,"b"],["l",170,120,290,120,"b"],["t",110,118,"a","ms"],["t",230,118,"a","ms"]]},"o":["{F;2m} · {a;√(a²−x²)}","{F;2m} · {x;√(a²−x²)}","{F;2m} · {x;a}","{F;2m} · {√(a²−x²);x}"],"c":1,"s":"When the separation is 2x, each half of the string (length a) makes angle θ with the horizontal, where cosθ = x/a and sinθ = √(a²−x²)/a.\nVertical balance at P (light, small F): 2T sinθ = F, so T = F/(2 sinθ).\nHorizontal motion of each mass: m a_m = T cosθ.\na_m = (F/2m) cotθ = (F/2m) × x/√(a²−x²).\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2006","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"Two blocks A and B of masses 2m and m, respectively are connected by a massless and inextensible string. The whole system is suspended by a massless spring as shown in the figure. The magnitudes of acceleration of A and B, immediately after the string is cut, are respectively","fig":{"w":240,"h":175,"alt":"A spring hangs from the ceiling holding block A of mass 2m, with block B of mass m hanging below A on a string","d":[["l",60,10,140,10,"B"],["p","M100 10 V22 L108 28 L92 40 L108 52 L92 64 L108 70 L100 76 V82"],["r",80,82,40,26,"f"],["t",100,100,"A","m"],["t",126,100,"2m"],["l",100,108,100,134],["r",86,134,28,22,"f"],["t",100,150,"B","m"],["t",122,150,"m"]]},"o":["g, g/2","g/2, g","g, g","g/2, g/2"],"c":1,"s":"Before cutting, the spring holds both blocks: spring force = 3mg (upwards).\nRight after the string is cut, the spring force cannot change instantly. It is still 3mg.\nBlock A (2m): net force = 3mg − 2mg = mg upwards, so a_A = mg/2m = g/2.\nBlock B (m): only gravity acts, so a_B = g.\nAnswer: (b) g/2, g."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2005","tp":"plf","st":"Pseudo force and accelerating frames","q":"A block is kept on a frictionless inclined surface with angle of inclination α. The incline is given an acceleration a to keep the block stationary. Then a is equal to","fig":{"w":300,"h":185,"alt":"A wedge with angle alpha at the bottom right carrying a block on its slope, with an arrow showing the wedge accelerating to the right with acceleration a","d":[["p","M40 40 V140 H250 Z"],["p","M94 66 L121 79 L129 62 L102 50 Z","f"],["t",228,134,"α","es"],["l",100,160,170,160,"a"],["t",135,177,"a","ms"]]},"o":["g","g tanα","{g;tanα}","g cosecα"],"c":1,"s":"In the frame of the incline the block is at rest. Forces on it: weight mg, normal force N (perpendicular to the slope) and a pseudo force ma (horizontal, opposite to the acceleration).\nAlong the slope: m a cosα = m g sinα (the block does not slide).\nSo a = g tanα.\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2005","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A particle of mass 0.3 kg is subjected to a force F = −kx with k = 15 N/m. What will be its initial acceleration if it is released from a point 20 cm away from the origin?","o":["5 m/s²","10 m/s²","3 m/s²","15 m/s²"],"c":1,"s":"Initial force: |F| = k x = 15 × 0.20 = 3 N (x = 20 cm = 0.2 m).\nAcceleration = F/m = 3/0.3 = 10 m/s².\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2004","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"Two masses m₁ = 5 kg and m₂ = 4.8 kg tied to a string are hanging over a light frictionless pulley. What is the acceleration of the masses when lift is free to move? (g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["0.2 m/s²","9.8 m/s²","5 m/s²","4.8 m/s²"],"c":0,"s":"Atwood machine (pulley at rest): a = (m₁ − m₂) g/(m₁ + m₂).\na = (5 − 4.8) × 9.8/(5 + 4.8) = 0.2 × 9.8/9.8 = 0.2 m/s².\nAnswer: (a)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2004","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"A machine gun fires a bullet of mass 40 g with a velocity 1200 m s⁻¹. The man holding it can exert a maximum force of 144 N on the gun. How many bullets can he fire per second at the most?","o":["one","four","two","three"],"c":3,"s":"Momentum given to each bullet = 0.04 × 1200 = 48 kg m/s.\nIf n bullets are fired per second, the gun gets a recoil force F = n × 48 N.\nMaximum force the man can exert: 144 N, so n = 144/48 = 3.\nAnswer: (d) three."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2003","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A rocket with a lift-off mass 3.5 × 10⁴ kg is blasted upwards with an initial acceleration of 10 m/s². Then the initial thrust of the blast is","o":["3.5 × 10⁵ N","7.0 × 10⁵ N","14.0 × 10⁵ N","1.75 × 10⁵ N"],"c":1,"s":"Two forces act on the rocket: the thrust T upward and the weight mg downward.\nNewton’s second law: T − mg = ma, so T = m(g + a).\nT = 3.5 × 10⁴ × (10 + 10) = 7.0 × 10⁵ N, option (b).\n(The printed key shows (a), which would be m × a only and leaves out the weight.)"},
{"y":"AIEEE 2003","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A light spring balance hangs from the hook of the other light spring balance and a block of mass M kg hangs from the former one. Then the true statement about the scale reading is","o":["both the scales read M kg each","the scale of the lower one reads M kg and of the upper one zero","the reading of the two scales can be anything but the sum of the reading will be M kg","both the scales read M/2 kg"],"c":0,"s":"The block is in equilibrium, so the lower balance pulls up with a force Mg. Its reading is M kg.\nThe lower balance is light (massless), so the force on it from above must also be Mg, which means it pulls the upper balance down with Mg.\nThe upper balance therefore carries a force Mg, and also reads M kg.\nAnswer: (a) both read M kg."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2003","tp":"plc","st":"Blocks pulled or pushed together","q":"A block of mass M is pulled along a horizontal frictionless surface by a rope of mass m. If a force P is applied at the free end of the rope, the force exerted by the rope on the block is","o":["{Pm;M+m}","{Pm;M−m}","P","{PM;M+m}"],"c":3,"s":"Treat the rope and the block as one system of mass (M + m): a = P/(M + m).\nThe only horizontal force on the block is the pull of the rope, so it is M a.\nForce = M × P/(M + m) = PM/(M + m).\nAnswer: (d)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2003","tp":"pln","st":"Lift and apparent weight","q":"A spring balance is attached to the ceiling of a lift. A man hangs his bag on the spring and the spring reads 49 N, when the lift is stationary. If the lift moves downward with an acceleration of 5 m/s², the reading of the spring balance will be","o":["24 N","74 N","15 N","49 N"],"c":0,"s":"Lift at rest: weight = m g = 49 N, so m = 49/9.8 = 5 kg.\nLift accelerating downwards with a: m g − T = m a.\nT = m (g − a) = 5 × (9.8 − 5) = 24 N.\nAnswer: (a) 24 N."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"plc","st":"Blocks pulled or pushed together","q":"Three identical blocks of masses m = 2 kg are drawn by a force F = 10.2 N with an acceleration of 0.6 m s⁻² on a frictionless surface, then what is the tension (in N) in the string between the blocks B and C?","fig":{"w":340,"h":90,"alt":"Three blocks C, B and A in a row on a frictionless surface joined by strings, with force F pulling on block A to the right","d":[["l",20,64,320,64],["r",30,32,42,32,"f"],["t",51,53,"C","m"],["l",72,48,120,48],["r",120,32,42,32,"f"],["t",141,53,"B","m"],["l",162,48,210,48],["r",210,32,42,32,"f"],["t",231,53,"A","m"],["l",252,48,312,48,"a"],["t",318,52,"F"]]},"o":["9.2","7.8","4","9.8"],"c":1,"s":"Apply Newton's law to the blocks in front of the string BC, taking m a = 2 × 0.6 = 1.2 N for each block.\nBlock A: F − T_AB = m a, so T_AB = 10.2 − 1.2 = 9.0 N.\nBlock B: T_AB − T_BC = m a, so T_BC = 9.0 − 1.2 = 7.8 N.\nAnswer: (b) 7.8 N."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"One end of a massless rope, which passes over a massless and frictionless pulley P is tied to a hook C while the other end is free. Maximum tension that the rope can bear is 960 N. With what value of maximum safe acceleration (in m s⁻²) can a man of 60 kg climb on the rope?","o":["16","6","4","8"],"c":1,"s":"The man climbs the rope upwards with acceleration a. The rope pulls him up with tension T.\nT − m g = m a (take g = 10 m/s²).\nMaximum tension 960 N: 960 − 60 × 10 = 60 a.\n360 = 60 a, so a = 6 m/s².\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"When forces F₁, F₂, F₃ are acting on a particle of mass m such that F₂ and F₃ are mutually perpendicular, then the particle remains stationary. If the force F₁ is now removed then the acceleration of the particle is","o":["{F₁;m}","{F₂F₃;mF₁}","{F₂−F₃;m}","{F₂;m}"],"c":0,"s":"The particle is stationary, so the net force is zero: F₁ + F₂ + F₃ = 0 (as vectors).\nTherefore F₂ + F₃ = −F₁.\nIf F₁ is removed, the net force is F₂ + F₃, whose magnitude is F₁.\na = F₁/m. Answer: (a)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"pln","st":"Lift and apparent weight","q":"A lift is moving down with acceleration a. A man in the lift drops a ball inside the lift. The acceleration of the ball as observed by the man in the lift and a man standing stationary on the ground are respectively","o":["g, g","g − a, g − a","g − a, g","a, g"],"c":2,"s":"After release, only gravity acts on the ball. Relative to the ground, its acceleration is g (downwards).\nThe lift itself accelerates downwards with a. For the man inside the lift: relative acceleration = g − a.\nSo: inside observer sees g − a, ground observer sees g.\nAnswer: (c)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2001","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"A string of negligible mass going over a clamped pulley of mass m supports a block of mass M as shown in the figure. The force on the pulley by the clamp is given by","fig":{"w":240,"h":170,"alt":"A pulley clamped to a wall with a string running horizontally to the wall over the top of the pulley and a block M hanging from the other end","d":[["l",20,10,20,150,"B"],["l",20,50,132,50,"B"],["c",150,50,18],["c",150,50,2,"F"],["p","M20 32 H150 A18 18 0 0 1 168 50 V120"],["r",154,120,28,24,"f"],["t",168,137,"M","ms"],["t",134,70,"m","s"]]},"o":["√2 Mg","√2 mg","√((M+m)²+m²) g","√((M+m)²+M²) g"],"c":3,"s":"The block is at rest, so the string tension is T = Mg everywhere.\nThe string pulls the pulley with two forces of size Mg: one horizontal (along the horizontal part) and one vertical downwards (along the hanging part).\nThe pulley also has weight mg. The clamp must balance all of these.\nHorizontal force from the clamp: Mg. Vertical force from the clamp: Mg + mg = (M + m) g.\nResultant = √[(Mg)² + ((M + m)g)²] = √((M+m)² + M²) g.\nAnswer: (d)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1982","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"In the arrangement shown in the figure, the ends P and Q of an unstretchable string move downwards with uniform speed u. Pulleys A and B are fixed. Mass M moves upwards with a speed","fig":{"w":340,"h":185,"alt":"Two fixed pulleys A and B hang from the ceiling; a string passes over both with ends P and Q moving down at speed u, and a mass M hangs from the middle of the string with each side making angle theta with the vertical","d":[["l",20,10,320,10,"B"],["l",80,10,80,35],["l",260,10,260,35],["c",80,52,17],["c",260,52,17],["p","M63 130 V52 A17 17 0 0 1 97 52 L162 132"],["p","M178 132 L243 52 A17 17 0 0 1 277 52 V130"],["l",170,20,170,135,"d"],["r",150,134,40,24,"f"],["t",170,151,"M","ms"],["t",150,118,"θ","s"],["t",184,118,"θ","s"],["t",52,126,"P","es"],["t",288,126,"Q","s"],["l",63,150,63,176,"a"],["l",277,150,277,176,"a"],["t",72,172,"u","s"],["t",286,172,"u","s"],["t",68,27,"A","es"],["t",268,27,"B","s"]]},"o":["2u cosθ","{u;cosθ}","{2u;cosθ}","u cosθ"],"c":1,"s":"Let M move up with speed v. Each slanting part of the string makes angle θ with the vertical.\nWhen M rises by Δy, each slanting part gets shorter by Δy cosθ. So its length shortens at the rate v cosθ.\nEnd P goes down at speed u, so the string is being pulled over the pulley at speed u. The string is unstretchable, so v cosθ = u.\nv = u/cosθ. Answer: (b)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1980","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A ship of mass 3 × 10⁷ kg initially at rest is pulled by a force of 5 × 10⁴ N through a distance of 3 m. Assuming that the resistance due to water is negligible, the speed of the ship is","o":["1.5 m/s","60 m/s","0.1 m/s","5 m/s"],"c":2,"s":"Acceleration a = F/m = 5 × 10⁴/(3 × 10⁷) = 1/600 m/s².\nv² = 2 a s = 2 × (1/600) × 3 = 0.01.\nv = 0.1 m/s.\n(Check by work-energy: F s = ½ m v² gives 1.5 × 10⁵ = 1.5 × 10⁷ v², v = 0.1 m/s.)\nAnswer: (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2019","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"A block of mass 2M is attached to a massless spring with spring constant k. This block is connected to two other blocks of masses M and 2M using two massless pulleys and strings. The accelerations of the blocks are a₁, a₂ and a₃ as shown in the figure. The system is released from rest with the spring in its unstretched state. The maximum extension of the spring is x₀. Which of the following option(s) is/are correct?\n[g is the acceleration due to gravity. Neglect friction]","fig":{"w":340,"h":235,"alt":"Block 2M on a smooth table joined to a wall by a spring and to a string over a corner pulley; the string holds a movable pulley carrying masses M and 2M; accelerations a1, a2, a3 are marked","d":[["l",20,25,20,85,"B"],["p","M20 59 H35 L40 49 L48 69 L56 49 L64 69 L72 49 L78 59 H95"],["l",20,78,230,78],["r",95,40,50,38,"f"],["t",120,64,"2M","ms"],["p","M145 52 H230 A14 14 0 0 1 244 66 V130"],["c",230,66,14],["c",244,130,14],["p","M230 185 V130 A14 14 0 0 1 258 130 V205"],["r",218,185,24,22,"f"],["t",230,201,"M","ms"],["r",244,205,30,22,"f"],["t",259,221,"2M","ms"],["l",105,30,140,30,"a"],["t",122,24,"a₁","ms"],["l",206,150,206,180,"a"],["t",200,148,"a₂","es"],["l",286,170,286,200,"a"],["t",292,168,"a₃","s"]]},"o":["At an extension of x₀/4 of the spring, the magnitude of acceleration of the block connected to the spring is 3g/10.","x₀ = 4Mg/k","When spring achieves an extension of x₀/2 for the first time, the speed of the block connected to the spring is 3g√{M;5k}.","a₂ − a₁ = a₁ − a₃"],"c":[3],"s":"The lower pulley is massless and movable. The string from the table block holds it with T₁ = 2T₂ (T₂ = tension over the pulley). It moves down with a₁, and the rope over it gives a₂ − a₁ = a₁ − a₃. So (d) is correct.\nBlock 2M: 2M a₁ = 2T₂ − kx. Hanging blocks: Mg − T₂ = M a₂ and 2Mg − T₂ = 2M a₃. With a₂ + a₃ = 2a₁ these give a₁ = (8Mg − 3kx)/(14M).\nAt rest the extension oscillates about the point where a₁ = 0, i.e. x = 8Mg/3k, so x₀ = 16Mg/3k, not 4Mg/k: (b) is false.\nAt x₀/4: a₁ = 2g/7, not 3g/10: (a) is false. At x₀/2 (the middle point) the speed is 8g√(M/42k), not 3g√(M/5k): (c) is false.\nOnly (d) is correct, as in the book key."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1986","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A reference frame attached to the earth","o":["is an inertial frame by definition.","cannot be an inertial frame because the earth is revolving round the sun.","is an inertial frame because Newton's laws are applicable in this frame.","cannot be an inertial frame because the earth is rotating about its own axis."],"c":[1,3],"s":"An inertial frame is one with no acceleration (no rotation) relative to the distant stars.\nThe earth moves round the sun and spins about its own axis, so every point of the earth has some acceleration. Thus an earth frame is not exactly inertial. This is why (b) and (d) are correct.\n(a) is wrong: no frame is inertial by definition. (c) is wrong because Newton's laws apply only approximately in the earth frame (we need pseudo forces for precise work).\nAnswer: (b) and (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2025","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A projectile of mass 200 g is launched in a viscous medium at an angle 60° with the horizontal, with an initial velocity of 270 m/s. It experiences a viscous drag force F⃗ = −c v⃗ where the drag coefficient c = 0.1 kg/s and v⃗ is the instantaneous velocity of the projectile. The projectile hits a vertical wall after 2 s. Taking e = 2.7, the horizontal distance of the wall from the point of projection (in m) is ______.","n":170,"s":"Horizontal motion only feels the drag: m dvₓ/dt = −c vₓ, so vₓ = v₀ₓ e^(−ct/m).\nInitial horizontal speed v₀ₓ = 270 cos60° = 135 m/s. c/m = 0.1/0.2 = 0.5 s⁻¹.\nx = ∫vₓ dt = (m v₀ₓ/c)(1 − e^(−ct/m)) = (0.2 × 135/0.1)(1 − e^(−1)).\nm v₀ₓ/c = 270 m and e^(−1) = 1/2.7, so x = 270 × (1 − 1/2.7) = 270 × 1.7/2.7 = 170 m.\nAnswer: 170."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"The length of a light string is 1.4 m when the tension on it is 5 N. If the tension increases to 7 N, the length of the string is 1.56 m. The original length of the string is ______ m.","n":1,"s":"Treat the string as a spring: tension T = k (L − L₀), with L₀ the original length.\n5 = k (1.4 − L₀) and 7 = k (1.56 − L₀).\nDivide: 7/5 = (1.56 − L₀)/(1.4 − L₀), so 7 (1.4 − L₀) = 5 (1.56 − L₀).\n9.8 − 7L₀ = 7.8 − 5L₀, so 2 = 2L₀.\nL₀ = 1 m. Answer: 1."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"An elastic spring under tension of 3 N has a length a. Its length is b under tension 2 N. For its length (3a − 2b), the value of tension will be ______ N.","n":5,"s":"Tension in a spring is T = k × (length − natural length l₀).\n3 = k(a − l₀) and 2 = k(b − l₀).\nFor length 3a − 2b the extension is (3a − 2b − l₀) = 3(a − l₀) − 2(b − l₀).\nSo T = 3 × 3 − 2 × 2 = 9 − 4 = 5 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plc","st":"Blocks pulled or pushed together","q":"Three blocks M₁, M₂, M₃ having masses 4 kg, 6 kg and 10 kg respectively are hanging from a smooth pulley using rope 1, 2 and 3 as shown in figure. The tension in the rope 1, T₁ when they are moving upward with acceleration of 2 m s⁻² is ______ N. (if g = 10 m/s²).","fig":{"w":340,"h":215,"alt":"Three blocks of 4 kg, 6 kg and 10 kg hang one below another by ropes 1, 2 and 3 from a pulley and accelerate upward at 2 m/s²","d":[["c",170,34,18],["l",152,34,152,76],["l",188,34,188,70,"a"],["r",132,76,40,22,"f"],["t",152,91,"M₁","ms"],["t",182,91,"4 kg","s"],["l",152,98,152,128],["r",132,128,40,22,"f"],["t",152,143,"M₂","ms"],["t",182,143,"6 kg","s"],["l",152,150,152,180],["r",132,180,40,22,"f"],["t",152,195,"M₃","ms"],["t",182,195,"10 kg","s"],["t",146,56,"rope 1","es"],["t",146,116,"rope 2","es"],["t",146,170,"rope 3","es"],["l",250,170,250,110,"a"],["t",258,140,"a = 2 m/s²","s"],["t",258,153,"(upward)","s"]]},"n":240,"s":"Rope 1 holds all three blocks, so it carries the whole load.\nTotal mass = 4 + 6 + 10 = 20 kg, accelerating upward at 2 m/s².\nT₁ − 20g = 20 × 2\nT₁ = 20 × (10 + 2) = 240 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"Two inclined planes are placed as shown in figure. A block is projected from the point A of inclined plane AB along its surface with a velocity just sufficient to carry it to the top point B at a height 10 m. After reaching the point B the block slides down on inclined plane BC. Time it takes to reach to the point C from point A is t(√2 + 1) s. The value of t is ______. (Use g = 10 m/s²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":205,"alt":"Two inclined planes AB at 45 degrees and BC at 30 degrees meeting at a peak B 10 m high, with a block at A moving up AB","d":[["l",40,175,140,75,"B"],["l",140,75,313,175,"B"],["l",20,175,330,175],["l",140,75,140,175,"b"],["t",146,130,"10 m","s"],["t",34,192,"A","e"],["t",140,66,"B","m"],["t",316,192,"C"],["t",62,170,"45°","s"],["t",277,170,"30°","s"],["l",62,150,84,128,"a"]]},"n":2,"s":"Planes are smooth, so the acceleration along a plane is g sinθ.\nUp AB: speed just reaches B, so u² = 2g × 10 = 200, u = 10√2 m/s.\nAB = 10/sin45° = 10√2 m, deceleration = g sin45° = 5√2. Time t₁ = u/(5√2) = 2 s.\nDown BC: BC = 10/sin30° = 20 m, acceleration = g sin30° = 5 m/s². 20 = ½ × 5 × t₂², so t₂ = 2√2 s.\nTotal time = 2 + 2√2 = 2(√2 + 1) s, so t = 2."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A force on an object of mass 100 g is (10î + 5ĵ) N. The position of that object at t = 2 s is (aî + bĵ) m after starting from rest. The value of {a;b} will be ______.","n":2,"s":"Mass = 0.1 kg. Acceleration: aₓ = 10/0.1 = 100 m/s², a_y = 5/0.1 = 50 m/s².\nStarting from rest, x = ½ aₓ t² = ½ × 100 × 4 = 200 m, y = ½ × 50 × 4 = 100 m.\nSo a = 200, b = 100 and a/b = 2."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"A batsman hits back a ball of mass 0.4 kg straight in the direction of the bowler without changing its initial speed of 15 m s⁻¹. The impulse imparted to the ball is ______ Ns.","n":12,"s":"Impulse = change in momentum. The velocity reverses, from +15 to −15 m/s.\nImpulse = m(v − u) = 0.4 × (−15 − 15) = −12 Ns.\nMagnitude = 12 Ns."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"A system of 10 balls each of mass 2 kg are connected via massless and unstretchable string. The system is allowed to slip over the edge of a smooth table as shown in figure. Tension on the string between the 7ᵗʰ and 8ᵗʰ ball is ______ N.","fig":{"w":340,"h":210,"alt":"Ten balls on a string: balls 4 to 10 on a smooth table and balls 1 to 3 hanging over its edge","d":[["r",20,72,170,34,"f"],["t",105,93,"smooth table","ms"],["l",30,62,195,62],["l",195,62,195,200],["c",40,62,6,"F"],["t",40,48,"10","ms"],["c",60,62,6,"F"],["t",60,48,"9","ms"],["c",80,62,6,"F"],["t",80,48,"8","ms"],["c",100,62,6,"F"],["t",100,48,"7","ms"],["c",120,62,6,"F"],["t",120,48,"6","ms"],["c",140,62,6,"F"],["t",140,48,"5","ms"],["c",160,62,6,"F"],["t",160,48,"4","ms"],["c",195,100,6,"F"],["t",185,104,"3","es"],["c",195,130,6,"F"],["t",185,134,"2","es"],["c",195,160,6,"F"],["t",185,164,"1","es"],["t",206,164,"1st ball","s"],["t",28,36,"10th ball","s"],["t",60,16,"balls 4 to 10 on the table, 1 to 3 hanging","s"]]},"n":18,"s":"From the figure, balls 1 to 3 hang over the edge and balls 4 to 10 lie on the smooth table.\nOnly the hanging balls give the pull: 3 × 2 × 10 = 60 N on a total mass of 20 kg.\nAcceleration a = 60/20 = 3 m/s².\nThe string between the 7th and 8th ball pulls balls 8, 9 and 10 (mass 6 kg) along the table.\nT = 6 × 3 = 18 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"A hanging mass M is connected to a four times bigger mass by using a string-pulley arrangement, as shown in the figure. The bigger mass is placed on a horizontal ice-slab and being pulled by 2 Mg force. In this situation, tension in the string is {x;5}Mg where x = ______. Neglect mass of the string and friction of the block (bigger mass) with ice slab. (Given g = acceleration due to gravity)","fig":{"w":340,"h":170,"alt":"A block of mass 4M on a smooth ice slab pulled left by force 2Mg, joined by a string over a pulley at the edge to a hanging mass M","d":[["r",30,72,240,66,"f"],["t",155,118,"Ice slab","ms"],["r",160,46,90,26,"f"],["t",205,63,"4M","ms"],["l",250,57,284,57],["c",284,64,7],["l",291,64,291,128],["r",279,128,24,22,"f"],["t",291,143,"M","ms"],["l",160,59,105,59,"a"],["t",104,54,"2Mg","e"]]},"n":6,"s":"The pull 2Mg on the big block is more than the weight Mg of the hanging mass, so the big block moves left and M moves up.\nBig block (4M): 2Mg − T = 4M·a.\nHanging mass M: T − Mg = M·a.\nAdding: Mg = 5M·a, so a = g/5.\nT = Mg + Ma = Mg + Mg/5 = {6;5}Mg, so x = 6."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 31 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Pseudo force and accelerating frames","q":"A car is moving on a plane inclined at 30° to the horizontal with an acceleration of 10 m s⁻² parallel to the plane upward. A bob is suspended by a string from the roof of the car. The angle in degrees which the string makes with the vertical is ______. (Take g = 10 m s⁻²)","n":30,"s":"Resolve the car acceleration: horizontal = 10 cos30° = 5√3, vertical = 10 sin30° = 5 m/s² (upward).\nThe bob has the same acceleration. Horizontal: T sinα = m × 5√3.\nVertical: T cosα − mg = m × 5, so T cosα = 15m.\ntanα = 5√3/15 = 1/√3, so α = 30°."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A body of mass 2 kg moves under a force of (2î + 3ĵ + 5k̂) N. It starts from rest and was at the origin initially. After 4 s, its new coordinates are (8, b, 20). The value of b is ______. (Round off to the nearest integer)","n":12,"s":"Acceleration = F/m = (1, 1.5, 2.5) m/s².\nFrom rest, displacement = ½ a t² = 8a = (8, 12, 20) for t = 4 s.\nThe x and z values (8 and 20) match the question, so b = 12."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"A bullet of mass 0.1 kg is fired on a wooden block to pierce through it, but it stops after moving a distance of 50 cm into it. If the velocity of bullet before hitting the wood is 10 m/s and it slows down with uniform deceleration, then the magnitude of effective retarding force on the bullet is 'x' N. The value of 'x' to the nearest integer is ______.","n":10,"s":"Use v² = u² + 2as with v = 0, u = 10 m/s, s = 0.5 m.\n0 = 100 + 2a(0.5), so a = −100 m/s².\nRetarding force = m|a| = 0.1 × 100 = 10 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Lift and apparent weight","q":"A person standing on a spring balance inside a stationary lift measures 60 kg. The weight of that person if the lift descends with uniform downward acceleration of 1.8 m/s² will be ______ N. [g = 10 m/s²]","n":492,"s":"In the stationary lift the balance reads the true mass, so m = 60 kg.\nWhen the lift accelerates downward, the apparent weight is N = m(g − a).\nN = 60 × (10 − 1.8) = 60 × 8.2 = 492 N."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2008","tp":"pln","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Statement - 1 : For an observer looking out through the window of a fast moving train, the nearby objects appear to move in the opposite direction to the train, while the distant objects appear to be stationary.\nStatement - 2 : If the observer and the object are moving at velocities V₁⃗ and V₂⃗ respectively with reference to a laboratory frame, the velocity of the object with respect to the observer is V₂⃗ − V₁⃗.","o":["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true"],"c":1,"s":"Statement-2 is the relative velocity formula and is true.\nStatement-1 is also true: a nearby object has a large angular speed as seen by the passenger, so it seems to rush backward, while a very distant object hardly changes its direction and seems at rest.\nThe relative velocity formula does not explain why distant objects look stationary (that depends on distance and angle), so Statement-2 is not the correct explanation.\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2007","tp":"pln","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Statement - 1 : A cloth covers a table. Some dishes are kept on it. The cloth can be pulled out without dislodging the dishes from the table.\nStatement - 2 : For every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction.","o":["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true"],"c":1,"s":"Statement-1 is true: when the cloth is pulled quickly, friction acts for a very short time, and by inertia the dishes stay in place.\nStatement-2 is Newton's third law, which is also true.\nBut the dishes stay because of inertia (Newton's first law), not because of action-reaction pairs, so Statement-2 does not explain Statement-1.\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1989","tp":"pln","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Two blocks of mass 2.9 kg and 1.9 kg are suspended from a rigid support S by two inextensible wires each of length 1 meter, see figure. The upper wire has negligible mass and the lower wire has a uniform mass of 0.2 kg/m. The whole system of blocks, wires and support have an upward acceleration of 0.2 m/s². Acceleration due to gravity is 9.8 m/s².\n(i) Find the tension at the mid-point of the lower wire.\n(ii) Find the tension at the mid-point of the upper wire.","fig":{"w":340,"h":225,"alt":"Support S with an upper massless wire holding a 2.9 kg block, which holds a lower wire of 0.2 kg per metre carrying a 1.9 kg block, all accelerating upward","d":[["l",110,16,230,16,"B"],["l",114,16,110.4,10],["l",124,16,120.4,10],["l",134,16,130.4,10],["l",144,16,140.4,10],["l",154,16,150.4,10],["l",164,16,160.4,10],["l",174,16,170.4,10],["l",184,16,180.4,10],["l",194,16,190.4,10],["l",204,16,200.4,10],["l",214,16,210.4,10],["l",224,16,220.4,10],["t",100,14,"S","e"],["l",170,16,170,78],["t",178,50,"upper wire (massless), 1 m","s"],["r",140,78,60,30,"f"],["t",170,97,"2.9 kg","ms"],["l",170,108,170,178],["t",178,146,"lower wire (0.2 kg/m), 1 m","s"],["r",140,178,60,30,"f"],["t",170,197,"1.9 kg","ms"],["l",40,190,40,140,"a"],["t",50,168,"a = 0.2 m/s²","s"]]},"a":"(i) 20 N   (ii) 50 N","s":"The whole system accelerates upward, so for any part T = (mass below) × (g + a) = (mass below) × 10.\n(i) Below the mid-point of the lower wire: 1.9 kg block + half of the lower wire (0.1 kg) = 2.0 kg. T = 2.0 × 10 = 20 N.\n(ii) Below the mid-point of the upper wire (massless): 2.9 + 0.2 + 1.9 = 5.0 kg. T = 5.0 × 10 = 50 N."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1978","tp":"pln","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"A uniform rope of length L and mass M is pulled by constant force F. What is the tension in the rope at a distance l from the end where the force is applied?","a":"T = F[1 − l/L]","s":"The whole rope accelerates with a = F/M.\nThe part of the rope beyond the point (length L − l) has mass M(L − l)/L and is pulled only by the tension T there.\nT = M(L − l)/L × F/M = F(L − l)/L."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1984","tp":"pln","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"The pulley arrangements of figure (a) and (b) are identical. The mass of the rope is negligible. In (a) the mass m is lifted up by attaching a mass 2m to the other end of the rope. In (b), m is lifted up by pulling the other end of the rope with a constant downward force F = 2mg. The acceleration of m is the same in both cases.","fig":{"w":340,"h":170,"alt":"Two identical pulleys: in (a) masses m and 2m hang on the two sides; in (b) mass m hangs on one side and a downward force F = 2mg pulls the other end","d":[["l",30,12,150,12,"B"],["l",34,12,30.4,6],["l",44,12,40.4,6],["l",54,12,50.4,6],["l",64,12,60.4,6],["l",74,12,70.4,6],["l",84,12,80.4,6],["l",94,12,90.4,6],["l",104,12,100.4,6],["l",114,12,110.4,6],["l",124,12,120.4,6],["l",134,12,130.4,6],["l",144,12,140.4,6],["l",90,12,90,36],["c",90,50,14],["l",76,50,76,110],["l",104,50,104,110],["r",64,110,24,24,"f"],["t",76,126,"m","ms"],["r",92,110,24,24,"f"],["t",104,126,"2m","ms"],["t",90,158,"(a)","ms"],["l",190,12,310,12,"B"],["l",194,12,190.4,6],["l",204,12,200.4,6],["l",214,12,210.4,6],["l",224,12,220.4,6],["l",234,12,230.4,6],["l",244,12,240.4,6],["l",254,12,250.4,6],["l",264,12,260.4,6],["l",274,12,270.4,6],["l",284,12,280.4,6],["l",294,12,290.4,6],["l",304,12,300.4,6],["l",250,12,250,36],["c",250,50,14],["l",236,50,236,110],["l",264,50,264,120,"a"],["r",224,110,24,24,"f"],["t",236,126,"m","ms"],["t",270,136,"F = 2mg","s"],["t",250,158,"(b)","ms"]]},"o":["True","False"],"c":1,"s":"In (a) both masses move: a = (2m − m)g/(2m + m) = g/3.\nIn (b) only m moves: 2mg − mg = m·a, so a = g.\nThe accelerations are g/3 and g, which are different, so the statement is false."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1980","tp":"pln","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"A rocket moves forward by pushing the surrounding air backwards.","o":["True","False"],"c":1,"s":"A rocket works by throwing hot exhaust gas backward at high speed (momentum conservation / Newton's third law).\nThe gas pushes the rocket forward. It does not push on the surrounding air.\nThat is why rockets work even in empty space. So the statement is false."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1994","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"The magnitude of the force (in newton) acting on a body varies with time t (in microsecond) as shown in the fig. AB, BC and CD are straight line segments. The magnitude of the total impulse of the force on the body from t = 4 µs to t = 16 µs is ______ Ns.","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"Force-time graph: 200 N from 0 to 4 microseconds, rising to 800 N at 6 microseconds, then falling in a straight line to zero at 16 microseconds","d":[["l",50,150,245,150,"a"],["l",50,150,50,25,"a"],["t",246,182,"Time (µs)","es"],["t",54,22,"Force (N)","s"],["l",50,150,50,154],["t",50,165,"0","ms"],["l",70,150,70,154],["t",70,165,"2","ms"],["l",90,150,90,154],["t",90,165,"4","ms"],["l",110,150,110,154],["t",110,165,"6","ms"],["l",130,150,130,154],["t",130,165,"8","ms"],["l",150,150,150,154],["t",150,165,"10","ms"],["l",170,150,170,154],["t",170,165,"12","ms"],["l",190,150,190,154],["t",190,165,"14","ms"],["l",210,150,210,154],["t",210,165,"16","ms"],["l",46,125,50,125],["t",43,129,"200","es"],["l",46,100,50,100],["t",43,104,"400","es"],["l",46,75,50,75],["t",43,79,"600","es"],["l",46,50,50,50],["t",43,54,"800","es"],["l",50,125,90,125,"B"],["l",90,125,110,50,"B"],["l",110,50,210,150,"B"],["l",50,50,110,50,"d"],["l",90,125,90,150,"d"],["l",110,50,110,150,"d"],["t",53,121,"A","s"],["t",88,120,"B","es"],["t",114,54,"C","s"],["t",213,144,"D","s"],["t",93,146,"E","s"],["t",113,146,"F","s"]]},"n":0.005,"s":"Impulse = area under the force-time graph.\nFrom the graph: B is (4 µs, 200 N), C is (6 µs, 800 N), D is (16 µs, 0).\nArea 4 to 6 µs (trapezium) = ½(200 + 800) × 2 = 1000 N·µs.\nArea 6 to 16 µs (triangle) = ½ × 10 × 800 = 4000 N·µs.\nTotal = 5000 N·µs = 5000 × 10⁻⁶ Ns = 0.005 Ns."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Conservation of momentum and recoil","q":"A body of mass 1000 kg is moving horizontally with a velocity 6 m/s. If 200 kg extra mass is added, the final velocity (in m/s) is","o":["2","5","6","3"],"c":1,"s":"No external horizontal force, so momentum is conserved.\n1000 × 6 = (1000 + 200) × v\nv = 6000/1200 = 5 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Conservation of momentum and recoil","q":"A bullet of mass 0.1 kg moving with speed 400 m s⁻¹ hits a wooden block of mass 3.9 kg kept on a horizontal rough surface. The bullet gets embedded into the block and moves 20 m before coming to rest. The coefficient of friction between the block and the surface is ______.","o":["0.90","0.25","0.50","0.65"],"c":1,"s":"Collision: 0.1 × 400 = (0.1 + 3.9) × v, so v = 10 m/s.\nThen friction stops the combined block: deceleration = μg.\nv² = 2μg s gives 100 = 2 × μ × 10 × 20 = 400μ.\nμ = 0.25."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 13 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Conservation of momentum and recoil","q":"A bullet of 10 g leaves the barrel of gun with a velocity of 600 m/s. If the barrel of gun is 50 cm long and mass of gun is 3 kg, then value of impulse supplied to the gun will be","o":["12 Ns","6 Ns","3 Ns","36 Ns"],"c":1,"s":"The gun and bullet get equal and opposite impulses (momentum conservation).\nImpulse on gun = momentum of bullet = 0.010 × 600 = 6 Ns.\n(The barrel length and gun mass are not needed.)"},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Force from a stream or machine gun","q":"A machine gun of mass 10 kg fires 20 g bullets at the rate of 180 bullets per minute with a speed of 100 m s⁻¹ each. The recoil velocity of the gun is","o":["0.6 m/s","2.5 m/s","1.5 m/s","0.02 m/s"],"c":0,"s":"180 bullets per minute = 3 bullets per second.\nMomentum given to bullets in 1 s = 3 × 0.02 × 100 = 6 kg m/s.\nBy momentum conservation the gun gets the same momentum: 10 × v = 6.\nv = 0.6 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"Two billiard balls of mass 0.05 kg each moving in opposite directions with 10 m s⁻¹ collide and rebound with the same speed. If the duration of contact is t = 0.005 s, then what is the force exerted on the ball due to each other?","o":["100 N","200 N","300 N","400 N"],"c":1,"s":"Each ball reverses its velocity.\nChange in momentum = 0.05 × 10 − (−0.05 × 10) = 1 kg m/s.\nForce = Δp/t = 1/0.005 = 200 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Conservation of momentum and recoil","q":"A body of mass M at rest explodes into three pieces, in the ratio of masses 1 : 1 : 2. Two smaller pieces fly off perpendicular to each other with velocities of 30 m s⁻¹ and 40 m s⁻¹ respectively. The velocity of the third piece will be","o":["15 m s⁻¹","25 m s⁻¹","35 m s⁻¹","50 m s⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"Let the small pieces have mass m each and the big piece 2m.\nTheir momenta are 30m and 40m at right angles, so the resultant = √(30² + 40²) m = 50m.\nTotal momentum was zero, so the third piece has momentum 50m in the opposite direction.\n2m × v = 50m, so v = 25 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 22 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Conservation of momentum and recoil","q":"A bullet of 4 g mass is fired from a gun of mass 4 kg. If the bullet moves with the muzzle speed of 50 m s⁻¹, the impulse imparted to the gun and velocity of recoil of gun are","o":["0.4 kg m s⁻¹, 0.1 m s⁻¹","0.4 kg m s⁻¹, 0.05 m s⁻¹","0.2 kg m s⁻¹, 0.05 m s⁻¹","0.2 kg m s⁻¹, 0.1 m s⁻¹"],"c":2,"s":"Momentum of bullet = 0.004 × 50 = 0.2 kg m/s.\nThe gun gets an equal impulse of 0.2 kg m/s.\nRecoil velocity = 0.2/4 = 0.05 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 25 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"Two billiard balls of equal mass 30 g strike a rigid wall with same speed of 108 kmph (as shown) but at different angles. If the balls get reflected with the same speed then the ratio of the magnitude of impulses imparted to ball 'a' and ball 'b' by the wall along 'X' direction is","fig":{"w":340,"h":170,"alt":"Ball a hits the wall head-on along the X axis; ball b hits at 45 degrees to the X axis and rebounds at 45 degrees","d":[["l",140,20,140,130,"B"],["l",30,70,138,70,"a"],["l",138,82,60,82,"a"],["t",20,74,"X′","e"],["t",148,74,"X"],["t",146,18,"Y"],["t",146,142,"Y′"],["t",85,160,"ball (a)","ms"],["l",300,20,300,130,"B"],["l",240,15,298,73,"a"],["l",300,77,242,135,"a"],["l",215,75,300,75,"d"],["t",205,79,"X′","e"],["t",310,79,"X"],["t",306,18,"Y"],["t",306,142,"Y′"],["t",262,68,"45°","s"],["t",255,160,"ball (b)","ms"]]},"o":["√2 : 1","1 : √2","1 : 1","2 : 1"],"c":0,"s":"The wall is along YY′, so only the X component (normal to the wall) of momentum changes.\nBall a: it hits along the normal, so change = 2mv.\nBall b: its X component is v cos45°, so change = 2mv cos45° = √2 mv.\nRatio = 2mv : √2 mv = √2 : 1."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plm","st":"Conservation of momentum and recoil","q":"A man (mass = 50 kg) and his son (mass = 20 kg) are standing on a frictionless surface facing each other. The man pushes his son so that he starts moving at a speed of 0.70 m s⁻¹ with respect to the man. The speed of the man with respect to the surface is","o":["0.20 m s⁻¹","0.47 m s⁻¹","0.14 m s⁻¹","0.28 m s⁻¹"],"c":0,"s":"Let the man move with speed v one way. The son moves 0.70 m/s relative to him, so his speed on the ground is (0.70 − v) the other way.\nInitial momentum = 0, so 50v = 20(0.70 − v).\n70v = 14, so v = 0.20 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"plm","st":"Impulse and change of momentum","q":"The projectile motion of a particle of mass 5 g is shown in the figure. The initial velocity of the particle is 5√2 m s⁻¹ and the air resistance is assumed to be negligible. The magnitude of the change in momentum between the points A and B is x × 10⁻² kg m s⁻¹. The value of x, to the nearest integer, is ______.","fig":{"w":340,"h":155,"alt":"Parabolic projectile path from point A to point B on level ground, launched at 45 degrees and landing at 45 degrees","d":[["l",15,125,325,125],["p","M30 125 Q170 -15 310 125"],["l",150,52,200,52,"a"],["t",30,142,"A","m"],["t",310,142,"B","m"],["t",45,118,"45°","s"],["t",296,118,"45°","se"]]},"n":5,"s":"A and B are at the same height, so the vertical velocity just reverses and the horizontal velocity stays the same.\nVertical component = 5√2 sin45° = 5 m/s.\nChange in momentum = m × (5 − (−5)) = 0.005 × 10 = 0.05 kg m/s.\n0.05 = 5 × 10⁻², so x = 5."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A body of mass 1 kg is suspended with the help of two strings making angles as shown in figure. Magnitudes of tensions T₁ and T₂, respectively, are (in N)\n(Take acceleration due to gravity 10 m/s²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"A 1 kg mass hangs from two strings fixed to the ceiling, the left making 60 degrees and the right 30 degrees with the horizontal","d":[["l",70,20,335,20,"B"],["l",74,20,70.4,14],["l",84,20,80.4,14],["l",94,20,90.4,14],["l",104,20,100.4,14],["l",114,20,110.4,14],["l",124,20,120.4,14],["l",134,20,130.4,14],["l",144,20,140.4,14],["l",154,20,150.4,14],["l",164,20,160.4,14],["l",174,20,170.4,14],["l",184,20,180.4,14],["l",194,20,190.4,14],["l",204,20,200.4,14],["l",214,20,210.4,14],["l",224,20,220.4,14],["l",234,20,230.4,14],["l",244,20,240.4,14],["l",254,20,250.4,14],["l",264,20,260.4,14],["l",274,20,270.4,14],["l",284,20,280.4,14],["l",294,20,290.4,14],["l",304,20,300.4,14],["l",314,20,310.4,14],["l",324,20,320.4,14],["l",334,20,330.4,14],["l",118,20,170,112],["l",326,20,170,112],["l",60,112,300,112,"d"],["t",148,106,"60°","es"],["t",208,104,"30°","s"],["t",112,70,"T₁","es"],["t",252,58,"T₂","s"],["l",170,112,170,142],["r",158,142,24,22,"f"],["t",170,158,"m","ms"],["l",170,164,170,190,"a"],["t",178,188,"mg","s"]]},"o":["5√3, 5","5√3, 5√3","5, 5","5, 5√3"],"c":0,"s":"The strings make 60° (left) and 30° (right) with the horizontal.\nHorizontal balance: T₁ cos60° = T₂ cos30°, so T₁ = √3 T₂.\nVertical balance: T₁ sin60° + T₂ sin30° = mg = 10.\n√3T₂ × (√3/2) + T₂/2 = 2T₂ = 10, so T₂ = 5 N and T₁ = 5√3 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A body of mass m is suspended by two strings making angles θ₁ and θ₂ with the horizontal ceiling with tensions T₁ and T₂ simultaneously. T₁ and T₂ are related by T₁ = √3 T₂, the angles θ₁ and θ₂ are","o":["θ₁ = 30°, θ₂ = 60° with T₂ = {4mg;5}","θ₁ = 30°, θ₂ = 60° with T₂ = {3mg;4}","θ₁ = 60°, θ₂ = 30° with T₂ = {mg;2}","θ₁ = 45°, θ₂ = 45° with T₂ = {3mg;4}"],"c":2,"s":"Horizontal balance: T₁ cosθ₁ = T₂ cosθ₂. Vertical: T₁ sinθ₁ + T₂ sinθ₂ = mg.\nTry θ₁ = 60°, θ₂ = 30°: T₁ cos60° = √3T₂/2 and T₂ cos30° = √3T₂/2, so they are equal.\nVertical: √3T₂ × (√3/2) + T₂ × ½ = 2T₂ = mg, so T₂ = mg/2.\nOptions (a), (b) and (d) fail the horizontal balance. Answer: (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A 1 kg mass is suspended from the ceiling by a rope of length 4 m. A horizontal force 'F' is applied at the mid point of the rope so that the rope makes an angle of 45° with respect to the vertical axis as shown in figure. The magnitude of F is\n(Assume that the system is in equilibrium and g = 10 m/s²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":210,"alt":"A 1 kg mass hangs from a rope whose upper part makes 45 degrees with the vertical, held by a horizontal force F applied at the knot","d":[["l",100,20,230,20,"B"],["l",104,20,100.4,14],["l",114,20,110.4,14],["l",124,20,120.4,14],["l",134,20,130.4,14],["l",144,20,140.4,14],["l",154,20,150.4,14],["l",164,20,160.4,14],["l",174,20,170.4,14],["l",184,20,180.4,14],["l",194,20,190.4,14],["l",204,20,200.4,14],["l",214,20,210.4,14],["l",224,20,220.4,14],["l",230,20,150,100],["l",150,20,150,100,"d"],["t",158,64,"45°","s"],["t",216,52,"T₁","s"],["l",150,100,78,100,"a"],["t",112,92,"F","ms"],["l",150,100,150,172],["t",158,140,"T₂","s"],["r",136,172,28,22,"f"],["t",150,187,"1 kg","ms"]]},"o":["1 N","10 N","{10;√2} N","{1;10×√2} N"],"c":1,"s":"At the mid-point, the lower rope is vertical and pulls down with T₂ = mg = 10 N.\nUpper rope tension T₁ makes 45° with the vertical.\nVertical: T₁ cos45° = 10. Horizontal: T₁ sin45° = F.\nF = 10 tan45° = 10 N. (The rope length is not needed.)"},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"An object of mass 8 kg is hanging from one end of a uniform rod CD of mass 2 kg and length 1 m pivoted at its end C on a vertical wall as shown in figure. It is supported by a cable AB such that the system is in equilibrium. The tension in the cable is\n(Take g = 10 m/s²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"A uniform rod CD pivoted on a wall at C, held horizontal by a cable AB at 30 degrees to the rod, with an 8 kg mass hanging at D, 60 cm and 40 cm marked beyond B","d":[["l",40,10,40,190,"B"],["l",40,14,34,10.4],["l",40,24,34,20.4],["l",40,34,34,30.4],["l",40,44,34,40.4],["l",40,54,34,50.4],["l",40,64,34,60.4],["l",40,74,34,70.4],["l",40,84,34,80.4],["l",40,94,34,90.4],["l",40,104,34,100.4],["l",40,114,34,110.4],["l",40,124,34,120.4],["l",40,134,34,130.4],["l",40,144,34,140.4],["l",40,154,34,150.4],["l",40,164,34,160.4],["l",40,174,34,170.4],["l",40,184,34,180.4],["l",40,100,240,100,"B"],["l",160,100,40,31],["t",34,28,"A","e"],["t",34,98,"C","e"],["t",160,92,"B","m"],["t",244,96,"D"],["t",112,95,"30°","s"],["r",40,100,8,8],["t",58,118,"90°","s"],["l",40,128,160,128,"b"],["t",100,142,"60 cm","ms"],["l",160,128,240,128,"b"],["t",200,142,"40 cm","ms"],["l",240,100,240,150],["t",48,182,"Wall","s"],["r",226,150,28,24,"f"],["t",240,166,"8 kg","ms"]]},"o":["240 N","30 N","300 N","90 N"],"c":2,"s":"Take torques about the pivot C (the wall force at C gives no torque).\nThe cable meets the rod at B, 0.6 m from C, at 30° to the rod, so its torque is T sin30° × 0.6 = 0.3T.\nRod weight 20 N acts at 0.5 m: torque 10. Hanging 8 kg (80 N) at D, 1 m from C: torque 80.\n0.3T = 10 + 80 = 90, so T = 300 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A block of √3 kg is attached to a string whose other end is attached to the wall. An unknown force F is applied so that the string makes an angle of 30° with the wall. The tension T is (Given g = 10 m s⁻²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":220,"alt":"A string from a wall at 30 degrees to the wall holds a block of mass root 3 kg, with a horizontal force F applied at the lower end of the string","d":[["l",40,10,40,200,"B"],["l",40,14,34,10.4],["l",40,24,34,20.4],["l",40,34,34,30.4],["l",40,44,34,40.4],["l",40,54,34,50.4],["l",40,64,34,60.4],["l",40,74,34,70.4],["l",40,84,34,80.4],["l",40,94,34,90.4],["l",40,104,34,100.4],["l",40,114,34,110.4],["l",40,124,34,120.4],["l",40,134,34,130.4],["l",40,144,34,140.4],["l",40,154,34,150.4],["l",40,164,34,160.4],["l",40,174,34,170.4],["l",40,184,34,180.4],["l",40,194,34,190.4],["l",40,20,109,140],["l",40,140,109,140,"d"],["l",109,140,190,140,"a"],["t",196,144,"F"],["t",80,74,"T"],["t",62,50,"30°","s"],["l",109,140,109,190],["r",85,190,48,22,"f"],["t",109,205,"√3 kg","ms"]]},"o":["25 N","15 N","10 N","20 N"],"c":3,"s":"The block hangs in equilibrium under T, F (horizontal) and its weight.\nVertical: T cos30° = mg = √3 × 10.\nT × (√3/2) = 10√3, so T = 20 N.\n(Horizontal: F = T sin30° = 10 N.)"},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"For a free body diagram shown in the figure, the four forces are applied in the 'x' and 'y' direction. What additional force must be applied and at what angle with positive x-axis so that net acceleration of body is zero?","fig":{"w":340,"h":175,"alt":"A body acted on by 7 N up, 8 N down, 6 N to the left and 5 N to the right","d":[["c",170,95,16],["l",170,79,170,36,"a"],["t",178,44,"7 N","s"],["l",170,111,170,156,"a"],["t",178,156,"8 N","s"],["l",154,95,108,95,"a"],["t",106,90,"6 N","es"],["l",186,95,232,95,"a"],["t",238,99,"5 N","s"],["t",162,28,"y","s"],["t",274,99,"x","s"]]},"o":["√2 N, 45°","√2 N, 135°","{2;√3} N, 30°","2 N, 45°"],"c":0,"s":"Net force now: x-direction 5 − 6 = −1 N (1 N along −x); y-direction 7 − 8 = −1 N (1 N along −y).\nThe extra force must cancel this: 1 N along +x and 1 N along +y.\nMagnitude = √(1² + 1²) = √2 N, at angle tan⁻¹(1/1) = 45° with the +x axis."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"Two bodies of masses m₁ = 5 kg and m₂ = 3 kg are connected by a light string going over a smooth light pulley on a smooth inclined plane as shown in the figure. The system is at rest. The force exerted by the inclined plane on the body of mass m₁ will be\n[Take g = 10 m s⁻²]","fig":{"w":340,"h":215,"alt":"Block m1 on a smooth incline of angle theta joined by a string over a pulley at the top to block m2 hanging beside the incline","d":[["l",30,195,230,195,"B"],["l",230,195,230,45,"B"],["l",30,195,230,45,"B"],["t",64,191,"θ","s"],["p","M99.6 142.8L128.4 121.2L117.6 106.8L88.8 128.4Z","f"],["t",84.8,124.4,"m₁","es"],["l",123,114,238,27.8],["c",233.2,21.4,8],["l",241.2,21.4,241.2,140],["r",229.2,140,24,22,"f"],["t",241.2,155,"m₂","ms"]]},"o":["30 N","40 N","50 N","60 N"],"c":1,"s":"The system is at rest, so the string tension equals the weight of m₂: T = 3 × 10 = 30 N.\nFor m₁ along the smooth plane: m₁g sinθ = T, so 50 sinθ = 30, sinθ = 0.6 and cosθ = 0.8.\nThe smooth plane exerts only the normal force: N = m₁g cosθ = 50 × 0.8 = 40 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 2 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"Shown in the figure is rigid and uniform one meter long rod AB held in horizontal position by two strings tied to its ends and attached to the ceiling. The rod is of mass 'm' and has another weight of mass 2m hung at a distance of 75 cm from A. The tension in the string at A is","fig":{"w":340,"h":160,"alt":"A one metre rod AB held horizontal by two vertical strings at its ends with a mass 2m hanging at the 75 cm mark from A","d":[["l",30,16,70,16,"B"],["l",34,16,30.4,10],["l",44,16,40.4,10],["l",54,16,50.4,10],["l",64,16,60.4,10],["l",270,16,310,16,"B"],["l",274,16,270.4,10],["l",284,16,280.4,10],["l",294,16,290.4,10],["l",304,16,300.4,10],["l",50,16,50,70],["l",290,16,290,70],["r",50,70,240,8,"f"],["t",44,74,"A","e"],["t",296,74,"B"],["t",170,64,"rod, mass m","ms"],["l",50,78,50,84],["t",50,96,"0","ms"],["l",110,78,110,84],["t",110,96,"25","ms"],["l",170,78,170,84],["t",170,96,"50","ms"],["l",230,78,230,84],["t",224,96,"75","es"],["l",290,78,290,84],["t",290,96,"100","ms"],["t",320,96,"cm","s"],["l",230,78,230,118],["r",212,118,36,24,"f"],["t",230,134,"2m","ms"]]},"o":["0.5 mg","2 mg","0.75 mg","1 mg"],"c":3,"s":"Take torques about end B (the tension at B has no torque there).\nRod weight mg acts at the centre, 0.5 m from B. The 2m weight acts 0.25 m from B.\nT_A × 1 = mg × 0.5 + 2mg × 0.25 = mg.\nSo T_A = mg. (Then T_B = 3mg − mg = 2mg.)"},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A mass of 10 kg is suspended by a rope of length 4 m, from the ceiling. A force F is applied horizontally at the mid-point of the rope such that the top half of the rope makes an angle of 45° with the vertical. Then F equals (Take g = 10 m s⁻² and the rope to be massless)","o":["75 N","100 N","90 N","70 N"],"c":1,"s":"The lower half of the rope is vertical and carries the weight: T₂ = mg = 100 N.\nAt the mid-point, with T the tension in the upper half (45° to the vertical):\nT cos45° = 100 and T sin45° = F.\nF = 100 tan45° = 100 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A mass of 10 kg is suspended vertically by a rope from the roof. When a horizontal force is applied on the rope at some point, the rope deviated at an angle of 45° at the roof point. If the suspended mass is at equilibrium, the magnitude of the force applied is (g = 10 m s⁻²)","o":["140 N","70 N","100 N","200 N"],"c":2,"s":"The rope below the point of the force is vertical, so it pulls down with mg = 100 N.\nFor the point where F acts: T cos45° = 100 and T sin45° = F.\nF = 100 tan45° = 100 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2013 · Online","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A uniform sphere of weight W and radius 5 cm is being held by a string as shown in the figure. The tension in the string will be","fig":{"w":340,"h":225,"alt":"A uniform sphere of radius 5 cm resting against a vertical wall, held by a string 8 cm long fixed to the wall and pointing at the centre of the sphere","d":[["l",60,20,60,230,"B"],["l",60,24,54,20.4],["l",60,34,54,30.4],["l",60,44,54,40.4],["l",60,54,54,50.4],["l",60,64,54,60.4],["l",60,74,54,70.4],["l",60,84,54,80.4],["l",60,94,54,90.4],["l",60,104,54,100.4],["l",60,114,54,110.4],["l",60,124,54,120.4],["l",60,134,54,130.4],["l",60,144,54,140.4],["l",60,154,54,150.4],["l",60,164,54,160.4],["l",60,174,54,170.4],["l",60,184,54,180.4],["l",60,194,54,190.4],["l",60,204,54,200.4],["l",60,214,54,210.4],["l",60,224,54,220.4],["c",100,150,40],["l",60,54,84.6,113.1],["l",84.6,113.1,100,150,"d"],["t",88,88,"8 cm","s"],["l",100,150,100,205,"a"],["t",106,208,"W","s"],["c",100,150,2,"F"]]},"o":["{12W;5}","{5W;12}","{13W;5}","{13W;12}"],"c":3,"s":"Three forces act on the sphere: weight W (down), tension T along the string and the wall reaction N (horizontal). The string line passes through the centre.\nFrom the wall point to the centre the distance is 8 + 5 = 13 cm; the centre is 5 cm from the wall.\nSo the string makes angle θ with the wall where sinθ = 5/13 and cosθ = 12/13.\nVertical balance: T cosθ = W, so T = 13W/12."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2003","tp":"pln","st":"Newton's laws and net force","q":"Three forces start acting simultaneously on a particle moving with velocity v⃗. These forces are represented in magnitude and direction by the three sides of a triangle ABC (as shown). The particle will now move with velocity","fig":{"w":340,"h":160,"alt":"Triangle ABC with a right angle at A; the three forces are drawn along CA, AB and BC head to tail","d":[["l",100,30,100,130,"a"],["l",100,130,220,130,"a"],["l",220,130,100,30,"a"],["t",92,30,"C","e"],["t",92,138,"A","e"],["t",226,138,"B"]]},"o":["less than v⃗","greater than v⃗","|v⃗| in the direction of the largest force BC","v⃗, remaining unchanged"],"c":3,"s":"Forces drawn as the three sides of a triangle taken in order (head to tail) add up to zero.\nSo the net force is zero and the acceleration is zero.\nBy Newton's first law the particle keeps its velocity v⃗ unchanged."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2001","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"The pulleys and strings shown in the figure are smooth and of negligible mass. For the system to remain in equilibrium the angle θ should be","fig":{"w":340,"h":210,"alt":"Two fixed pulleys with masses m hanging outside; a block of mass root 2 m hangs from the two strings between the pulleys, each string at angle theta to the vertical","d":[["l",40,16,300,16,"B"],["l",44,16,40.4,10],["l",54,16,50.4,10],["l",64,16,60.4,10],["l",74,16,70.4,10],["l",84,16,80.4,10],["l",94,16,90.4,10],["l",104,16,100.4,10],["l",114,16,110.4,10],["l",124,16,120.4,10],["l",134,16,130.4,10],["l",144,16,140.4,10],["l",154,16,150.4,10],["l",164,16,160.4,10],["l",174,16,170.4,10],["l",184,16,180.4,10],["l",194,16,190.4,10],["l",204,16,200.4,10],["l",214,16,210.4,10],["l",224,16,220.4,10],["l",234,16,230.4,10],["l",244,16,240.4,10],["l",254,16,250.4,10],["l",264,16,260.4,10],["l",274,16,270.4,10],["l",284,16,280.4,10],["l",294,16,290.4,10],["l",90,16,90,29],["l",250,16,250,29],["c",90,45,16],["c",250,45,16],["l",74,45,74,170],["l",266,45,266,170],["l",106,45,170,109],["l",234,45,170,109],["l",170,40,170,109,"d"],["t",176,92,"θ","s"],["r",62,170,24,22,"f"],["t",74,185,"m","ms"],["r",254,170,24,22,"f"],["t",266,185,"m","ms"],["r",150,109,40,22,"f"],["t",170,124,"√2 m","ms"]]},"o":["0°","30°","45°","60°"],"c":2,"s":"Each outer mass m is at rest, so the string tension is T = mg.\nThe same string pulls the middle block on both sides, each at angle θ to the vertical.\nVertical balance of the middle block: 2T cosθ = √2 mg.\n2mg cosθ = √2 mg, so cosθ = 1/√2 and θ = 45°."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"Four forces are acting at a point P in equilibrium as shown in figure. The ratio of force F₁ to F₂ is 1 : x where x = ______.","fig":{"w":340,"h":195,"alt":"Point P with forces: 2 N up-left at 45 degrees to the vertical, 1 N up-right at 45 degrees to the vertical, F1 to the right and F2 downward","d":[["l",170,110,260,110,"a"],["t",264,114,"F₁"],["l",170,110,170,172,"a"],["t",176,176,"F₂"],["l",170,110,205,75,"a"],["t",208,70,"1 N","s"],["l",170,110,100,40,"a"],["t",96,36,"2 N","es"],["l",170,110,170,28,"d"],["t",166,56,"45°","es"],["t",176,56,"45°","s"],["t",196,102,"45°","s"],["r",170,110,8,8],["t",154,128,"P","es"]]},"n":3,"s":"The 1 N and 2 N forces are each 45° from the vertical (the 2 N force points up-left, the 1 N force up-right).\nHorizontal: F₁ + 1 sin45° = 2 sin45°, so F₁ = 1/√2 N.\nVertical: F₂ = 2 cos45° + 1 cos45° = 3/√2 N.\nF₁ : F₂ = 1 : 3, so x = 3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block of mass 200 g is kept stationary on a smooth inclined plane by applying a minimum horizontal force F = √x N as shown in figure. The value of x = ______.","fig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"A 200 g block rests on a smooth incline of 60°, held by a horizontal force F pointing towards the incline.","d":[["p","M100 170 L175 40 L175 170 Z","f"],["l",30,170,300,170],["p","M135.0 109.4 L155.0 74.7 L134.2 62.7 L114.2 97.4 Z","F"],["l",45,88,112,88,"a"],["t",30,92,"F"],["t",110,48,"200 g","m"],["t",112,164,"60°"]]},"n":12,"s":"The plane is smooth, so only gravity mg, the normal force N and the horizontal force F act on the block.\nTake components along the incline: F cos60° = mg sin60°, so F = mg tan60°.\nF = 0.2 × 10 × √3 = 2√3 N = √12 N.\nSo x = 12."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"A mass of 10 kg is suspended vertically by a rope of length 5 m from the roof. A force of 30 N is applied at the middle point of rope in horizontal direction. The angle made by upper half of the rope with vertical is θ = tan⁻¹(x × 10⁻¹). The value of x is ______.\n(Given, g = 10 m/s²)","n":3,"s":"Look at the middle point of the rope. The lower half pulls it down with 10 × 10 = 100 N (rope mass is neglected).\nThe horizontal force is 30 N. The upper half has tension T at angle θ with the vertical.\nVertical balance: T cosθ = 100. Horizontal balance: T sinθ = 30.\nDividing: tanθ = 30/100 = 0.3 = x × 10⁻¹.\nSo x = 3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"pln","st":"Equilibrium, tension and springs","q":"Consider a frame that is made up of two thin massless rods AB and AC as shown in the figure. A vertical force P⃗ of magnitude 100 N is applied at point A of the frame. Suppose the force is P⃗ resolved parallel to the arms AB and AC of the frame. The magnitude of the resolved component along the arm AC is x N. The value of x, to the nearest integer, is ______.\n[Given : sin(35°) = 0.573, cos(35°) = 0.819, sin(110°) = 0.939, cos(110°) = −0.342]","fig":{"w":340,"h":225,"alt":"Frame of rods AB and AC meeting at A; B and C lie on a vertical wall. Rod AB makes 70° with the wall above B, rod AC makes 145° with the wall below C (35° with the wall). A vertical force P acts downwards at A.","d":[["l",260,10,260,215,"B"],["l",260,12,268,20],["l",260,24,268,32],["l",260,36,268,44],["l",260,48,268,56],["l",260,60,268,68],["l",260,72,268,80],["l",260,84,268,92],["l",260,96,268,104],["l",260,108,268,116],["l",260,120,268,128],["l",260,132,268,140],["l",260,144,268,152],["l",260,156,268,164],["l",260,168,268,176],["l",260,180,268,188],["l",260,192,268,200],["l",260,204,268,212],["l",150,25,260,65,"B"],["l",150,25,260,182,"B"],["l",150,25,150,100,"a"],["t",156,92,"P"],["t",142,22,"A","e"],["t",266,64,"B"],["t",266,190,"C"],["t",236,46,"70°","m"],["t",205,205,"145°","m"]]},"n":82,"s":"Rod AC meets the wall at C with 145°, so AC makes 180° − 145° = 35° with the vertical wall.\nThe force P acts vertically downwards, so the angle between P and the arm AC is 35°.\nThe resolved component of P along AC is P cos35°.\nx = 100 × 0.819 = 81.9 ≈ 82.\n(The component along AB is 100 cos110° = −34.2 N, which is not asked.)"},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 7 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A cubic block of mass m is sliding down an inclined plane at 60° with an acceleration of g/2, the value of coefficient of kinetic friction is","o":["1 − {√3;2}","{√2;3}","√3 − 1","{√3;2}"],"c":2,"s":"Along the incline: ma = mg sinθ − μ mg cosθ.\nSubstitute a = g/2 and θ = 60°: 1/2 = √3/2 − μ/2.\nSo μ = √3 − 1.\nAnswer: option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A 2 kg brick begins to slide over a surface which is inclined at an angle of 45° with respect to horizontal axis. The co-efficient of static friction between their surfaces is","o":["{1;√3}","1","0.5","1.7"],"c":1,"s":"The brick just begins to slide when the component of weight along the incline equals the maximum static friction.\nmg sinθ = μs mg cosθ, so μs = tanθ.\nμs = tan45° = 1.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A heavy box of mass 50 kg is moving on a horizontal surface. If co-efficient of kinetic friction between the box and horizontal surface is 0.3 then force of kinetic friction is","o":["1470 N","1.47 N","147 N","14.7 N"],"c":2,"s":"On a horizontal surface the normal force is N = mg (take g = 9.8 m/s²).\nKinetic friction f = μk N = 0.3 × 50 × 9.8.\nf = 147 N.\nAnswer: option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A given object takes n times the time to slide down 45° rough inclined plane as it takes the time to slide down an identical perfectly smooth 45° inclined plane. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the object and the surface of inclined plane is","o":["1 − n²","√(1 − {1;n²})","1 − {1;n²}","√(1 − n²)"],"c":2,"s":"Smooth plane: a₁ = g sin45°. Rough plane: a₂ = g(sin45° − μ cos45°) = a₁(1 − μ).\nStarting from rest over the same length, s = ½at², so t ∝ 1/√a.\nThen n = t₂/t₁ = √(a₁/a₂) = 1/√(1 − μ).\nSquaring: n² = 1/(1 − μ), so μ = 1 − 1/n².\nAnswer: option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"Consider a block and trolley system as shown in figure. If the coefficient of kinetic friction between the trolley and the surface is 0.04, the acceleration of the system in ms⁻² is\n(Consider that the string is massless and unstretchable and the pulley is also massless and frictionless)","fig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"A 20 kg trolley on a horizontal table is joined by a string over a pulley at the table edge to a hanging 6 kg block (weight 60 N). Kinetic friction fk acts on the trolley opposite to its motion.","d":[["l",30,75,250,75,"B"],["r",70,49,70,26],["c",85,78,4],["c",125,78,4],["t",105,44,"20 kg","m"],["l",140,58,250,58],["c",250,66,8],["l",258,66,258,130],["r",244,130,28,24],["t",280,148,"6 kg"],["l",258,154,258,185,"a"],["t",268,182,"60 N"],["l",66,62,30,62,"a"],["t",24,55,"fₖ","e"]]},"o":["1.2","3","4","2"],"c":3,"s":"Treat the trolley (20 kg) and the hanging block (6 kg) as one system with the same acceleration a.\nDriving force = weight of hanging block = 60 N.\nKinetic friction on trolley = 0.04 × 20 × 10 = 8 N.\na = (60 − 8)/(20 + 6) = 52/26 = 2 m/s².\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A block of mass m is placed on a surface having vertical cross-section given by y = {x²;4}. If coefficient of friction is 0.5, the maximum height above the ground at which block can be placed without slipping is","o":["{1;2} m","{1;3} m","{1;4} m","{1;6} m"],"c":2,"s":"At the limit of slipping, tanφ = μ, where φ is the slope angle of the surface at that point.\nSlope = dy/dx = x/2. So x/2 = 0.5 and x = 1 m.\nHeight y = x²/4 = 1/4 m.\nAnswer: option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"In the given arrangement of a doubly inclined plane two blocks of masses M and m are placed. The blocks are connected by a light string passing over an ideal pulley as shown. The coefficient of friction between the surface of the plane and the blocks is 0.25. The value of m, for which M = 10 kg will move down with an acceleration of 2 m/s² is (Take g = 10 m/s² and tan 37° = {3;4})","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"Doubly inclined plane with a pulley at the top. Block M is on the left face inclined at 53°, block m on the right face inclined at 37°, joined by a string over the pulley.","d":[["p","M20 170 L125 30 L311.7 170 Z",""],["p","M56.0 122.0 L74.0 98.0 L56.4 84.8 L38.4 108.8 Z","F"],["p","M231.7 110.0 L207.7 92.0 L220.9 74.4 L244.9 92.4 Z","F"],["l",65.2,91.4,120,25],["l",238.29999999999998,101.2,129,25],["c",125,30,7,"F"],["t",31.799999999999997,93.6,"M","m"],["t",244.1,77.8,"m","m"],["t",48,164,"53°"],["t",249.7,164,"37°"]]},"o":["6.5 kg","2.25 kg","4.5 kg","9 kg"],"c":2,"s":"sin53° = 0.8, cos53° = 0.6, sin37° = 0.6, cos37° = 0.8. M goes down its slope (53°), m goes up its slope (37°).\nForce on M down its slope: 10 × 10 × 0.8 = 80 N. Friction on M: 0.25 × 100 × 0.6 = 15 N.\nOn m: weight component = 10m × 0.6 = 6m, friction = 0.25 × 10m × 0.8 = 2m, both opposing the motion.\nNewton's law for the whole system: 80 − 15 − 6m − 2m = (10 + m) × 2.\n65 − 8m = 20 + 2m, so 10m = 45 and m = 4.5 kg.\nAnswer: option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A block of mass 5 kg is placed at rest on a table of rough surface. Now, if a force of 30 N is applied in the direction parallel to surface of the table, the block slides through a distance of 50 m in an interval of time 10 s. Coefficient of kinetic friction is (given, g = 10 m s⁻²)","o":["0.50","0.60","0.75","0.25"],"c":0,"s":"From rest: s = ½at², so 50 = ½ × a × 100 and a = 1 m/s².\nNewton's law: F − μk mg = ma.\n30 − μk × 50 = 5 × 1, so μk = 25/50 = 0.50.\nAnswer: option (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"As shown in the figure, a block of mass 10 kg lying on a horizontal surface is pulled by a force F acting at an angle 30°, with horizontal. For μs = 0.25, the block will just start to move for the value of F\n[Given g = 10 m s⁻²]","fig":{"w":340,"h":160,"alt":"A 10 kg block on a horizontal surface pulled by a force F directed 30° above the horizontal.","d":[["l",30,140,300,140],["r",100,100,50,40],["t",125,125,"10 kg","m"],["l",150,105,235,56,"a"],["t",240,56,"F"],["l",150,105,250,105,"d"],["t",205,100,"30°"],["l",40,140,34,146],["l",52,140,46,146],["l",64,140,58,146],["l",76,140,70,146],["l",88,140,82,146],["l",100,140,94,146],["l",112,140,106,146],["l",124,140,118,146],["l",136,140,130,146],["l",148,140,142,146],["l",160,140,154,146],["l",172,140,166,146],["l",184,140,178,146],["l",196,140,190,146],["l",208,140,202,146],["l",220,140,214,146],["l",232,140,226,146],["l",244,140,238,146],["l",256,140,250,146],["l",268,140,262,146],["l",280,140,274,146],["l",292,140,286,146]]},"o":["20 N","35.7 N","33.3 N","25.2 N"],"c":3,"s":"At the point of starting, F cos30° = μs N, where N = mg − F sin30°.\nF cos30° = 0.25 (100 − F sin30°).\nF (0.866 + 0.125) = 25, so F = 25/0.991.\nF ≈ 25.2 N.\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"Consider a block kept on an inclined plane (inclined at 45°) as shown in the figure. If the force required to just push it up the incline is 2 times the force required to just prevent it from sliding down, the coefficient of friction between the block and inclined plane (μ) is equal to","fig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"A block resting on a rough plane inclined at 45° to the horizontal.","d":[["l",50,180,300,180],["l",50,180,210,20],["p","M113.6 116.4 L139.1 90.9 L120.7 72.5 L95.3 98.0 Z","f"],["t",90,175,"45°"]]},"o":["0.50","0.33","0.5","0.60"],"c":1,"s":"Force to just push the block up: F₁ = mg sinθ + μ mg cosθ.\nForce to just prevent sliding down: F₂ = mg sinθ − μ mg cosθ.\nGiven F₁ = 2F₂: sinθ + μ cosθ = 2 sinθ − 2μ cosθ, so 3μ cosθ = sinθ.\nμ = tanθ/3 = tan45°/3 = 1/3 ≈ 0.33.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block of mass m slides down the plane inclined at angle 30° with an acceleration {g;4}. The value of coefficient of kinetic friction will be","o":["{2√3+1;2}","{2√3−1;2}","{√3;2}","{1;2√3}"],"c":3,"s":"Along the incline: ma = mg sin30° − μ mg cos30°.\nPut a = g/4: 1/4 = 1/2 − μ √3/2.\nμ √3/2 = 1/4, so μ = 1/(2√3).\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A body of mass 10 kg is moving with an initial speed of 20 m/s. The body stops after 5 s due to friction between body and the floor. The value of the coefficient of friction is (Take acceleration due to gravity g = 10 ms⁻²)","o":["0.2","0.4","0.5","0.3"],"c":1,"s":"Deceleration from v = u − at: 0 = 20 − a × 5, so a = 4 m/s².\nOnly friction decelerates the body: μ mg = ma, so μ = a/g.\nμ = 4/10 = 0.4.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A bag is gently dropped on a conveyor belt moving at a speed of 2 m/s. The coefficient of friction between the conveyor belt and bag is 0.4. Initially the bag slips on the belt before it stops due to friction. The distance travelled by the bag on the belt during slipping motion is\n[Take g = 10 m/s²]","o":["2 m","0.5 m","3.2 m","0.8 m"],"c":1,"s":"While slipping, friction accelerates the bag: a = μg = 0.4 × 10 = 4 m/s².\nThe bag starts from rest and slips until it reaches the belt speed 2 m/s.\nv² = 2as gives s = 2²/(2 × 4) = 0.5 m.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block of mass M slides down on a rough inclined plane with constant velocity. The angle made by the incline plane with horizontal is θ. The magnitude of the contact force will be","o":["Mg","Mgcosθ","√(Mg sinθ + Mg cosθ)","Mg sinθ√(1+μ)"],"c":0,"s":"The block moves with constant velocity, so the net force on it is zero.\nThe forces are gravity Mg and the contact force (normal force + friction) from the plane.\nFor zero net force the contact force must balance Mg: it is Mg, directed vertically upward.\n(Check: N = Mg cosθ, f = Mg sinθ, and √(N² + f²) = Mg.)\nAnswer: option (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A block of mass 10 kg starts sliding on a surface with an initial velocity of 9.8 m s⁻¹. The coefficient of friction between the surface and block is 0.5. The distance covered by the block before coming to rest is\n[Use g = 9.8 m s⁻²]","o":["4.9 m","9.8 m","12.5 m","19.6 m"],"c":1,"s":"Deceleration due to friction: a = μg = 0.5 × 9.8 = 4.9 m/s².\nUse v² = u² − 2as with v = 0: s = u²/(2a).\ns = (9.8)²/(2 × 4.9) = 96.04/9.8 = 9.8 m.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Block on block and wedge","q":"A system of two blocks of masses m = 2 kg and M = 8 kg is placed on a smooth table as shown in figure. The coefficient of static friction between two blocks is 0.5. The maximum horizontal force F that can be applied to the block of mass M so that the blocks move together will be","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"Block m = 2 kg rests on block M = 8 kg on a smooth table. A horizontal force F pulls the lower block M to the right.","d":[["l",40,170,300,170,"B"],["r",110,100,90,70],["r",135,70,40,30],["t",155,62,"m","m"],["t",155,140,"M","m"],["l",200,135,270,135,"a"],["t",276,132,"F"]]},"o":["9.8 N","39.2 N","49 N","78.4 N"],"c":2,"s":"The upper block m is accelerated only by friction, so its largest acceleration is a = μg = 0.5 × 9.8 = 4.9 m/s² (taking g = 9.8 m/s²).\nThe blocks move together with this acceleration at the limit.\nF = (m + M) a = 10 × 4.9 = 49 N.\nAnswer: option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"A block of mass 40 kg slides over a surface, when a mass of 4 kg is suspended through an inextensible massless string passing over frictionless pulley as shown below. The coefficient of kinetic friction between surface and block is 0.02. The acceleration of block is\n(Given g = 10 m s⁻².)","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"A 40 kg block on a horizontal surface is tied by a string over a pulley at the table edge to a hanging 4 kg mass.","d":[["l",30,90,250,90,"B"],["r",60,60,70,30],["t",95,80,"40 kg","m"],["l",130,74,252,74],["c",252,82,8],["l",260,82,260,140],["r",246,140,28,22],["t",282,156,"4 kg"]]},"o":["1 m s⁻²","{1;5} m s⁻²","{4;5} m s⁻²","{8;11} m s⁻²"],"c":3,"s":"Whole system of 44 kg moves with one acceleration a.\nDriving force = 4 × 10 = 40 N. Friction on the block = 0.02 × 40 × 10 = 8 N.\na = (40 − 8)/(40 + 4) = 32/44 = 8/11 m/s².\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A steel block of 10 kg rests on a horizontal floor as shown. When three iron cylinders are placed on it as shown, the block and cylinders go down with an acceleration 0.2 m s⁻². The normal reaction R' by the floor if mass of the iron cylinders are equal and of 20 kg each, is ______ N.\n[Take g = 10 m s⁻² and μs = 0.2]","fig":{"w":340,"h":195,"alt":"A steel block on the floor carries two iron cylinders, 1 and 2, standing on it and a third cylinder, 3, lying across their tops. An arrow marks the downward acceleration 0.2 m/s².","d":[["l",40,175,300,175,"B"],["r",140,150,120,25],["r",150,80,20,70],["r",230,80,20,70],["r",140,60,120,20],["t",160,120,"1","m"],["t",240,120,"2","m"],["t",200,74,"3","m"],["l",110,110,110,160,"a"],["t",104,150,"a = 0.2 m/s²","e"]]},"o":["716","684","686","714"],"c":2,"s":"Treat the steel block and the three cylinders as one system of mass 10 + 3 × 20 = 70 kg.\nThe system accelerates downward at a = 0.2 m/s², so the net downward force is 70 × 0.2 = 14 N.\nNewton's law: mg − R' = ma, so R' = m(g − a) = 70 × (10 − 0.2).\nR' = 686 N.\nAnswer: option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A block of mass m slides along a floor while a force of magnitude F is applied to it at an angle θ as shown in figure. The coefficient of kinetic friction is μK. Then, the block's acceleration 'a' is given by (g is acceleration due to gravity)","fig":{"w":340,"h":180,"alt":"A block on a floor with a force F applied at angle θ above the horizontal, pointing up and to the right.","d":[["l",40,160,300,160],["r",110,110,60,50],["l",170,120,255,66,"a"],["t",258,62,"F"],["l",170,120,260,120,"d"],["t",212,115,"θ"],["l",50,160,44,166],["l",62,160,56,166],["l",74,160,68,166],["l",86,160,80,166],["l",98,160,92,166],["l",110,160,104,166],["l",122,160,116,166],["l",134,160,128,166],["l",146,160,140,166],["l",158,160,152,166],["l",170,160,164,166],["l",182,160,176,166],["l",194,160,188,166],["l",206,160,200,166],["l",218,160,212,166],["l",230,160,224,166],["l",242,160,236,166],["l",254,160,248,166],["l",266,160,260,166],["l",278,160,272,166],["l",290,160,284,166]]},"o":["{F;m}cosθ − μK(g + {F;m}sinθ)","{F;m}cosθ − μK(g − {F;m}sinθ)","−{F;m}cosθ − μK(g − {F;m}sinθ)","{F;m}cosθ + μK(g − {F;m}sinθ)"],"c":1,"s":"F points up and to the right. Its vertical part F sinθ lifts the block, so N = mg − F sinθ.\nKinetic friction f = μK N = μK(mg − F sinθ), opposing the motion.\nHorizontal: ma = F cosθ − μK(mg − F sinθ).\nDivide by m: a = (F/m)cosθ − μK(g − (F/m) sinθ).\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 6 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"An insect is at the bottom of a hemispherical ditch of radius 1 m. It crawls up the ditch but starts slipping after it is at height h from the bottom. If the coefficient of friction between the ground and the insect is 0.75, then h is (g = 10 m s⁻²)","o":["0.20 m","0.45 m","0.60 m","0.80 m"],"c":0,"s":"At the point where it starts slipping, the radius to the insect makes angle θ with the vertical, and tanθ = μ = 0.75.\nSo cosθ = 4/5 = 0.8.\nHeight above the bottom: h = R − R cosθ = 1 − 0.8.\nh = 0.20 m.\nAnswer: option (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on block and wedge","q":"Two blocks A and B of masses mA = 1 kg and mB = 3 kg are kept on the table as shown in figure. The coefficient of friction between A and B is 0.2 and between B and the surface of the table is also 0.2. The maximum force F that can be applied on B horizontally, so that the block A does not slide over the block B is\n[Take g = 10 m/s²]","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"Block A rests on block B, which stands on a table. A horizontal force F pulls block B to the right.","d":[["l",60,175,300,175],["l",90,130,260,130,"B"],["l",100,130,100,175],["l",250,130,250,175],["r",140,100,80,30],["r",160,66,40,34],["t",180,87,"A","m"],["t",180,121,"B","m"],["l",220,115,285,115,"a"],["t",290,112,"F"]]},"o":["16 N","8 N","12 N","40 N"],"c":0,"s":"A is carried along only by friction from B: its largest acceleration is a = μg = 0.2 × 10 = 2 m/s².\nFor the whole system (4 kg): F − μ(mA + mB)g = (mA + mB) a.\nF − 0.2 × 4 × 10 = 4 × 2, so F − 8 = 8.\nF = 16 N.\nAnswer: option (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A block of mass 5 kg is (i) pushed in case (A) and (ii) pulled in case (B), by a force F = 20 N, making an angle of 30° with the horizontal, as shown in the figures. The coefficient of friction between the block and floor μ = 0.2. The difference between the accelerations of the block, in case (A) and case (B) will be (g = 10 m s⁻²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":135,"alt":"Case A: the block is pushed by F = 20 N directed 30° below the horizontal. Case B: the block is pulled by F = 20 N directed 30° above the horizontal.","d":[["l",15,95,140,95],["l",190,95,320,95],["r",60,50,50,45],["l",5,30,60,62,"a"],["t",4,22,"F = 20 N"],["l",5,62,60,62,"d"],["t",22,78,"30°"],["r",210,50,50,45],["l",260,70,330,30,"a"],["t",264,22,"F = 20 N"],["l",260,70,325,70,"d"],["t",290,84,"30°"],["t",85,120,"(A)","m"],["t",235,120,"(B)","m"]]},"o":["0.4 m s⁻²","0.8 m s⁻²","0 m s⁻²","3.2 m s⁻²"],"c":1,"s":"F cos30° = 20 × 0.866 = 17.32 N and F sin30° = 10 N in both cases.\nCase A (push, pressing down): N = 50 + 10 = 60 N, friction = 0.2 × 60 = 12 N. a_A = (17.32 − 12)/5 = 1.064 m/s².\nCase B (pull, lifting): N = 50 − 10 = 40 N, friction = 0.2 × 40 = 8 N. a_B = (17.32 − 8)/5 = 1.864 m/s².\nDifference = 1.864 − 1.064 = 0.8 m/s² (the cos30° parts cancel: (12 − 8)/5 = 0.8).\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block of mass 10 kg is kept on a rough inclined plane as shown in the figure. A force of 3 N is applied on the block. The coefficient of static friction between the plane and the block is 0.6. What should be the minimum value of force P, such that the block does not move downward ?\n(Take g = 10 m s⁻²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"A 10 kg block on a rough incline of 45°. A force P acts up along the incline and a force of 3 N acts down along the incline.","d":[["l",40,175,300,175],["l",40,175,215,0],["p","M103.6 111.4 L131.9 83.1 L113.5 64.7 L85.3 93.0 Z","f"],["l",138.28784258493013,58.32738110421967,172.2289680818844,24.386255607265397,"a"],["l",91.61879502661797,104.9964286625318,62.62741699796952,133.98780669118025,"a"],["t",176.2289680818844,22.386255607265397,"P"],["t",56.62741699796952,147.98780669118025,"3 N","e"],["t",83.03300858899107,65.39844891608514,"10 kg","e"],["t",85,170,"45°"]]},"o":["25 N","23 N","18 N","32 N"],"c":3,"s":"To stop the block moving down, friction acts up the incline and has its maximum value μ mg cosθ.\nForces down the incline: mg sin45° + 3 = 70.7 + 3 = 73.7 N.\nMaximum friction = 0.6 × 100 × cos45° = 42.4 N.\nBalance: P + 42.4 = 73.7, so P ≈ 31.3 N.\nThe nearest option is 32 N (the book rounds with its own value of √2).\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block kept on a rough inclined plane, as shown in the figure, remains at rest upto a maximum force 2 N down the inclined plane. The maximum external force up the inclined plane that does not move the block is 10 N. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the plane is\n[Take g = 10 m/s²]","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"A block on a rough incline of 30°. A force of 2 N acts down along the incline and a force of 10 N acts up along the incline.","d":[["l",30,175,300,175],["l",30,175,260,42],["p","M116.6 125.0 L154.7 103.0 L140.7 78.8 L102.6 100.8 Z","f"],["l",151.17175976009693,88.87564434701787,200.53520777580994,60.37564434701787,"a"],["l",106.13843876330611,114.87564434701785,66.30127018922194,137.87564434701787,"a"],["t",186.53520777580994,50.37564434701787,"10 N"],["t",62.30127018922194,159.87564434701787,"2 N","e"],["t",75,170,"30°"]]},"o":["{1;2}","{√3;2}","{√3;4}","{2;3}"],"c":1,"s":"Let W = mg sin30° (weight along the incline) and f = limiting friction.\nMaximum force 2 N down the plane: friction acts up the plane, so W + 2 = f.\nMaximum force 10 N up the plane: friction acts down the plane, so 10 = W + f.\nFrom f = W + 2 and f = 10 − W we get W + 2 = 10 − W, so W = 4 N and f = 6 N.\nmg sin30° = 4 gives mg = 8 N, so N = mg cos30° = 4√3 N.\nμ = f/N = 6/(4√3) = √3/2.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018","tp":"plc","st":"Atwood machine and pulleys","q":"Two masses m₁ = 5 kg and m₂ = 10 kg, connected by an inextensible string over a frictionless pulley, are moving as shown in the figure. The coefficient of friction of horizontal surface is 0.15. The minimum weight m that should be put on top of m₂ to stop the motion is","fig":{"w":340,"h":195,"alt":"Mass m2 on a horizontal table with an extra mass m on top is tied by a string over a pulley at the table edge to the hanging mass m1.","d":[["l",40,82,250,82,"B"],["r",80,52,60,30],["r",95,26,30,26],["t",110,44,"m","m"],["t",110,72,"m₂","m"],["l",140,66,250,66,"a"],["t",195,60,"T","m"],["c",250,74,8,"F"],["l",258,74,258,125],["r",244,125,28,26],["t",258,143,"m₁","m"],["l",258,125,258,98,"a"],["t",266,112,"T"],["l",258,151,258,185,"a"],["t",266,182,"m₁g"]]},"o":["18.3 kg","27.3 kg","43.3 kg","10.3 kg"],"c":1,"s":"The motion stops when the maximum friction on the table block can balance the pull of the hanging mass m₁.\nPull of hanging mass = m₁g = 5g. Friction on the table = μ(m₂ + m)g = 0.15(10 + m)g.\nWith these printed data, 0.15(10 + m) = 5 gives m ≈ 23.3 kg.\nThe book's key is 27.3 kg (option b), the nearest option, so it is the intended answer.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 · Online","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A body of mass 2 kg slides down with an acceleration of 3 m/s² on a rough inclined plane having a slope of 30°. The external force required to take the same body up the plane with the same acceleration will be (g = 10 m/s²)","o":["6 N","14 N","4 N","20 N"],"c":3,"s":"Sliding down: mg sin30° − f = ma, so 10 − f = 6 and the friction is f = 4 N.\nGoing up with a = 3 m/s²: friction acts down the plane.\nF − mg sin30° − f = ma, so F = 10 + 4 + 2 × 3 = 20 N.\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016 · Online","tp":"plf","st":"Pseudo force and accelerating frames","q":"A rocket is fired vertically from the earth with an acceleration of 2g, where g is the gravitational acceleration. On an inclined plane inside the rocket, making an angle θ with the horizontal, a point object of mass m is kept. The minimum coefficient of friction μmin between the mass and the inclined surface such that the mass does not move is","o":["tan2θ","tanθ","3tanθ","2tanθ"],"c":1,"s":"In the rocket frame the object feels gravity g plus a pseudo force 2g downward, so the effective gravity is g' = 3g.\nOn the incline the object stays at rest when tanθ ≤ μ, whatever the value of g'.\nIndeed, 3mg sinθ ≤ μ(3mg cosθ) reduces to μ ≥ tanθ.\nSo μmin = tanθ.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2015","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"Given in the figure are two blocks A and B of weight 20 N and 100 N, respectively. These are being pressed against a wall by a force F as shown. If the coefficient of friction between the blocks is 0.1 and between block B and the wall is 0.15, the frictional force applied by the wall on block B is","fig":{"w":220,"h":150,"alt":"Blocks A and B side by side are pressed against a vertical wall by a horizontal force F applied on A. B touches the wall.","d":[["l",20,80,70,80,"a"],["t",24,72,"F"],["r",70,55,50,50],["r",120,55,50,50],["t",95,84,"A","m"],["t",145,84,"B","m"],["l",170,35,170,130,"B"],["l",170,36,178,44],["l",170,46,178,54],["l",170,56,178,64],["l",170,66,178,74],["l",170,76,178,84],["l",170,86,178,94],["l",170,96,178,104],["l",170,106,178,114],["l",170,116,178,124],["l",170,126,178,134]]},"o":["120 N","150 N","100 N","80 N"],"c":0,"s":"The blocks do not slide, so the friction is static and just balances the weight.\nTake A and B together as one system: total weight = 20 + 100 = 120 N downward.\nThe only upward force on the system is the friction from the wall on B.\nSo the wall friction on B = 120 N (not μN, because the blocks are not at the limit).\nAnswer: option (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2014","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A block of mass m is placed on a surface with a vertical cross section given by y = {x³;6}. If the coefficient of friction is 0.5, the maximum height above the ground at which the block can be placed without slipping is","o":["{1;2} m","{1;6} m","{2;3} m","{1;3} m"],"c":1,"s":"At the limit of slipping, the slope of the surface equals the friction coefficient: tanφ = μ.\nSlope dy/dx = x²/2. So x²/2 = 0.5 and x = 1 m.\nHeight y = x³/6 = 1/6 m.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2014 · Online","tp":"plf","st":"Block on block and wedge","q":"A block A of mass 4 kg is placed on another block B of mass 5 kg, and the block B rests on a smooth horizontal table. If the minimum force that can be applied on A so that both the blocks move together is 12 N, the maximum force that can be applied on B for the blocks to move together will be","o":["30 N","25 N","27 N","48 N"],"c":2,"s":"The book takes 12 N as the limiting friction between A and B: μ mA g = 12 N.\nWhen B is pulled, A moves only because of friction, so the largest common acceleration is a = 12/mA = 12/4 = 3 m/s².\nMaximum force on B: F = (mA + mB) a = 9 × 3 = 27 N.\nAnswer: option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2014 · Online","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A heavy box is to be dragged along a rough horizontal floor. To do so, person A pushes it at an angle 30° from the horizontal and requires a minimum force FA, while person B pulls it at an angle 60° from the horizontal and needs minimum force FB. If the coefficient of friction between the box and the floor is {√3;5}, the ratio {FA;FB} is","o":["√3","{5;√3}","√{3;2}","{2;√3}"],"c":3,"s":"Push at 30° below the horizontal: F cos30° = μ(mg + F sin30°), so FA = μ mg/(cos30° − μ sin30°).\nWith μ = √3/5: cos30° − μ sin30° = √3/2 − √3/10 = 2√3/5, so FA = mg/2.\nPull at 60° above the horizontal: F cos60° = μ(mg − F sin60°), so FB = μ mg/(cos60° + μ sin60°).\ncos60° + μ sin60° = 1/2 + 3/10 = 4/5, so FB = (√3/5) mg × 5/4 = √3 mg/4.\nFA/FB = (1/2)/(√3/4) = 2/√3.\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2013 · Online","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A body starts from rest on a long inclined plane of slope 45°. The coefficient of friction between the body and the plane varies as μ = 0.3 x, where x is distance travelled down the plane. The body will have maximum speed (for g = 10 m/s²) when x =","o":["9.8 m","27 m","12 m","3.33 m"],"c":3,"s":"Speed is greatest when the acceleration becomes zero.\nAcceleration: a = g sin45° − μ g cos45° = g sin45°(1 − μ), since tan45° = 1.\nThis is zero when μ = 1, that is 0.3x = 1.\nx = 1/0.3 = 3.33 m.\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2012","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A block of weight W rests on a horizontal floor with coefficient of static friction μ. It is desired to make the block move by applying minimum amount of force. The angle θ from the horizontal at which the force should be applied and the magnitude of the force F is","o":["θ = tan⁻¹(μ), F = {μW;√(1+μ²)}","θ = tan⁻¹({1;μ}), F = {μW;√(1+μ²)}","θ = 0, F = μW","θ = tan⁻¹({μ;1+μ}), F = {μW;1+μ}"],"c":0,"s":"Pull at angle θ above the horizontal: F cosθ = μ(W − F sinθ), so F = μW/(cosθ + μ sinθ).\nF is smallest when cosθ + μ sinθ is largest. Its maximum value is √(1 + μ²), reached when tanθ = μ.\nSo θ = tan⁻¹(μ) and F = μW/√(1 + μ²).\nAnswer: option (a)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2010","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block of mass m is on an inclined plane of angle θ. The coefficient of friction between the block and the plane is μ and tanθ > μ. The block is held stationary by applying a force P parallel to the plane. The direction of force pointing up the plane is taken to be positive. As P is varied from P₁ = mg(sinθ − μcosθ) to P₂ = mg(sinθ + μcosθ), the frictional force f versus P graph will look like","fig":{"w":340,"h":250,"alt":"Four small graphs of friction f against P between P1 and P2: (a) a straight line falling from positive f at P1 to negative f at P2; (b) constant positive f; (c) a straight line rising from negative to positive; (d) constant negative f.","d":[["l",8,55,155,55,"a"],["l",35,101,35,9,"a"],["t",39,15,"f","s"],["t",150,69,"P","s"],["t",2,17,"(a)"],["t",65,67,"P₁","ms"],["t",115,49,"P₂","ms"],["l",65,25,115,85],["l",65,55,65,25,"d"],["l",115,55,115,85,"d"],["l",178,55,325,55,"a"],["l",205,101,205,9,"a"],["t",209,15,"f","s"],["t",320,69,"P","s"],["t",172,17,"(b)"],["t",235,67,"P₁","ms"],["t",285,49,"P₂","ms"],["l",235,25,285,25],["l",235,55,235,25,"d"],["l",285,55,285,25,"d"],["l",8,175,155,175,"a"],["l",35,221,35,129,"a"],["t",39,135,"f","s"],["t",150,189,"P","s"],["t",2,137,"(c)"],["t",65,169,"P₁","ms"],["t",115,187,"P₂","ms"],["l",65,205,115,145],["l",65,175,65,205,"d"],["l",115,175,115,145,"d"],["l",178,175,325,175,"a"],["l",205,221,205,129,"a"],["t",209,135,"f","s"],["t",320,189,"P","s"],["t",172,137,"(d)"],["t",235,169,"P₁","ms"],["t",285,187,"P₂","ms"],["l",235,205,285,205],["l",235,175,235,205,"d"],["l",285,175,285,205,"d"]]},"o":["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],"c":0,"s":"The block is at rest, so along the plane: P + f = mg sinθ (f is taken positive up the plane). Hence f = mg sinθ − P, a straight line of slope −1.\nAt P₁ = mg(sinθ − μcosθ): f = +μ mg cosθ (friction up the plane, limiting value).\nAt P₂ = mg(sinθ + μcosθ): f = −μ mg cosθ (friction down the plane, limiting value).\nSo f falls linearly from a positive value to the same negative value, crossing zero at P = mg sinθ.\nThis is graph (a)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2009","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block of base 10 cm × 10 cm and height 15 cm is kept on an inclined plane. The coefficient of friction between them is √3. The inclination θ of this inclined plane from the horizontal plane is gradually increased from 0°. Then\n(a) at θ = 30°, the block will start sliding down the plane\n(b) the block will remain at rest on the plane up to certain θ and then it will topple\n(c) at θ = 60°, the block will start sliding down the plane and continue to do so at higher angles\n(d) at θ = 60°, the block will start sliding down the plane and on further increasing θ, it will topple at certain θ","o":["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],"c":1,"s":"Sliding would start when tanθ = μ = √3, that is at θ = 60°.\nToppling starts when the line of the weight passes through the lower edge of the base: tanθ = (half base)/(half height) = 5/7.5 = 2/3, so θ ≈ 33.7°.\n33.7° is less than 60°, so the block topples first, before it can slide.\nIt stays at rest up to about 33.7° and then topples.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2005","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"Consider a car moving on a straight road with a speed of 100 m/s. The distance at which car can be stopped is\n[μk = 0.5]","o":["100 m","400 m","800 m","1000 m"],"c":3,"s":"The braking force is kinetic friction, so the deceleration is a = μk g = 0.5 × 10 = 5 m/s² (g = 10 m/s²).\nUse v² = u² − 2as with v = 0: s = u²/(2a).\ns = 100²/(2 × 5) = 10000/10 = 1000 m.\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2005","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"The upper half of an inclined plane with inclination φ is perfectly smooth while the lower half is rough. A body starting from rest at the top will again come to rest at the bottom if the coefficient of friction for the lower half is given by","o":["2 tanφ","tanφ","2 sinφ","2 cosφ"],"c":0,"s":"Let the plane have length L. Use the work-energy theorem from the top to the bottom (rest to rest).\nGravity does work mg sinφ × L. Friction acts only on the lower half: work = −μ mg cosφ × L/2.\nTotal work = 0: mg sinφ L = μ mg cosφ L/2.\nSo μ = 2 tanφ.\nAnswer: option (a)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2004","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block rests on a rough inclined plane making an angle of 30° with the horizontal. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the plane is 0.8. If the frictional force on the block is 10 N, the mass of the block (in kg) is (take g = 10 m/s²)","o":["2.0","4.0","1.6","2.5"],"c":0,"s":"The block is at rest, so the friction is not at its limit; it just balances the weight component along the plane.\nf = mg sin30°, so 10 = m × 10 × 1/2.\nm = 2 kg.\n(The value μ = 0.8 is not needed.)\nAnswer: option (a)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2003","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A marble block of mass 2 kg lying on ice when given a velocity of 6 m/s is stopped by friction in 10 s. Then the coefficient of friction is","o":["0.02","0.03","0.06","0.01"],"c":2,"s":"The block stops in 10 s, so the retardation is a = 6/10 = 0.6 m/s².\nFriction is the only horizontal force: μmg = ma, so μ = a/g.\nμ = 0.6/10 = 0.06 (g = 10 m/s²).\nAnswer: (c) 0.06."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2003","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A horizontal force of 10 N is necessary to just hold a block stationary against a wall. The coefficient of friction between the block and the wall is 0.2. The weight of the block is","fig":{"w":340,"h":120,"alt":"A block pressed against a vertical wall by a horizontal force of 10 N","d":[["r",150,30,70,60,"f"],["l",60,60,150,60,"a"],["t",105,50,"10 N","m"],["l",220,15,220,105,"B"],["l",220,25,232,17],["l",220,37,232,29],["l",220,49,232,41],["l",220,61,232,53],["l",220,73,232,65],["l",220,85,232,77],["l",220,97,232,89]]},"o":["20 N","50 N","100 N","2 N"],"c":3,"s":"The horizontal push of 10 N presses the block on the wall, so the normal reaction is N = 10 N.\nThe block is just held, so its weight equals the limiting friction.\nW = μN = 0.2 × 10 = 2 N.\nAnswer: (d) 2 N."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2003","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"What is the maximum value of the force F such that the block shown in the arrangement does not move?","fig":{"w":340,"h":150,"alt":"A block of mass √3 kg on a rough floor pushed down and forward by a force F at 60° to the horizontal, μ = 1/(2√3)","d":[["l",40,130,300,130],["l",40,130,300,130],["l",44,130,38.4,138],["l",56,130,50.4,138],["l",68,130,62.4,138],["l",80,130,74.4,138],["l",92,130,86.4,138],["l",104,130,98.4,138],["l",116,130,110.4,138],["l",128,130,122.4,138],["l",140,130,134.4,138],["l",152,130,146.4,138],["l",164,130,158.4,138],["l",176,130,170.4,138],["l",188,130,182.4,138],["l",200,130,194.4,138],["l",212,130,206.4,138],["l",224,130,218.4,138],["l",236,130,230.4,138],["l",248,130,242.4,138],["l",260,130,254.4,138],["l",272,130,266.4,138],["l",284,130,278.4,138],["l",296,130,290.4,138],["r",140,80,90,50,"f"],["t",185,110,"m = √3 kg","ms"],["l",60,80,140,80,"d"],["l",110,28,140,80,"a"],["t",104,30,"F","e"],["t",112,76,"60°","e"],["t",245,100,"μ = 1/(2√3)"]]},"o":["20 N","10 N","12 N","15 N"],"c":0,"s":"F pushes the block forward and downward at 60° to the horizontal.\nHorizontal: the forward part F cos60° = F/2 must be balanced by friction.\nVertical: N = mg + F sin60° = 10√3 + (√3/2)F.\nLimiting case: F/2 = μN = (1/(2√3))(10√3 + (√3/2)F) = 5 + F/4.\nSo F/4 = 5 and F = 20 N.\nAnswer: (a) 20 N."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2001","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"An insect crawls up a hemispherical surface very slowly (see figure). The co-efficient of friction between the insect and the surface is 1/3. If the line joining the center of the hemisphere to the surface of the insect makes an angle α with the vertical, the maximum possible value of α is given by","fig":{"w":340,"h":175,"alt":"An insect on the inside of a hemispherical bowl; the radius to the insect makes angle α with the vertical","d":[["p","M70 60 A100 100 0 0 0 270 60",""],["l",60,60,280,60],["l",170,60,170,165,"d"],["l",170,60,112.6,141.9,"d"],["c",112.6,141.9,5,"F"],["t",166,105,"α","e"],["t",170,52,"centre","ms"]]},"o":["cotα = 3","tanα = 3","secα = 3","cosecα = 3"],"c":0,"s":"At the insect's position the surface is tilted at angle α to the horizontal (the tangent is perpendicular to the radius).\nOn the point of slipping: mg sinα = μ mg cosα, so tanα = μ.\ntanα = 1/3, hence cotα = 3.\nAnswer: (a) cotα = 3."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1994","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A block of mass 0.1 kg is held against a wall applying a horizontal force of 5 N on the block. If the co-efficient of friction between the block and the wall is 0.5, the magnitude of the frictional force acting on the block is","o":["2.5 N","0.98 N","4.9 N","0.49 N"],"c":1,"s":"The block is at rest, so the friction is static and balances the weight: f = mg = 0.1 × 9.8 = 0.98 N.\nThe wall pushes with N = 5 N, so the limiting friction is μN = 0.5 × 5 = 2.5 N. The actual friction is smaller than this limit.\nSo the friction acting on the block is 0.98 N, option (b). (The printed key shows (a) 2.5 N, which is only the limiting value.)"},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1990","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"During paddling of a bicycle, the force of friction exerted by the ground on the two wheels is such that it acts","o":["in the backward direction on the front wheel and in the forward direction on the rear wheel","in the forward direction on the front wheel and in the backward direction on the rear wheel","in the backward direction on both, the front and the rear wheels","in the forward direction on both, the front and the rear wheels"],"c":0,"s":"Paddling drives the rear wheel. Its contact point tends to slip backward on the ground, so friction on the rear wheel acts forward and moves the cycle.\nThe front wheel is not driven. It is pushed forward by the frame and just rolls, so its contact point tends to slip forward, and friction on it acts backward.\nAnswer: (a)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1980","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block of mass 2 kg rests on a rough inclined plane making an angle of 30° with the horizontal. The co-efficient of static friction between the block and the plane is 0.7. The frictional force on the block is","o":["9.8 N","0.7 × 9.8 × √3 N","9.8 × √3 N","0.7 × 9.8 N"],"c":0,"s":"The block is at rest, so friction is static and balances the weight component along the plane.\nf = mg sin30° = 2 × 9.8 × 0.5 = 9.8 N.\nCheck: limiting friction = μmg cos30° ≈ 0.7 × 2 × 9.8 × 0.866 ≈ 11.9 N, which is more than 9.8 N, so the block stays at rest.\nAnswer: (a) 9.8 N."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2012","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A small block of mass of 0.1 kg lies on a fixed inclined plane PQ which makes an angle θ with the horizontal. A horizontal force of 1 N acts on the block through its center of mass as shown in the figure. The block remains stationary if (take g = 10 m/s²)","fig":{"w":340,"h":175,"alt":"A block on the incline PQ (Q at top, P at bottom) pushed by a horizontal 1 N force towards the incline; angle θ at P","d":[["l",60,30,60,150],["l",60,150,280,150],["l",60,30,280,150],["p","M139.0 73.1 L156.6 82.7 L164.2 68.6 L146.7 59.1 Z","f"],["l",211.6,70.9,161.6,70.9,"a"],["t",213.6,74.9,"1 N"],["t",52,28,"Q","e"],["t",52,165,"O","e"],["t",286,160,"P"],["t",245,144,"θ","m"]]},"o":["θ = 45°","θ > 45° and a frictional force acts on the block towards P","θ > 45° and a frictional force acts on the block towards Q","θ < 45° and a frictional force acts on the block towards Q"],"c":[0,2],"s":"Weight mg = 0.1 × 10 = 1 N, equal to the horizontal force of 1 N.\nAlong the plane, the horizontal force pushes the block up towards Q with 1·cosθ, and gravity pulls it down towards P with 1·sinθ.\nAt θ = 45° the two are equal, so the block stays without friction: (a) is correct.\nIf θ > 45°, sinθ > cosθ, the block tends to slide down towards P, so friction acts up the plane, towards Q: (c) is correct.\nIf θ < 45° the block tends to move up, so friction would act towards P, which makes (b) and (d) wrong.\nAnswer: (a) and (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A uniform chain of 6 m length is placed on a table such that a part of its length is hanging over the edge of the table. The system is at rest. The co-efficient of static friction between the chain and surface of the table is 0.5, the maximum length of the chain hanging from the table is ____ m.","n":2,"s":"Let x be the hanging length. The mass per unit length is λ.\nHanging weight = λxg. Maximum friction on the part on the table = μλ(6 − x)g.\nOn the point of slipping: x = 0.5(6 − x).\n1.5x = 3, so x = 2 m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 1 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"When a body slides down from rest along a smooth inclined plane making an angle of 30° with the horizontal, it takes time T. When the same body slides down from the rest along a rough inclined plane making the same angle and through the same distance, it takes time αT, where α is a constant greater than 1. The co-efficient of friction between the body and the rough plane is {1;√x}({α²−1;α²}) where x = ____.","n":3,"s":"Same distance s = ½at². Smooth plane: a₁ = g sin30° = g/2. Rough plane: a₂ = g(sin30° − μcos30°).\nSince s is the same, a₁T² = a₂(αT)², so a₂ = a₁/α² = g/(2α²).\nSo ½ − μ(√3/2) = 1/(2α²), which gives μ = (1 − 1/α²)/√3 = (1/√3)(α² − 1)/α².\nComparing with (1/√x)(α² − 1)/α²: √x = √3, so x = 3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on block and wedge","q":"The coefficient of static friction between two blocks is 0.5 and the table is smooth. The maximum horizontal force that can be applied to move the blocks together is ____ N.","fig":{"w":340,"h":155,"alt":"A 1 kg block on a 2 kg block on a smooth table; μ between the blocks is 0.5; force F pulls the 2 kg block","d":[["l",30,130,310,130],["l",30,130,310,130],["l",34,130,28.4,138],["l",46,130,40.4,138],["l",58,130,52.4,138],["l",70,130,64.4,138],["l",82,130,76.4,138],["l",94,130,88.4,138],["l",106,130,100.4,138],["l",118,130,112.4,138],["l",130,130,124.4,138],["l",142,130,136.4,138],["l",154,130,148.4,138],["l",166,130,160.4,138],["l",178,130,172.4,138],["l",190,130,184.4,138],["l",202,130,196.4,138],["l",214,130,208.4,138],["l",226,130,220.4,138],["l",238,130,232.4,138],["l",250,130,244.4,138],["l",262,130,256.4,138],["l",274,130,268.4,138],["l",286,130,280.4,138],["l",298,130,292.4,138],["l",310,130,304.4,138],["r",90,90,160,40,"f"],["t",170,115,"2 kg","ms"],["r",120,55,70,35,"f"],["t",155,77,"1 kg","ms"],["t",200,77,"μ = 0.5"],["l",250,110,300,110,"a"],["t",304,114,"F"],["t",170,148,"smooth table","ms"]]},"n":15,"s":"The upper block (1 kg) is accelerated only by friction from the lower block.\nMaximum acceleration of the 1 kg block: a = μg = 0.5 × 10 = 5 m/s².\nFor the blocks to move together, both must have this acceleration at most.\nF = (1 + 2) × 5 = 15 N (table is smooth, g = 10 m/s²)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A body of mass 'm' is launched up on a rough inclined plane making an angle of 30° with the horizontal. The coefficient of friction between the body and plane is {√x;5} if the time of ascent is half of the time of descent. The value of x is ____.","n":3,"s":"Going up, friction acts down the plane: a₁ = g(sin30° + μcos30°). Coming down: a₂ = g(sin30° − μcos30°).\nThe distance is the same: ½a₁t₁² = ½a₂t₂². With t₁ = t₂/2, a₁ = 4a₂.\n½ + μ(√3/2) = 4(½ − μ√3/2), so 5μ√3/2 = 3/2.\nμ = 3/(5√3) = √3/5, so √x = √3 and x = 3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 17 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Block on block and wedge","q":"Two blocks (m = 0.5 kg and M = 4.5 kg) are arranged on a horizontal frictionless table. The coefficient of static friction between the two blocks is 3/7. Then the maximum horizontal force that can be applied on the larger block so that the blocks move together is ____ N.\n(Round off to the nearest integer)\n[Take g as 9.8 m s⁻²]","fig":{"w":340,"h":155,"alt":"Small block m on a larger block M on a frictionless table; force F pulls the larger block M","d":[["l",30,130,310,130],["l",30,130,310,130],["l",34,130,28.4,138],["l",46,130,40.4,138],["l",58,130,52.4,138],["l",70,130,64.4,138],["l",82,130,76.4,138],["l",94,130,88.4,138],["l",106,130,100.4,138],["l",118,130,112.4,138],["l",130,130,124.4,138],["l",142,130,136.4,138],["l",154,130,148.4,138],["l",166,130,160.4,138],["l",178,130,172.4,138],["l",190,130,184.4,138],["l",202,130,196.4,138],["l",214,130,208.4,138],["l",226,130,220.4,138],["l",238,130,232.4,138],["l",250,130,244.4,138],["l",262,130,256.4,138],["l",274,130,268.4,138],["l",286,130,280.4,138],["l",298,130,292.4,138],["l",310,130,304.4,138],["r",90,90,160,40,"f"],["t",170,115,"M","ms"],["r",140,55,60,35,"f"],["t",170,77,"m","ms"],["l",250,110,300,110,"a"],["t",304,114,"F"],["t",170,148,"frictionless table","ms"]]},"n":21,"s":"The small block m is accelerated only by static friction from M.\nMaximum common acceleration: a = μg = (3/7) × 9.8 = 4.2 m/s².\nF = (m + M)a = (0.5 + 4.5) × 4.2 = 21 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 17 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"plc","st":"Constraints and special systems","q":"A boy of mass 4 kg is standing on a piece of wood having mass 5 kg. If the coefficient of friction between the wood and the floor is 0.5, maximum force that the boy can exert on the rope so that the piece of wood does not move from its place is ____ N. (Round off to the nearest integer)\n[Take g = 10 m s⁻²]","fig":{"w":340,"h":210,"alt":"A boy standing on a block of wood pulls a rope that goes over a pulley, along the ceiling, down round a lower pulley and is tied horizontally to the wood","d":[["l",20,15,320,15,"B"],["l",20,200,320,200],["l",110,15,110,65],["c",110,65,20],["c",110,65,2,"F"],["l",260,15,260,65],["c",260,65,20],["c",260,65,2,"F"],["l",260,150,260,200],["c",260,150,20],["c",260,150,2,"F"],["p","M64 118 L90 65 A20 20 0 0 1 110 45 H260 A20 20 0 0 1 280 65 V150 A20 20 0 0 1 260 170 H140","B"],["r",40,130,100,70,"f"],["t",90,190,"wood 5 kg","ms"],["c",62,108,4],["l",62,112,62,126],["l",62,118,56,124],["t",190,38,"T","m"],["t",288,110,"T"],["t",190,164,"T","m"],["t",76,86,"T","e"],["t",40,100,"F","e"],["t",8,96,"boy","s"],["t",40,125,"4 kg","es"]]},"n":30,"s":"The rope is one light string, so the tension is T = F everywhere.\nThe end of the rope tied to the wood pulls it horizontally with T. The boy pulls the rope down with F, so the rope pulls the boy (and the wood he stands on) up with T.\nFor boy + wood (9 kg): floor reaction N = 90 − T.\nThe wood is about to move when T = μN = 0.5(90 − T).\n1.5T = 45, so T = F = 30 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 17 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"A body of mass 1 kg rests on a horizontal floor with which it has a coefficient of static friction 1/√3. It is desired to make the body move by applying the minimum possible force F N. The value of F will be ____. (Round off to the nearest integer)\n(Take g = 10 m/s²)","n":5,"s":"The minimum force to move the body is F = μmg/√(1 + μ²), applied at angle tanφ = μ above the horizontal.\nμ = 1/√3, so √(1 + μ²) = √(4/3) = 2/√3.\nF = (10/√3) × (√3/2) = 5 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 24 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"An inclined plane is bent in such a way that the vertical cross-section is given by y = {x²;4} where y is in vertical and x in horizontal direction. If the upper surface of this curved plane is rough with coefficient of friction μ = 0.5, the maximum height in cm at which a stationary block will not slip downward is ____ cm.","n":25,"s":"The slope of the curve at x is tanθ = dy/dx = x/2.\nThe block is just on the point of slipping when tanθ = μ = 0.5, so x/2 = 0.5 and x = 1 m.\nThe height there is y = x²/4 = 1/4 m = 25 cm."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 24 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"The coefficient of static friction between a wooden block of mass 0.5 kg and a vertical rough wall is 0.2. The magnitude of horizontal force that should be applied on the block to keep it adhere to the wall will be ____ N.\n[g = 10 m s⁻²]","n":25,"s":"The horizontal force F presses the block on the wall, so N = F.\nTo keep the block from sliding down: friction = weight, μN ≥ mg.\nF ≥ mg/μ = (0.5 × 10)/0.2 = 25 N.\nThe minimum force required is 25 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Static and kinetic friction","q":"As shown in figure, a block of mass √3 kg is kept on a horizontal rough surface of coefficient of friction μ = {1;3√3}. The critical force to be applied on the vertical surface as shown at an angle 60° with horizontal such that it does not move, will be 3x. The value of x will be ____.\n[g = 10 m/s², sin60° = {√3;2}; cos60° = {1;2}]","fig":{"w":340,"h":160,"alt":"A block of mass √3 kg on a rough floor pushed on its vertical face by a force F directed downward at 60° to the horizontal","d":[["l",40,140,300,140],["r",130,60,80,80,"f"],["t",170,105,"m = √3 kg","ms"],["l",100,48,130,100,"a"],["t",96,44,"F","e"],["l",60,100,130,100,"d"],["t",112,94,"60°","e"],["t",220,80,"μ = 1/(3√3)"]]},"n":3.33,"s":"F acts on the vertical face, pointing forward and downward at 60° to the horizontal.\nHorizontal: F cos60° = F/2 must not exceed friction.\nVertical: N = mg + F sin60° = 10√3 + (√3/2)F.\nCritical: F/2 = μN = (1/(3√3))(10√3 + (√3/2)F) = 10/3 + F/6.\nF/3 = 10/3, so F = 10 N.\n3x = 10, so x = 10/3 ≈ 3.33."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 2 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A small block starts slipping down from a point B on an inclined plane AB, which is making an angle θ with the horizontal. The section BC is smooth and the remaining section CA is rough with a coefficient of friction μ. It is found that the block comes to rest as it reaches the bottom (point A) of the inclined plane. If BC = 2AC, the coefficient of friction is given by μ = k tanθ. The value of k is ____.","fig":{"w":340,"h":160,"alt":"Inclined plane AB at angle θ; block at top B, C is one third of the way up from A","d":[["l",40,130,320,130],["l",40,130,290,39],["p","M266.5 47.6 L287.2 40.0 L282.4 26.9 L261.7 34.4 Z","f"],["l",123.3,95.7,123.3,103.7],["t",36,144,"A","m"],["t",292,32,"B"],["t",119.3,117.7,"C","m"],["t",90,148,"θ","m"],["t",205,50,"smooth","ms"],["t",82,98,"rough","ms"]]},"n":3,"s":"Use the work–energy theorem from B to A: the block starts and ends at rest.\nWork by gravity = mg·(BA)·sinθ. Friction acts only on CA: work done against it = μmg cosθ·(CA).\nBC = 2AC, so BA = 3AC.\nmg sinθ · 3AC = μ mg cosθ · AC, which gives μ = 3 tanθ.\nSo k = 3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 3 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block starts moving up an inclined plane of inclination 30° with an initial velocity of v₀. It comes back to its initial position with velocity {v₀;2}. The value of the coefficient of kinetic friction between the block and the inclined plane is close to {I;1000}. The nearest integer to I is ____.","n":346,"s":"Going up: a₁ = g(sin30° + μcos30°); distance d = v₀²/(2a₁).\nComing down over the same d: (v₀/2)² = 2a₂d with a₂ = g(sin30° − μcos30°).\nSubstituting d: v₀²/4 = a₂v₀²/a₁, so a₂/a₁ = 1/4.\n4(sin30° − μcos30°) = sin30° + μcos30°, so 3 sin30° = 5μ cos30°.\nμ = (3/5)tan30° = 0.6/√3 = 0.3464, so I = 346."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2011","tp":"plf","st":"Block on an incline","q":"A block is moving on an inclined plane making an angle 45° with the horizontal and the coefficient of friction is μ. The force required to just push it up the inclined plane is 3 times the force required to just prevent it from sliding down. If we define N = 10 μ, then N is ____.","n":5,"s":"Force to just push it up: F₁ = mg(sinθ + μcosθ). Force to just prevent sliding down: F₂ = mg(sinθ − μcosθ).\nF₁ = 3F₂: sinθ + μcosθ = 3 sinθ − 3μ cosθ.\n4μcosθ = 2 sinθ, so μ = ½ tanθ = ½ (θ = 45°).\nN = 10μ = 5."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2014","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"A block of mass m₁ = 1 kg, another mass m₂ = 2 kg, are placed together (see figure) on an inclined plane with angle of inclination θ. Various values of θ are given in List I. The coefficient of friction between the block m₁ and the plane is always zero. The coefficient of static and dynamic friction between the block m₂ and the plane are equal to μ = 0.3. In List II expressions for the friction on block m₂ are given. Match the correct expression of the friction in List II with the angles given in List I, and choose the correct option. The acceleration due to gravity is denoted by g.\n[Useful information : tan(5.5°) ≈ 0.1; tan(11.5°) ≈ 0.2; tan(16.5°) ≈ 0.3]\nList I:  P. θ = 5°   Q. θ = 10°   R. θ = 15°   S. θ = 20°\nList II:  1. m₂g sinθ   2. (m₁ + m₂)g sinθ   3. μm₂g cosθ   4. μ(m₁ + m₂)g cosθ","fig":{"w":340,"h":165,"alt":"Two blocks m₁ (upper) and m₂ (lower) in contact on a rough incline of angle θ","d":[["l",40,40,40,150],["l",40,150,300,150],["l",40,40,300,150],["p","M111.8 70.4 L152.4 87.5 L160.9 67.3 L120.4 50.1 Z","f"],["p","M154.2 88.3 L194.7 105.5 L203.3 85.2 L162.8 68.1 Z","f"],["t",141.7,42.1,"m₁","m"],["t",198,60,"m₂","m"],["t",255,144,"θ","m"]]},"o":["P-1, Q-1, R-1, S-3","P-2, Q-2, R-2, S-3","P-2, Q-2, R-2, S-4","P-2, Q-2, R-3, S-3"],"c":3,"s":"m₁ is frictionless and rests against m₂, so the two blocks move (or stay) together and m₂ alone has friction.\nThey stay at rest if (m₁ + m₂)g sinθ ≤ μm₂g cosθ, i.e. 3 sinθ ≤ 0.6 cosθ, i.e. tanθ ≤ 0.2 (θ ≤ 11.5°).\nθ = 5° and 10°: static friction balances the whole weight component: f = (m₁ + m₂)g sinθ, which is 2 (P-2, Q-2).\nθ = 15° and 20° (tanθ > 0.2): the blocks slide and friction is kinetic: f = μm₂g cosθ, which is 3 (R-3, S-3).\nAnswer: (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : The limiting force of static friction depends on the area of contact and independent of materials.\nStatement II : The limiting force of kinetic friction is independent of the area of contact and depends on materials.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."],"c":1,"s":"Limiting static friction f = μsN. It does not depend on the area of contact, and it does depend on the materials (through μs).\nSo Statement I is incorrect (both its parts are wrong).\nKinetic friction f = μkN is also independent of the area of contact and depends on the materials (through μk). So Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (b)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2008","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Statement-1 : It is easier to pull a heavy object than to push it on a level ground.\nStatement-2 : The magnitude of frictional force depends on the nature of the two surfaces in contact.","o":["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true."],"c":1,"s":"Statement-1 is true: when pulling at an upward angle, the vertical part of the pull reduces the normal reaction and so reduces friction; when pushing at a downward angle the normal reaction and friction increase.\nStatement-2 is also true: friction depends on the surfaces through the coefficient μ.\nBut the reason for Statement-1 is the change in normal reaction (f = μN), not the nature of the surfaces, which is the same in both cases.\nSo statement-2 is not the correct explanation. Answer: (b)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2007","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Statement-1 : A block of mass m starts moving on a rough horizontal surface with a velocity v. It stops due to friction between the block and the surface after moving through a certain distance. The surface is now tilted to an angle of 30° with the horizontal and the same block is made to go up with the same initial velocity v. The decrease in the mechanical energy in the second situation is smaller than that in the first situation.\nStatement-2 : The coefficient of friction between the block and the surface decreases with the increase in the angle of inclination.","o":["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true."],"c":2,"s":"The mechanical energy lost equals the work done against friction.\nHorizontal: loss = μmg × d₁, and the block loses all its kinetic energy ½mv².\nOn the incline the block also gains potential energy, so it stops after a shorter distance d₂ < d₁, and the normal force is mg cos30° < mg.\nLoss = μmg cos30° × d₂, which is smaller than μmg d₁. So Statement-1 is true.\nThe coefficient of friction depends only on the two surfaces, not on the angle, so Statement-2 is false.\nAnswer: (c)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2006","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"A circular disc with a groove along its diameter is placed horizontally on a rough surface. A block of mass 1 kg is placed as shown in the figure. The co-efficient of friction between the block and all surfaces of groove and horizontal surface in contact is 2/5. The disc has an acceleration of 25 m/s² towards left. Find the acceleration of the block with respect to disc. Given cosθ = 4/5, sinθ = 3/5.","fig":{"w":340,"h":228,"alt":"Top view of a horizontal disc with a groove along a diameter at angle θ to the horizontal; a block m sits in the groove; the disc accelerates to the left at 25 m/s²","d":[["c",170,100,80],["l",103.6,55.2,231.6,151.2],["l",108.4,48.8,236.4,144.8],["c",136.4,74.8,6,"F"],["t",126.4,68.8,"m","e"],["l",136.4,74.8,226.4,74.8,"d"],["t",184.4,90.8,"θ","m"],["l",215,200,125,200,"a"],["t",170,216,"disc: a = 25 m/s² to the left","ms"]]},"a":"10 m/s² (along the groove)","s":"Work in the frame of the disc. The block feels a pseudo force ma = 1 × 25 = 25 N to the right (opposite to the disc's acceleration).\nAlong the groove: 25 cosθ = 20 N (this tries to move the block along the groove).\nAcross the groove: 25 sinθ = 15 N presses the block on the groove wall, so the wall normal is 15 N.\nThe groove floor supports the weight: normal = mg = 10 N.\nFriction from the wall = 0.4 × 15 = 6 N and from the floor = 0.4 × 10 = 4 N, both along the groove, opposing motion.\nma' = 20 − 6 − 4 = 10, so the acceleration of the block relative to the disc is 10 m/s² along the groove."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2005","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Two identical ladders are arranged as shown in the figure. Mass of each ladder is M and length L. The system is in equilibrium. Find direction and magnitude of frictional force acting at A or B.","fig":{"w":340,"h":215,"alt":"Two identical ladders PA and PB hinged at P, standing on a rough floor with angle θ at A and B, a block of mass m hanging from P","d":[["l",20,190,320,190],["l",170,22,60,190],["l",170,22,280,190],["l",170,22,170,95],["r",148,95,44,32,"f"],["t",170,116,"m","ms"],["t",170,14,"P","m"],["t",52,186,"A","e"],["t",288,186,"B"],["t",82,184,"θ"],["t",258,184,"θ","e"],["t",170,208,"PA = PB = L","m"]]},"a":"(M + m) g cotθ / 2, along AB (friction at A acts towards B and at B towards A)","s":"Take the ladder PA. By symmetry the hinge P carries half of the hanging block, so it pushes down on each ladder with mg/2. The other ladder also pushes P sideways with a horizontal force H.\nVertical: N_A = Mg + mg/2. Horizontal: the friction f at A balances H, so f = H.\nTorque about A: Mg·(L/2)cosθ + (mg/2)·L cosθ = H·L sinθ.\nH = (M + m) g cotθ / 2, so f = (M + m) g cotθ / 2.\nThe feet tend to slide apart, so friction at A points along the floor towards B, and at B towards A."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2004","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Two blocks A and B of equal masses are placed on rough inclined plane as shown in figure. When and where will the two blocks come on the same line on the inclined plane if they are released simultaneously? Initially the block A is √2 m behind the block B. Co-efficient of kinetic friction for the blocks A and B are 0.2 and 0.3 respectively (g = 10 m/s²).","fig":{"w":340,"h":235,"alt":"Blocks A (higher) and B (lower) on a 45° incline, A is √2 m up the slope behind B","d":[["l",100,20,100,220],["l",100,220,300,220],["l",100,20,300,220],["p","M123.3 43.3 L140.3 60.3 L153.0 47.6 L136.1 30.6 Z","f"],["p","M165.8 85.8 L182.7 102.7 L195.5 90.0 L178.5 73.0 Z","f"],["t",149.8,33.8,"A"],["t",192.2,76.2,"B"],["t",252,214,"45°","e"],["t",105.8,81.8,"√2 m","e"],["l",117.8,65.8,160.2,108.2,"b"]]},"a":"After 2 s; block A moves S_A = 8√2 m and block B moves S_B = 7√2 m","s":"On the 45° incline each block slides with a = g(sin45° − μ cos45°) = (10/√2)(1 − μ).\na_A = 7.07 × 0.8 = 4√2 m/s²; a_B = 7.07 × 0.7 = 3.5√2 m/s².\nA gains on B with relative acceleration a_A − a_B = 1/√2 m/s².\nThey come in line when A has covered √2 m more than B: ½(1/√2)t² = √2, so t² = 4 and t = 2 s.\nS_A = ½(4√2)(4) = 8√2 m and S_B = ½(3.5√2)(4) = 7√2 m."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2000","tp":"plc","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"In the figure masses m₁, m₂ and M are 20 kg, 5 kg and 50 kg respectively. The co-efficient of friction between M and ground is zero. The coefficient of friction between m₁ and M and that between m₂ and ground is 0.3. The pulleys and the strings are massless. The string is perfectly horizontal between P₁ and m₁ and also between P₂ and m₂. The string is perfectly vertical between P₁ and P₂. An external horizontal force F is applied to the mass M. Take g = 10 m/s².\n(i) Draw a free body diagram for mass M, clearly showing all the forces.\n(ii) Let the magnitude of the force of friction between m₁ and M be f₁ and that between m₂ and ground be f₂. For a particular F it is found that f₁ = 2f₂. Find f₁ and f₂. Write equations of motion of all the masses. Find F, tension in the string and acceleration of the masses.","fig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"Block m₁ on top of block M, pulleys P₁ and P₂ fixed on M's left side joined by a vertical string, block m₂ on the ground to the left; force F pulls M to the right","d":[["l",20,190,330,190],["l",20,190,330,190],["l",24,190,18.4,198],["l",36,190,30.4,198],["l",48,190,42.4,198],["l",60,190,54.4,198],["l",72,190,66.4,198],["l",84,190,78.4,198],["l",96,190,90.4,198],["l",108,190,102.4,198],["l",120,190,114.4,198],["l",132,190,126.4,198],["l",144,190,138.4,198],["l",156,190,150.4,198],["l",168,190,162.4,198],["l",180,190,174.4,198],["l",192,190,186.4,198],["l",204,190,198.4,198],["l",216,190,210.4,198],["l",228,190,222.4,198],["l",240,190,234.4,198],["l",252,190,246.4,198],["l",264,190,258.4,198],["l",276,190,270.4,198],["l",288,190,282.4,198],["l",300,190,294.4,198],["l",312,190,306.4,198],["l",324,190,318.4,198],["r",200,100,95,90,"f"],["t",250,150,"M","ms"],["r",240,60,50,40,"f"],["t",265,85,"m₁","ms"],["c",200,84,14],["c",200,160,14],["l",200,84,200,100],["l",200,160,200,176],["p","M240 70 H200 A14 14 0 0 0 186 84 V160 A14 14 0 0 0 200 174 H105","B"],["r",60,160,45,30,"f"],["t",82,180,"m₂","ms"],["t",180,62,"P₁","e"],["t",178,146,"P₂","e"],["l",295,145,330,145,"a"],["t",316,138,"F","m"]]},"a":"(i) FBD of M: see solution.  (ii) f₁ = 30 N, f₂ = 15 N; F = 60 N, T = 18 N, a = 3/5 m/s² (= 0.6 m/s²) for all three masses","s":"(i) Forces on M: weight Mg down; ground reaction up (no friction with ground); F to the right; from m₁: weight m₁g down and friction f₁ to the left; at P₁: string pull T to the right (towards m₁) and T down; at P₂: string pull T up and T to the left (towards m₂). The horizontal pulls T cancel.\n(ii) The pulleys move with M, so the string length gives 2a = a₁ + a₂. If m₁ does not slip on M, a₁ = a and so a₂ = a: all masses have the same acceleration a.\nm₂ (slides on ground, kinetic friction): T − f₂ = 5a, and f₂ = μm₂g = 15 N.\nm₁: f₁ − T = 20a. M: F − f₁ = 50a.\nAdding the first two: f₁ − f₂ = 25a. With f₁ = 2f₂: f₂ = 25a, so a = 15/25 = 0.6 m/s², f₂ = 15 N, f₁ = 30 N.\nT = f₂ + 5a = 18 N and F = f₁ + 50a = 30 + 30 = 60 N.\n(Check: f₁ = 30 N is less than the limit μm₁g = 60 N, so m₁ does not slip.)"},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1997","tp":"plc","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Block A of mass m and block B of mass 2m are placed on a fixed triangular wedge by means of a massless inextensible string and a frictionless pulley as shown in figure. The wedge is inclined at 45° to the horizontal on both sides. The co-efficient of friction between block A and the wedge is 2/3 and that between block B and the wedge is 1/3. If the system A and B is released from rest, find\n(i) The acceleration of A,\n(ii) Tension in the string, and\n(iii) The magnitude and direction of the force of friction acting on A.","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"Block A (mass m) and block B (mass 2m) on the two 45° faces of a fixed wedge, joined by a string over a pulley at the top","d":[["l",20,170,330,170],["l",20,170,330,170],["l",24,170,18.4,178],["l",36,170,30.4,178],["l",48,170,42.4,178],["l",60,170,54.4,178],["l",72,170,66.4,178],["l",84,170,78.4,178],["l",96,170,90.4,178],["l",108,170,102.4,178],["l",120,170,114.4,178],["l",132,170,126.4,178],["l",144,170,138.4,178],["l",156,170,150.4,178],["l",168,170,162.4,178],["l",180,170,174.4,178],["l",192,170,186.4,178],["l",204,170,198.4,178],["l",216,170,210.4,178],["l",228,170,222.4,178],["l",240,170,234.4,178],["l",252,170,246.4,178],["l",264,170,258.4,178],["l",276,170,270.4,178],["l",288,170,282.4,178],["l",300,170,294.4,178],["l",312,170,306.4,178],["l",324,170,318.4,178],["l",30,170,170,30],["l",310,170,170,30],["c",170,30,10],["p","M90.8 109.2 L110.6 89.4 L95.1 73.8 L75.3 93.6 Z","f"],["p","M252.0 112.0 L226.6 86.6 L246.4 66.8 L271.8 92.2 Z","f"],["l",93.6,92.2,162.9,22.9],["l",246.4,92.2,177.1,22.9],["p","M162.9 22.9 A10 10 0 0 1 177.1 22.9",""],["t",70.7,79.3,"A (m)","e"],["t",269.3,73.3,"B (2m)"],["t",70,164,"45°"],["t",258,164,"45°","e"],["t",100,150,"μ = 2/3","m"],["t",240,150,"μ = 1/3","m"]]},"a":"(i) a = 0   (ii) T = 2√2 mg/3   (iii) F = mg/(3√2), acting down the incline on A","s":"Forces along the faces: A's weight component = mg sin45° = mg/√2 ≈ 0.707mg (down). B's = 2mg sin45° = √2 mg ≈ 1.414mg (down).\nIf B went down, A would go up: driving force = √2mg − mg/√2 = mg/√2 ≈ 0.707mg.\nMaximum friction: on A = (2/3)(mg cos45°) ≈ 0.471mg; on B = (1/3)(2mg cos45°) ≈ 0.471mg; total 0.943mg, which is more than 0.707mg. So the system stays at rest: a = 0.\nB tends to move down, so its friction is limiting (up its face): f_B = (1/3)(2mg)cos45° = √2mg/3.\nB: T + f_B = √2mg, so T = √2mg − √2mg/3 = 2√2mg/3.\nA tends to move up, so friction on A acts down its face: T = mg/√2 + f_A, so f_A = 2√2mg/3 − mg/√2 = mg/(3√2) ≈ 0.236mg (less than its limit 0.471mg)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1987","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"A particle of mass m rests on a horizontal floor with which it has a co-efficient of static friction μ. It is desired to make the body move by applying the minimum possible force F. Find the magnitude of F and the direction in which it has to be applied.","a":"F = mg sinθ, where θ = tan⁻¹μ above the horizontal (F_min = μmg/√(1 + μ²))","s":"Let F act at angle φ above the horizontal. Normal reaction N = mg − F sinφ.\nThe body is about to move when F cosφ = μ(mg − F sinφ), so F = μmg/(cosφ + μ sinφ).\nF is smallest when cosφ + μ sinφ is largest. Its maximum value is √(1 + μ²), when tanφ = μ.\nSo F_min = μmg/√(1 + μ²). With tanθ = μ, sinθ = μ/√(1 + μ²), so F_min = mg sinθ.\nIt must be applied at the angle θ = tan⁻¹μ above the horizontal."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1981","tp":"plc","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Masses M₁, M₂ and M₃ are connected by strings of negligible mass which pass over massless and frictionless pulleys P₁ and P₂ as shown in the figure. The masses move such that the portion of the string between P₁ and P₂ is parallel to the inclined plane and the portion of the string between P₂ and M₃ is horizontal. The masses M₂ and M₃ are 4.0 kg each and the co-efficient of kinetic friction between the masses and the surfaces is 0.25. The inclined plane makes an angle of 37° with the horizontal. If the mass M₁ moves downwards with a uniform velocity, find\n(i) The mass of M₁\n(ii) The tension in the horizontal portion of the string\n(g = 9.8 m/sec², sin37° ≅ 3/5).","fig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"M₁ hangs over pulley P₁ at the top of a 37° incline; M₂ on the incline is joined to M₁ and, over pulley P₂ at the foot, to M₃ on the horizontal surface","d":[["l",20,190,330,190],["l",60,40,60,190],["l",60,40,260,190],["c",57.5,43.4,10],["c",258,170,9],["p","M135.2 96.4 L160.8 115.6 L172.8 99.6 L147.2 80.4 Z","f"],["r",36.5,120,22,22,"f"],["t",47.5,135,"M₁","ms"],["p","M252.6 177.2 A9 9 0 0 0 258 179 H290",""],["r",290,159,30,30,"f"],["t",305,178,"M₃","ms"],["t",158,76,"M₂"],["t",66,28,"P₁"],["t",266,166,"P₂"],["t",218,186,"37°","m"],["p","M138.7 91.8 L63.5 35.4 A10 10 0 0 0 47.5 43.4 V120",""],["l",164.3,111,252.6,177.2]]},"a":"(i) M₁ = 4.2 kg   (ii) T = 9.8 N","s":"M₁ moves down with uniform velocity, so M₂ moves up the incline and M₃ slides towards P₂, all at constant speed (no acceleration).\nM₃ (horizontal): T₂ = μM₃g = 0.25 × 4 × 9.8 = 9.8 N. This is the tension in the horizontal portion.\nM₂ (moving up the plane, friction down the plane): T₁ = T₂ + M₂g sin37° + μM₂g cos37°\n= 9.8 + 4(9.8)(0.6) + 0.25(4)(9.8)(0.8) = 9.8 + 23.52 + 7.84 = 41.16 N.\nM₁: M₁g = T₁, so M₁ = 41.16/9.8 = 4.2 kg."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1979","tp":"plc","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Two blocks connected by a massless string slides down an inclined plane having an angle of inclination of 37°. The masses of the two blocks are M₁ = 4 kg and M₂ = 2 kg respectively and the coefficients of friction of M₁ and M₂ with the inclined plane are 0.75 and 0.25 respectively. Assuming the string to be taut, find (i) the common acceleration of two masses and (ii) the tension in the string.\n(sin 37° = 0.6, cos 37° = 0.8)\n[Take g = 9.8 m s⁻²]","fig":{"w":340,"h":195,"alt":"Blocks M₁ (upper) and M₂ (lower) joined by a string slide down a 37° incline","d":[["l",20,180,330,180],["l",60,30,60,180],["l",60,30,260,180],["p","M104.8 63.6 L127.2 80.4 L139.2 64.4 L116.8 47.6 Z","f"],["p","M160.8 105.6 L183.2 122.4 L195.2 106.4 L172.8 89.6 Z","f"],["l",132,74,165.6,99.2],["t",124,44,"M₁"],["t",180,86,"M₂"],["t",222,176,"37°","m"]]},"a":"(i) a = 1.3 m/s²   (ii) T = 5.24 N","s":"Treat both blocks as one system moving down with common acceleration a.\n(M₁ + M₂)a = (M₁ + M₂)g sin37° − μ₁M₁g cos37° − μ₂M₂g cos37°\n6a = 6(9.8)(0.6) − 0.75(4)(9.8)(0.8) − 0.25(2)(9.8)(0.8) = 35.28 − 23.52 − 3.92 = 7.84.\na = 7.84/6 ≈ 1.3 m/s².\nFor the lower block M₂ (the string pulls it up the plane): M₂g sin37° − μ₂M₂g cos37° − T = M₂a.\nT = 2(9.8)(0.6) − 0.25(2)(9.8)(0.8) − 2(1.3) = 11.76 − 3.92 − 2.6 = 5.24 N."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1978","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"A block of mass 2 kg slides on an inclined plane which makes an angle of 30° with the horizontal. The coefficient of friction between the block and the surface is {√3;√2}. What force should be applied to the block so that it moves (a) down and (b) up without any acceleration. (Force is along the plane; take g = 9.8 m/s².)","a":"(a) 10.99 N   (b) 30.58 N","s":"Along the plane: mg sin30° = 2 × 9.8 × 0.5 = 9.8 N.\nMaximum friction = μmg cos30° = (√3/√2)(2)(9.8)(√3/2) ≈ 20.79 N.\nSince μ > tan30°, the block does not slide by itself. To move it down at constant speed it must be pushed down the plane: F + 9.8 = 20.79, so F ≈ 10.99 N.\nTo move it up at constant speed it must be pulled up the plane: F = 9.8 + 20.79 ≈ 30.6 N (the book gives 30.58 N)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1978","tp":"plc","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"In the figure, the blocks A, B and C have masses 3 kg, 4 kg and 8 kg respectively. The coefficient of sliding friction between any two surfaces is 0.25. A is held at rest by a massless rigid rod fixed to the wall, while B and C are connected by a light flexible cord passing around a fixed frictionless pulley. Find the force F necessary to drag C along the horizontal surface to the left at a constant speed. Assume that the arrangement shown in the figure, i.e. B on C and A on B, is maintained throughout. (Take g = 9.8 m/s².)","fig":{"w":340,"h":200,"alt":"Block A on block B on block C on the floor; A is tied to the wall by a rod; B and C are joined by a cord round a pulley fixed to the wall; force F pulls C to the left","d":[["l",20,190,300,190],["l",20,190,300,190],["l",24,190,18.4,198],["l",36,190,30.4,198],["l",48,190,42.4,198],["l",60,190,54.4,198],["l",72,190,66.4,198],["l",84,190,78.4,198],["l",96,190,90.4,198],["l",108,190,102.4,198],["l",120,190,114.4,198],["l",132,190,126.4,198],["l",144,190,138.4,198],["l",156,190,150.4,198],["l",168,190,162.4,198],["l",180,190,174.4,198],["l",192,190,186.4,198],["l",204,190,198.4,198],["l",216,190,210.4,198],["l",228,190,222.4,198],["l",240,190,234.4,198],["l",252,190,246.4,198],["l",264,190,258.4,198],["l",276,190,270.4,198],["l",288,190,282.4,198],["l",300,190,294.4,198],["l",300,20,300,190,"B"],["l",300,30,308,22],["l",300,42,308,34],["l",300,54,308,46],["l",300,66,308,58],["l",300,78,308,70],["l",300,90,308,82],["l",300,102,308,94],["l",300,114,308,106],["l",300,126,308,118],["l",300,138,308,130],["l",300,150,308,142],["l",300,162,308,154],["l",300,174,308,166],["l",300,186,308,178],["r",70,140,160,50,"f"],["t",150,170,"C","ms"],["r",100,100,100,40,"f"],["t",150,124,"B","ms"],["r",120,60,60,40,"f"],["t",150,84,"A","ms"],["l",180,80,300,80],["c",282,140,10],["p","M200 130 H282 A10 10 0 0 1 282 150 H230",""],["l",70,165,30,165,"a"],["t",24,160,"F","e"]]},"a":"78.4 N","s":"C moves left at constant speed, so B moves right at the same speed (cord over the pulley). All accelerations are zero.\nFriction A–B: μm_Ag = 0.25 × 3 × 9.8 = 7.35 N. Friction B–C: μ(m_A + m_B)g = 0.25 × 7 × 9.8 = 17.15 N. Friction C–floor: μ(m_A + m_B + m_C)g = 0.25 × 15 × 9.8 = 36.75 N.\nBlock B (moves right): tension T balances both frictions on it: T = 7.35 + 17.15 = 24.5 N.\nBlock C (moves left): F = T + 17.15 + 36.75 = 24.5 + 17.15 + 36.75 = 78.4 N."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1981","tp":"plf","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"When a person walks on a rough surface, the frictional force exerted by the surface on the person is opposite to the direction of his motion.","o":["True","False"],"c":1,"s":"While walking, the foot pushes the ground backward.\nBy Newton's third law, the ground pushes the person forward with friction.\nSo the friction on the person acts in the direction of his motion, and it is what makes walking possible.\nThe statement is False."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1984","tp":"plf","st":"Pseudo force and accelerating frames","q":"A block of mass 1 kg lies on a horizontal surface in a truck. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the surface is 0.6. If the acceleration of the truck is 5 m/s², the frictional force acting on the block is ____ newton.","n":5,"s":"Maximum static friction = μmg = 0.6 × 1 × 10 = 6 N (5.9 N with g = 9.8).\nThe block needs a force ma = 1 × 5 = 5 N to move with the truck.\nThis is less than the maximum, so the block does not slip and static friction supplies exactly 5 N.\nFrictional force = 5 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 24 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A car of mass 'm' moves on a banked road having radius 'r' and banking angle θ. To avoid slipping from banked road, the maximum permissible speed of the car is v₀. The coefficient of friction μ between the wheels of the car and the banked road is","o":["μ = {v₀² + rg tanθ;rg − v₀² tanθ}","μ = {v₀² − rg tanθ;rg + v₀² tanθ}","μ = {v₀² + rg tanθ;rg + v₀² tanθ}","μ = {v₀² − rg tanθ;rg − v₀² tanθ}"],"c":1,"s":"At the maximum speed friction acts down the slope with its limiting value μN.\nVertical: N cosθ = mg + μN sinθ. Horizontal: N sinθ + μN cosθ = mv₀²/r.\nDividing: v₀²/(rg) = (sinθ + μcosθ)/(cosθ − μ sinθ) = (tanθ + μ)/(1 − μ tanθ).\nv₀²(1 − μ tanθ) = rg tanθ + μ rg, so μ(rg + v₀² tanθ) = v₀² − rg tanθ.\nμ = (v₀² − rg tanθ)/(rg + v₀² tanθ). Answer: (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A car of 800 kg is taking turn on a banked road of radius 300 m and angle of banking 30°. If coefficient of static friction is 0.2 then the maximum speed with which car can negotiate the turn safely (g = 10 m/s², √3 = 1.73)","o":["51.4 m/s","70.4 m/s","264 m/s","102.8 m/s"],"c":0,"s":"Maximum safe speed on a banked road with friction: v² = rg(tanθ + μ)/(1 − μ tanθ).\ntan30° = 1/√3 = 0.578. Numerator = 0.578 + 0.2 = 0.778. Denominator = 1 − 0.2 × 0.578 = 0.884.\nv² = 300 × 10 × 0.778/0.884 ≈ 2640, so v ≈ 51.4 m/s.\nAnswer: (a) 51.4 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A ball of mass 0.5 kg is attached to a string of length 50 cm. The ball is rotated on a horizontal circular path about its vertical axis. The maximum tension that the string can bear is 400 N. The maximum possible value of angular velocity of the ball in rad/s is","o":["20","1000","1600","40"],"c":3,"s":"The string tension gives the centripetal force: T = mω²L.\nAt the maximum tension: 400 = 0.5 × ω² × 0.5.\nω² = 400/0.25 = 1600, so ω = 40 rad/s.\nAnswer: (d) 40."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A train is moving with a speed of 12 m/s on rails which are 1.5 m apart. To negotiate a curve of radius 400 m, the height by which the outer rail should be raised with respect to the inner rail is (Given, g = 10 m/s²)","o":["5.4 cm","4.8 cm","4.2 cm","6.0 cm"],"c":0,"s":"Ideal banking (no sideways force on the rails): tanθ = v²/(rg).\ntanθ = 144/(400 × 10) = 0.036.\nFor a small angle, h = l sinθ ≈ l tanθ = 1.5 × 0.036 = 0.054 m.\nh = 5.4 cm. Answer: (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"If the radius of curvature of the path of two particles of same mass are in the ratio 3 : 4, then in order to have constant centripetal force, their velocities will be in the ratio of","o":["√3 : 1","1 : √3","2 : √3","√3 : 2"],"c":3,"s":"Centripetal force F = mv²/r. The mass and force are the same for both particles.\nSo v² ∝ r, and v ∝ √r.\nv₁ : v₂ = √3 : √4 = √3 : 2.\nAnswer: (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Vertical circle","q":"A stone of mass 900 g is tied to a string and moved in a vertical circular orbit of radius 1 m making 10 rpm. The tension in the string, when the stone is at the lowest point is (if π² = 9.8 and g = 9.8 m/s²)","o":["8.82 N","9.8 N","97 N","17.8 N"],"c":1,"s":"ω = 10 rpm = 10 × 2π/60 = π/3 rad/s, so ω² = π²/9 = 9.8/9 = 1.089 rad²/s².\nAt the lowest point: T − mg = mω²r.\nT = m(g + ω²r) = 0.9 × (9.8 + 1.089 × 1) = 0.9 × 10.889 ≈ 9.8 N.\nAnswer: 9.8 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A coin is placed on a disc. The coefficient of friction between the coin and the disc is μ. If the distance of the coin from the center of the disc is r, the maximum angular velocity which can be given to the disc, so that the coin does not slip away, is","o":["{μ;√(rg)}","√{r;μg}","{μg;r}","√{μg;r}"],"c":3,"s":"Friction provides the centripetal force. The largest friction is μmg.\nmω²r = μmg at the limit.\nω² = μg/r, so ω = √(μg/r).\nAnswer: option (d)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A small block of mass 100 g is tied to a spring of spring constant 7.5 N/m and length 20 cm. The other end of spring fixed at a particular point A. If the block moves in a circular path on a smooth horizontal surface with constant angular velocity 5 rad/s about point A, then tension in the spring is","o":["0.25 N","1.5 N","0.50 N","0.75 N"],"c":3,"s":"Let the stretch be x. The radius of the circle is 0.2 + x (in m).\nThe spring force is the centripetal force: kx = mω²(0.2 + x).\n7.5x = 0.1 × 25 × (0.2 + x) = 0.5 + 2.5x, so 5x = 0.5 and x = 0.1 m.\nTension = kx = 7.5 × 0.1 = 0.75 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A child of mass 5 kg is going round a merry-go-round that makes 1 rotation in 3.14 s. The radius of the merry-go-round is 2 m. The centrifugal force on the child will be","o":["80 N","50 N","40 N","100 N"],"c":2,"s":"Time period T = 3.14 s, so ω = 2π/T = 2 rad/s.\nCentrifugal force has the same size as the centripetal force: F = mω²r.\nF = 5 × 2² × 2 = 40 N.\nAnswer: 40 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 11 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A coin placed on a rotating table just slips when it is placed at a distance of 1 cm from the center. If the angular velocity of the table is halved, it will just slip when placed at a distance of ______ from the centre.","o":["2 cm","1 cm","8 cm","4 cm"],"c":3,"s":"At the slipping point, friction is maximum: μmg = mω²r, so ω²r = μg = constant.\nIf ω becomes ω/2, then ω² becomes ω²/4, so r must become 4 times larger.\nr = 4 × 1 cm = 4 cm."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 13 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A vehicle of mass 200 kg is moving along a levelled curved road of radius 70 m with angular velocity of 0.2 rad/s. The centripetal force acting on the vehicle is","o":["2240 N","2800 N","560 N","14 N"],"c":2,"s":"Centripetal force F = mω²r.\nF = 200 × (0.2)² × 70 = 200 × 0.04 × 70 = 560 N.\nAnswer: 560 N."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 24 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A body of mass 200 g is tied to a spring of spring constant 12.5 N/m, while the other end of spring is fixed at point O. If the body moves about O in a circular path on a smooth horizontal surface with constant angular speed 5 rad/s. Then, the ratio of extension in the spring to its natural length will be (Take g = 10 m/s²)","o":["1 : 1","2 : 3","2 : 5","1 : 2"],"c":1,"s":"Let the natural length be l and the extension x. The radius of the circle is l + x.\nThe spring force gives the centripetal force: kx = mω²(l + x).\n12.5x = 0.2 × 25 × (l + x) = 5(l + x).\n7.5x = 5l, so x/l = 5/7.5 = 2/3.\nThe ratio is 2 : 3."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A car is moving with a constant speed of 20 m/s in a circular horizontal track of radius 40 m. A bob is suspended from the roof of the car by a massless string. The angle made by the string with the vertical will be (Take g = 10 m/s²)","o":["{π;6}","{π;2}","{π;4}","{π;3}"],"c":2,"s":"In the car's frame the bob is in equilibrium under mg, tension T and the pseudo force mv²/R.\nT cosθ = mg and T sinθ = mv²/R, so tanθ = v²/(Rg).\ntanθ = 400/(40 × 10) = 1, so θ = 45° = π/4."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A car is moving on a horizontal curved road with radius 50 m. The approximate maximum speed of car will be, if friction between tyres and road is 0.34. [Take g = 10 m s⁻²]","o":["3.4 m s⁻¹","13 m s⁻¹","17 m s⁻¹","22.4 m s⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"At the maximum speed, friction supplies the centripetal force: μmg = mv²/r.\nv = √(μgr) = √(0.34 × 10 × 50) = √170.\n√170 ≈ 13 m/s.\nAnswer: 13 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A stone of mass 1 kg is tied to end of a massless string of length 1 m. If the breaking tension of the string is 400 N, then maximum linear velocity, the stone can have without breaking the string, while rotating in horizontal plane, is","o":["40 m s⁻¹","20 m s⁻¹","400 m s⁻¹","10 m s⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"The tension gives the centripetal force: T = mv²/r.\nAt the breaking limit: 400 = 1 × v²/1, so v² = 400.\nv = 20 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A smooth circular groove has a smooth vertical wall. A block of mass m moves against the wall with a speed v. Which of the following curves represents the correct relation between the normal reaction on the block by the wall (N) and the speed of the block (v)?","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"Four graphs of N against v: (a) a parabola rising from the origin, (b) a straight line through the origin, (c) a horizontal line, (d) a curve rising steeply then flattening.","d":[["t",2,14,"(a)",""],["l",30,72,30,14,"a"],["l",30,72,150,72,"a"],["t",24,22,"N","es"],["t",148,83,"v","es"],["p","M30 72 Q90 72 135 20",""],["t",172,14,"(b)",""],["l",200,72,200,14,"a"],["l",200,72,320,72,"a"],["t",194,22,"N","es"],["t",318,83,"v","es"],["p","M200 72 L305 20",""],["t",2,109,"(c)",""],["l",30,167,30,109,"a"],["l",30,167,150,167,"a"],["t",24,117,"N","es"],["t",148,178,"v","es"],["p","M30 127 L135 127",""],["t",172,109,"(d)",""],["l",200,167,200,109,"a"],["l",200,167,320,167,"a"],["t",194,117,"N","es"],["t",318,178,"v","es"],["p","M200 167 Q212 117 305 115",""]]},"o":["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],"c":0,"s":"The smooth vertical wall pushes the block towards the centre. Only this normal reaction provides the centripetal force.\nN = mv²/R, with m and R constant.\nSo N is proportional to v². The graph of N against v is a parabola starting from the origin and getting steeper.\nThis is graph (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A boy ties a stone of mass 100 g to the end of a 2 m long string and whirls it around in a horizontal plane. The string can withstand the maximum tension of 80 N. If the maximum speed with which the stone can revolve is {K;π} rev./min. The value of K is\n(Assume the string is massless and unstretchable)","o":["400","300","600","800"],"c":2,"s":"At the limit, T = mω²r, so ω² = 80/(0.1 × 2) = 400 and ω = 20 rad/s.\nFrequency = ω/2π = 10/π rev/s = (10/π) × 60 = 600/π rev/min.\nComparing with K/π, K = 600."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Vertical circle","q":"A stone of mass m, tied to a string is being whirled in a vertical circle with a uniform speed. The tension in the string is","o":["the same throughout the motion.","minimum at the highest position of the circular path.","minimum at the lowest position of the circular path.","minimum when the rope is in the horizontal position."],"c":1,"s":"The speed is constant, so mv²/r is the same everywhere. Only the part of weight along the string changes.\nAt the top: T + mg = mv²/r, so T = mv²/r − mg (smallest).\nAt the bottom: T − mg = mv²/r, so T = mv²/r + mg (largest).\nSo the tension is minimum at the highest position."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"For a particle in uniform circular motion, the acceleration a⃗ at any point P(R, θ) on the circular path of radius R is (when θ is measured from the positive x-axis and v is uniform speed)","o":["−{v²;R} sinθ î + {v²;R} cosθ ĵ","−{v²;R} cosθ î + {v²;R} sinθ ĵ","−{v²;R} cosθ î − {v²;R} sinθ ĵ","−{v²;R} î + {v²;R} ĵ"],"c":2,"s":"For uniform circular motion the acceleration is v²/R and points towards the centre.\nThe position of P from the centre is (R cosθ, R sinθ), so the direction from P to the centre is −(cosθ î + sinθ ĵ).\na⃗ = −(v²/R)(cosθ î + sinθ ĵ).\nThis is option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A disc with a flat small bottom beaker placed on it at a distance R from its center is revolving about an axis passing through the center and perpendicular to its plane with an angular velocity ω. The coefficient of static friction between the bottom of the beaker and the surface of the disc is μ. The beaker will revolve with the disc if","o":["R ≤ {μg;2ω²}","R ≤ {μg;ω²}","R ≥ {μg;2ω²}","R ≥ {μg;ω²}"],"c":1,"s":"The beaker stays on the disc if the needed centripetal force is not more than the maximum friction.\nmω²R ≤ μmg.\nR ≤ μg/ω².\nThis is option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Vertical circle","q":"A ball is released from rest from point P of a smooth semi-spherical vessel as shown in figure. The ratio of the centripetal force and normal reaction on the ball at point Q is A while angular position of point Q is α with respect to point P. Which of the following graphs represent the correct relation between A and α when ball goes from Q to R?","fig":{"w":340,"h":285,"alt":"A smooth semicircular bowl with the ball starting at P on the rim; Q is at angle α from OP and R is the lowest point. Below are four graphs of A against α.","d":[["p","M95 20 A55 55 0 0 0 205 20"],["l",87,20,213,20],["c",95,20,3,"F"],["c",107.86755562845622,55.35331853275966,3,"F"],["l",150,20,107.86755562845622,55.35331853275966,"d"],["l",150,20,150,75,"d"],["l",150,20,95,20,"d"],["t",91,14,"P","e"],["t",154,14,"O",""],["t",101.86755562845622,67.35331853275966,"Q","e"],["t",154,78,"R",""],["t",126,33,"α","m"],["t",2,109,"(a)",""],["l",30,167,30,109,"a"],["l",30,167,150,167,"a"],["t",24,117,"A","es"],["t",148,178,"α","es"],["p","M34 115 Q44 161 135 163",""],["t",172,109,"(b)",""],["l",200,167,200,109,"a"],["l",200,167,320,167,"a"],["t",194,117,"A","es"],["t",318,178,"α","es"],["p","M200 167 Q212 117 305 115",""],["t",2,204,"(c)",""],["l",30,262,30,204,"a"],["l",30,262,150,262,"a"],["t",24,212,"A","es"],["t",148,273,"α","es"],["p","M30 222 L135 222",""],["t",172,204,"(d)",""],["l",200,262,200,204,"a"],["l",200,262,320,262,"a"],["t",194,212,"A","es"],["t",318,273,"α","es"],["p","M200 210 A95 52 0 0 1 295 262",""]]},"o":["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],"c":2,"s":"Let the ball be at angular position α below the level of P. Its height below P is R sinα.\nEnergy conservation: v² = 2gR sinα. Centripetal force = mv²/R = 2mg sinα.\nAlong the radius: N − mg sinα = mv²/R, so N = 3mg sinα.\nA = (2mg sinα)/(3mg sinα) = 2/3, which does not depend on α.\nSo the graph is a horizontal line: graph (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"One end of a massless spring of spring constant k and natural length l₀ is fixed while the other end is connected to a small objects of mass m lying on a frictionless table. The spring remains horizontal on the table. If the object is made to rotate at an angular velocity ω about an axis passing through fixed end, then the elongation of the spring will be","o":["{(k−mω²)l₀;mω²}","{mω²l₀;k+mω²}","{mω²l₀;k−mω²}","{(k+mω²)l₀;mω²}"],"c":2,"s":"Let the elongation be x. The radius of the circle is l₀ + x.\nThe spring force is the centripetal force: kx = mω²(l₀ + x).\nx(k − mω²) = mω²l₀.\nx = mω²l₀/(k − mω²), option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"The normal reaction 'N' for a vehicle of 800 kg mass, negotiating a turn on a 30° banked road at maximum possible speed without skidding is ______ × 10³ kg m s⁻². (Given cos 30° = 0.87, μₛ = 0.2, g = 9.8 m s⁻²)","o":["6.96","10.2","12.4","7.2"],"c":1,"s":"At the maximum speed friction acts down the slope with value μN. There is no vertical acceleration.\nVertical balance: N cosθ = mg + μN sinθ, so N = mg/(cosθ − μ sinθ).\nWith g = 9.8 m/s², cos30° ≈ 0.87 and sin30° = 0.5: N = 800 × 9.8/(0.87 − 0.2 × 0.5) = 7840/0.77 ≈ 10.2 × 10³ N.\nAnswer: 10.2 (the unit kg m s⁻² is newton)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A block of 200 g mass moves with a uniform speed in a horizontal circular groove, with vertical side walls of radius 20 cm. If the block takes 40 s to complete one round, the normal force by the side walls of the groove is","o":["9.859 × 10⁻⁴ N","6.28 × 10⁻³ N","9.859 × 10⁻² N","0.0314 N"],"c":0,"s":"The normal force of the side wall gives the centripetal force: N = mω²r.\nω = 2π/T = 2π/40 = π/20 rad/s, so ω² = π²/400 ≈ 9.86/400 = 0.02465.\nN = 0.2 × 0.02465 × 0.2 ≈ 9.86 × 10⁻⁴ N, option (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 17 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A modern grand-prix racing car of mass m is travelling on a flat track in a circular arc of radius R with a speed v. If the coefficient of static friction between the tyres and the track is μₛ, then the magnitude of negative lift F_L acting downwards on the car is\n(Assume forces on the four tyres are identical and g = acceleration due to gravity)","o":["m({v²;μₛR} − g)","−m(g + {v²;μₛR})","m({v²;μₛR} + g)","m(g − {v²;μₛR})"],"c":0,"s":"The normal force on the road is N = mg + F_L (the lift pushes down as well).\nAt the limit, friction gives the centripetal force: μₛN = mv²/R.\nμₛ(mg + F_L) = mv²/R, so F_L = mv²/(μₛR) − mg.\nF_L = m(v²/(μₛR) − g), option (a)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A particle is moving with uniform speed along the circumference of a circle of radius R under the action of a central fictitious force F which is inversely proportional to R³. Its time period of revolution will be given by","o":["T ∝ R^(4/3)","T ∝ R²","T ∝ R^(5/2)","T ∝ R^(3/2)"],"c":1,"s":"F = mv²/R and F ∝ 1/R³, so v² ∝ 1/R², giving v ∝ 1/R.\nTime period T = 2πR/v ∝ R/(1/R) = R².\nSo T ∝ R², option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 8 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A particle of mass m is fixed to one end of a light spring having force constant k and unstretched length l. The other end is fixed. The system is given an angular speed ω about the fixed end of the spring such that it rotates in a circle in gravity free space. Then the stretch in the spring is","o":["{mlω²;k−ωm}","{mlω²;k−mω²}","{mlω²;k+mω²}","{mlω²;k+mω}"],"c":1,"s":"Let the stretch be x, so the radius is l + x.\nSpring force gives the centripetal force: kx = mω²(l + x).\nx(k − mω²) = mω²l.\nx = mlω²/(k − mω²), option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 9 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A spring mass system (mass m, spring constant k and natural length l) rests in equilibrium on a horizontal disc. The free end of the spring is fixed at the centre of the disc. If the disc together with spring mass system, rotates about its axis with an angular velocity ω, (k >> mω²) the relative change in the length of the spring is best given by the option","o":["{2mω²;k}","{mω²;3k}","{mω²;k}","√{2;3}({mω²;k})"],"c":2,"s":"Let the stretch be x. Then kx = mω²(l + x), so x = mω²l/(k − mω²).\nRelative change = x/l = mω²/(k − mω²).\nSince k >> mω², the denominator is almost k.\nx/l ≈ mω²/k, option (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A uniform rod of length l is being rotated in a horizontal plane with a constant angular speed about an axis passing through one of its ends. If the tension generated in the rod due to rotation is T(x) at a distance x from the axis, then which of the following graphs depicts it most closely?","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"Four graphs of tension T(x) against distance x from 0 to l: (a) constant then dropping at l, (b) a straight line falling to zero at l, (c) a curve starting flat at the top and falling steeply to zero at l, (d) a curve rising steeply from zero and flattening.","d":[["t",2,14,"(a)",""],["l",30,72,30,14,"a"],["l",30,72,150,72,"a"],["t",24,22,"T(x)","es"],["t",148,83,"x","es"],["p","M30 27 L130 27 L130 72",""],["t",130,83,"l","m"],["t",172,14,"(b)",""],["l",200,72,200,14,"a"],["l",200,72,320,72,"a"],["t",194,22,"T(x)","es"],["t",318,83,"x","es"],["p","M200 27 L300 72",""],["t",300,83,"l","m"],["t",2,109,"(c)",""],["l",30,167,30,109,"a"],["l",30,167,150,167,"a"],["t",24,117,"T(x)","es"],["t",148,178,"x","es"],["p","M30 122 A100 45 0 0 1 130 167",""],["t",130,178,"l","m"],["t",172,109,"(d)",""],["l",200,167,200,109,"a"],["l",200,167,320,167,"a"],["t",194,117,"T(x)","es"],["t",318,178,"x","es"],["p","M200 167 Q210 122 300 122 L300 167",""],["t",300,178,"l","m"]]},"o":["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],"c":2,"s":"Take the rod of mass M. The tension at x must supply the centripetal force of the part of the rod from x to l.\nMass of that part = M(l − x)/l, and its centre is at (l + x)/2 from the axis.\nT(x) = [M(l−x)/l] ω² [(l+x)/2] = Mω²(l² − x²)/(2l).\nThis is maximum at x = 0, has zero slope there, and falls to zero at x = l. It is a downward-curving parabola.\nSo the graph is (c)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A smooth wire of length 2πr is bent into a circle and kept in a vertical plane. A bead can slide smoothly on the wire. When the circle is rotating with angular speed ω about the vertical diameter AB, as shown in figure, the bead is at rest with respect to the circular ring at position P as shown. Then the value of ω² is equal to","fig":{"w":340,"h":230,"alt":"A circle of radius r with centre O and vertical diameter AB as the axis of rotation. The bead P is on the circle below O, at a horizontal distance r/2 from the axis.","d":[["c",170,110,80,""],["l",170,10,170,222,""],["t",176,34,"A",""],["t",176,216,"B",""],["c",170,110,2.5,"F"],["t",176,106,"O",""],["l",170,110,210,179.3,"d"],["c",210,179.3,4,"F"],["t",218,183,"P",""],["l",170,179.3,210,179.3,"b"],["t",190,194,"r/2","m"],["t",136,140,"r","m"],["t",120,14,"ω (about AB)",""]]},"o":["{√3 g;2r}","2g/(r√3)","2g/r","(g√3)/r"],"c":1,"s":"From the figure, the bead moves in a horizontal circle of radius r/2 about the axis. So its distance from O makes angle θ with the vertical where sinθ = (r/2)/r = 1/2, θ = 30°.\nForces: weight mg and normal reaction N along the radius towards O.\nVertical: N cosθ = mg. Horizontal: N sinθ = mω²(r sinθ).\nDividing: N = mω²r, so mω²r cosθ = mg and ω² = g/(r cosθ).\nω² = g/(r cos30°) = 2g/(r√3), option (b)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 · Online","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A disc rotates about its axis of symmetry in a horizontal plane at a steady rate of 3.5 revolutions per second. A coin placed at a distance of 1.25 cm from the axis of rotation remains at rest on the disc. The coefficient of friction between the coin and the disc is (g = 10 m/s²)","o":["0.7","0.5","0.3","0.6"],"c":3,"s":"ω = 2π × 3.5 = 7π ≈ 22 rad/s, so ω² ≈ 484 rad²/s².\nThe coin stays at rest if μmg ≥ mω²r, so the least μ is ω²r/g.\nμ = 484 × 0.0125/10 = 6.05/10 ≈ 0.6.\nAnswer: 0.6."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2017 · Online","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A conical pendulum of length 1 m makes an angle θ = 45° w.r.t. Z-axis and moves in the XY plane. The radius of the circle is 0.4 m and its center is vertically below O. The speed of the pendulum, in its circular path, will be (Take g = 10 m s⁻²)","o":["0.4 m/s","2 m/s","0.2 m/s","4 m/s"],"c":1,"s":"T cosθ = mg and T sinθ = mv²/r, so tanθ = v²/(rg).\nv² = r g tanθ = 0.4 × 10 × tan45° = 4.\nv = 2 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016 · Online","tp":"plr","st":"Vertical circle","q":"Concrete mixture is made by mixing cement, stone and sand in a rotating cylindrical drum. If the drum rotates too fast, the ingredients remain stuck to the wall of the drum, proper mixing of ingredients does not take place. The maximum rotational speed of the drum in revolutions per minute to ensure proper mixing is close to\n(Take the radius of the drum to be 1.25 m and its axle to be horizontal)","o":["27.0","0.4","1.3","8.0"],"c":0,"s":"Material stays stuck all the way round if, at the top, mω²R ≥ mg. For mixing it must fall off at the top, so ω must stay below ω = √(g/R).\nTaking g = 10 m/s²: ω = √(10/1.25) = √8 = 2.83 rad/s.\nIn rpm: 2.83 × 60/(2π) ≈ 27 rpm.\nAnswer: about 27.0 rpm."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2011","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A ball of mass (m) 0.5 kg is attached to the end of a string having length (L) 0.5 m. The ball is rotated on a horizontal circular path about vertical axis. The maximum tension that the string can bear is 324 N. The maximum possible value of angular velocity of ball (in radian/s) is","o":["9","18","27","36"],"c":3,"s":"Let the string make angle θ with the vertical. The radius is L sinθ.\nVertical: T cosθ = mg. Horizontal: T sinθ = mω²L sinθ, so T = mω²L.\nAt the breaking tension: 324 = 0.5 × ω² × 0.5, so ω² = 1296.\nω = 36 rad/s."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2005","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"An annular ring with inner and outer radii R₁ and R₂ is rolling without slipping with a uniform angular speed. The ratio of the forces experienced by the two particles situated on the inner and outer parts of the ring, F₁/F₂ is","o":["1","{R₁;R₂}","{R₂;R₁}","({R₁;R₂})²"],"c":1,"s":"In the frame of the ring's centre, each particle moves in a circle with the same ω.\nThe force on a particle of mass m is the centripetal force F = mω²r.\nFor equal masses F is proportional to r, so F₁/F₂ = R₁/R₂.\nAnswer: option (b)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"The maximum velocity (in m s⁻¹) with which a car driver must traverse a flat curve of radius 150 m and coefficient of friction 0.6 to avoid skidding is","o":["60","30","15","25"],"c":1,"s":"Friction provides the centripetal force: μmg = mv²/r.\nv = √(μgr) = √(0.6 × 10 × 150) = √900 = 30 m/s.\nAt a higher speed the car skids, so 30 m/s is the limit speed. Answer: 30 m/s."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2001","tp":"plr","st":"Vertical circle","q":"A small block is shot into each of the four tracks as shown below. Each of the tracks rises to the same height. The speed with which the block enters the track is the same in all cases. At the highest point of the track, the normal reaction is maximum in","fig":{"w":340,"h":190,"alt":"Four tracks, each rising to the same height. Track (a) curls over in a tight circular loop at the top; (b), (c) and (d) have progressively flatter, wider curves at the top.","d":[["r",8,68,12,12,""],["l",20,74,36,74,"a"],["t",25,70,"v","ms"],["p","M40 80 H95 A22 22 0 0 0 95 36 H80",""],["t",2,90,"(a)",""],["r",178,68,12,12,""],["l",190,74,206,74,"a"],["t",195,70,"v","ms"],["p","M210 80 H265 A36 22 0 0 0 265 36",""],["t",172,90,"(b)",""],["r",8,158,12,12,""],["l",20,164,36,164,"a"],["t",25,160,"v","ms"],["p","M40 170 H95 A50 22 0 0 0 95 126",""],["t",2,180,"(c)",""],["r",178,158,12,12,""],["l",190,164,206,164,"a"],["t",195,160,"v","ms"],["p","M210 170 H265 A70 22 0 0 0 265 126",""],["t",172,180,"(d)",""]]},"o":["Track (a)","Track (b)","Track (c)","Track (d)"],"c":0,"s":"The height is the same, so by energy conservation the speed at the top is the same in all tracks.\nAt the top, the track pushes down with N and gravity also acts down: N + mg = mv²/r, where r is the radius of curvature of the track there.\nN = mv²/r − mg. With v the same, N is largest where r is smallest.\nTrack (a) bends most sharply at the top, so the normal reaction is maximum in (a)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1992","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A car is moving in a circular horizontal track of radius 10 m with a constant speed of 10 m/s. A plumb bob is suspended from the roof of the car by a light rigid rod. The angle made by the rod with the track is\n(Take g = 10 m/s²)","o":["zero","30°","45°","60°"],"c":2,"s":"In the car's frame the bob is at rest under mg (down), the pseudo force mv²/r (outwards) and the rod force.\ntanθ = (mv²/r)/(mg) = v²/(rg) = 100/(10 × 10) = 1.\nSo the rod makes 45° with the vertical, and also 45° with the horizontal track.\nAnswer: 45°."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 22 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A tube of length 1 m is filled completely with an ideal liquid of mass 2M, and closed at both ends. The tube is rotated uniformly in horizontal plane about one of its ends. If the force exerted by the liquid at the other end is F then angular velocity of the tube is √{F;αM} in SI unit.\nThe value of α is ______.","n":1,"s":"Take a thin slice of liquid of length dr at distance r from the axis. Its mass is dm = (2M/l) dr, with l = 1 m.\nThe force F at the far end must give the centripetal force to all the liquid: F = ∫ dm ω² r = (2M/l) ω² ∫₀^l r dr.\nF = (2M/l) ω² (l²/2) = Mω²l = Mω² (l = 1 m).\nSo ω = √(F/M) and α = 1."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Conical pendulum and other circular motion","q":"A car is moving on a circular path of radius 600 m such that the magnitudes of the tangential acceleration and centripetal acceleration are equal. The time taken by the car to complete first quarter of revolution, if it is moving with an initial speed of 54 km/hr is t(1 − e^(−π/2)) s. The value of t is ______.","n":40,"s":"Initial speed v₀ = 54 km/h = 15 m/s. Tangential acceleration = v²/R, so v dv/ds = v²/R, where s is the distance along the path.\ndv/v = ds/R, so v = v₀ e^(s/R).\nThen ds/dt = v₀ e^(s/R), so e^(−s/R) ds = v₀ dt.\nFor a quarter circle s goes 0 to πR/2: R(1 − e^(−π/2)) = v₀ t_total.\nt_total = (R/v₀)(1 − e^(−π/2)) = (600/15)(1 − e^(−π/2)) s. So t = 40."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A stone tied to 180 cm long string at its end is making 28 revolutions in horizontal circle in every minute. The magnitude of acceleration of stone is {1936;x} m s⁻². The value of x is ______.\n(Take π = {22;7})","n":125,"s":"ω = 28 × 2π/60 = 28 × 2 × (22/7)/60 = 176/60 = 44/15 rad/s.\nr = 180 cm = 1.8 m.\na = ω²r = (44/15)² × 1.8 = (1936/225) × 1.8 = 1936/125 m/s².\nComparing with 1936/x, x = 125."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Horizontal circle and banking","q":"A curved in a level road has a radius 75 m. The maximum speed of a car turning this curved road can be 30 m/s without skidding. If radius of curved road is changed to 48 m and the coefficient of friction between the tyres and the road remains same, then maximum allowed speed would be ______ m/s.","n":24,"s":"At the limit, μmg = mv²/r, so v² = μgr. For the same μ, v² is proportional to r.\nv₂² = v₁² × (r₂/r₁) = 900 × 48/75 = 576.\nv₂ = 24 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 25 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"plr","st":"Vertical circle","q":"A small bob tied at one end of a thin string of length 1 m is describing a vertical circle so that the maximum and minimum tension in the string are in the ratio 5 : 1. The velocity of the bob at the highest position is ______ m/s. (Take g = 10 m/s²)","n":5,"s":"Let u be the speed at the top, l = 1 m. Speed at the bottom: v² = u² + 4gl (energy conservation).\nT_min (top) = mu²/l − mg. T_max (bottom) = mv²/l + mg = mu²/l + 5mg.\nRatio 5 : 1: mu²/l + 5mg = 5(mu²/l − mg), so 4u²/l = 10g.\nu² = 2.5gl = 2.5 × 10 × 1 = 25, so u = 5 m/s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"plr","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Statement I : A cyclist is moving on an unbanked road with a speed of 7 km h⁻¹ and takes a sharp circular turn along a path of radius of 2 m without reducing the speed. The static friction coefficient is 0.2. The cyclist will not slip and pass the curve. (g = 9.8 m/s²)\nStatement II : If the road is banked at an angle of 45°, cyclist can take the curve of 2 m radius with speed of 18.5 km h⁻¹ without slipping.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.","o":["Both statement I and statement II are false","Statement I is incorrect and statement II is correct","Statement I is correct and statement II is incorrect","Both statement I and statement II are true"],"c":3,"s":"Statement I: v = 7 km/h = 1.94 m/s, so v² = 3.78. The safe limit is μrg = 0.2 × 2 × 9.8 = 3.92. Since 3.78 < 3.92, the cyclist does not slip. True.\nStatement II: on a banked road with friction the limit is v² = rg(μ + tanθ)/(1 − μ tanθ) = 2 × 9.8 × (1.2/0.8) = 29.4, so v ≈ 5.42 m/s ≈ 19.5 km/h.\n18.5 km/h = 5.14 m/s is below this, so the cyclist does not slip. True.\nBoth statements are true: option (d)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1997","tp":"plr","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"Two blocks of mass m₁ = 10 kg and m₂ = 5 kg, connected to each other by a massless inextensible string of length 0.3 m are placed along a diameter of a turn table. The co-efficient of friction between the table and m₁ is 0.5 while there is no friction between m₂ and the table. The table is rotating with an angular velocity of 10 rad/s about a vertical axis passing through its centre O. The masses are placed along the diameter of the table on either side of the centre O such that the mass m₁ is at a distance of 0.124 m from O. The masses are observed to be at rest with respect to an observer on the turn table.\n(a) Calculate the frictional force on m₁.\n(b) What should be the minimum angular speed of the turn table so that the masses will slip from this position?\n(c) How should the masses be placed with the string remaining taut, so that there is no frictional force acting on the mass m₁?","a":"(a) 36 N   (b) 11.67 rad/s   (c) m₁ at 0.1 m and m₂ at 0.2 m from the centre, on opposite sides of it","s":"Distances: r₁ = 0.124 m, r₂ = 0.3 − 0.124 = 0.176 m (opposite sides of O).\nm₂ has no friction, so the tension is T = m₂ω²r₂ = 5 × 100 × 0.176 = 88 N.\n(a) m₁ needs m₁ω²r₁ = 10 × 100 × 0.124 = 124 N towards O. The string gives 88 N, so friction gives f = 124 − 88 = 36 N (towards O).\n(b) In general f = (m₁r₁ − m₂r₂)ω² = 0.36ω². Slipping starts when f = μm₁g = 0.5 × 10 × 9.8 = 49 N. So ω² = 49/0.36 = 136.1 and ω = 11.67 rad/s.\n(c) No friction means T alone gives the force on m₁: m₂ω²r₂ = m₁ω²r₁, so r₂ = 2r₁. With r₁ + r₂ = 0.3 m: r₁ = 0.1 m and r₂ = 0.2 m, on opposite sides of the centre."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1996","tp":"plr","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"A smooth semicircular wire-track of radius R is fixed in a vertical plane. One end of a massless spring of natural length 3R/4 is attached to the lowest point O of the wire-track. A small ring of mass m, which can slide on the track, is attached to the other end of the spring. The ring is held stationary at point P such that the spring makes an angle 60° with the vertical. The spring constant K = mg/R. Consider the instant when the ring is released, (i) draw the free body diagram of the ring, (ii) determine the tangential acceleration of the ring and the normal reaction.","fig":{"w":340,"h":215,"alt":"A semicircular wire track with vertical diameter through centre C and lowest point O. A spring joins O to the ring at P, making 60 degrees with the vertical. Arrows at P show weight mg downwards, the spring force Kx along PO and the normal reaction N along PC.","d":[["p","M110 25 A80 80 0 0 1 110 185"],["l",110,25,110,193,""],["l",110,105,215,105,"d"],["t",104,109,"C","e"],["t",104,189,"O","e"],["p","M110.0 185.0 L116.2 175.7 L127.7 180.6 L129.2 168.2 L140.6 173.1 L142.1 160.7 L153.6 165.6 L155.1 153.2 L166.6 158.1 L168.1 145.7 L179.3 145.0"],["c",179.28203230275508,145,4,"F"],["t",187.28203230275508,139,"P",""],["t",118,171,"60°","s"],["l",179.28203230275508,145,179.28203230275508,183,"a"],["t",183.28203230275508,191,"mg",""],["l",179.28203230275508,145,149.8380323027551,128,"a"],["t",145.28203230275508,119,"N","e"],["l",179.28203230275508,145,149.8380323027551,162,"a"],["t",143.28203230275508,179,"Kx","e"]]},"a":"(i) Forces on the ring at P: weight mg downwards, spring force along PO (pulling towards O), normal reaction N along the radius (towards the centre C).   (ii) Tangential acceleration = 5√3 g/8 (along the track, down towards O); N = 3mg/8 (directed towards the centre).","s":"Geometry: C is the centre, so CO = CP = R. The spring makes 60° with the diameter at O, so triangle COP is equilateral. The spring length is OP = R and P is 60° from the lowest point.\nNatural length is 3R/4, so the stretch is R/4 and the spring force is Kx = (mg/R)(R/4) = mg/4, directed from P towards O.\nFBD: mg down, mg/4 along PO, N along PC.\nTangent at P: gravity gives mg sin60° = (√3/2)mg down the track. The spring force makes 30° with the tangent (60° with the radius PC), so its tangential part is (mg/4)cos30° = √3mg/8, also towards O.\nma = √3mg/2 + √3mg/8 = 5√3mg/8, so a = 5√3g/8.\nRadial (towards C): the speed is zero, so net radial force is zero. Gravity has a part mg cos60° = mg/2 away from C. The spring force has a part (mg/4)cos60° = mg/8 towards C. So N + mg/8 = mg/2 and N = 3mg/8, towards C."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1993","tp":"plr","st":"Statements, matching and subjective","q":"A hemispherical bowl of radius R = 0.1 m is rotating about its own axis (which is vertical) with an angular velocity ω. A particle of mass 10⁻² kg on the frictionless inner surface of the bowl is also rotating with the same ω. The particle is at a height h from the bottom of the bowl.\n(i) Obtain the relation between h and ω. What is the minimum value of ω needed, in order to have a non-zero value of h?\n(ii) It is desired to measure g (acceleration due to gravity) using the set-up, by measuring h accurately. Assuming that R and ω are known precisely, and that the least count in the measurement of h is 10⁻⁴ m, what is the minimum possible error Δg in the measured value of g?","a":"(i) h = R − g/ω²; ω(min) = √(g/R) ≈ 9.9 rad/s   (ii) Δg = 9.8 × 10⁻³ m/s²","s":"(i) Let the radius to the particle make angle θ with the vertical. Forces: weight mg and normal reaction N along the radius towards the centre.\nVertical: N cosθ = mg. Horizontal: N sinθ = mω²(R sinθ), so N = mω²R.\nThen cosθ = g/(ω²R). The height from the bottom is h = R − R cosθ = R − g/ω².\nh is non-zero only if g/ω² < R, i.e. ω² > g/R. Minimum ω = √(g/R) = √(9.8/0.1) ≈ 9.9 rad/s.\n(ii) g = ω²(R − h). With R and ω fixed, Δg = ω²Δh.\nThe error is smallest when ω is smallest, ω² = g/R = 98 s⁻². Δg = 98 × 10⁻⁴ = 9.8 × 10⁻³ m/s²."}
];
